00:09:56.490 --> 00:09:57.900
Okay, let's talk about light.

00:09:57.900 --> 00:10:00.520
First, I want to talk
about the physics of light,

00:10:00.520 --> 00:10:02.010
and I promise to make that very clear,

00:10:02.010 --> 00:10:03.800
even if you don't have
a background in physics.

00:10:03.800 --> 00:10:06.660
And then I want to talk
about the biology of light,

00:10:06.660 --> 00:10:09.710
meaning how light is
converted into signals

00:10:09.710 --> 00:10:11.880
that your brain and body can use

00:10:11.880 --> 00:10:15.310
to impact things like
organ health or disease,

00:10:15.310 --> 00:10:17.000
or how it can use light

00:10:17.000 --> 00:10:19.170
in order to repair particular organs,

00:10:19.170 --> 00:10:22.120
like your skin, your eyes,
your brain, et cetera.

00:10:22.120 --> 00:10:24.180
The physics of light
can be made very simple

00:10:24.180 --> 00:10:26.880
by just illustrating a
few key bullet points.

00:10:26.880 --> 00:10:28.170
The first bullet point

00:10:28.170 --> 00:10:31.760
is that light is electromagnetic energy.

00:10:31.760 --> 00:10:34.690
If the word electromagnetic
feels daunting to you,

00:10:34.690 --> 00:10:35.900
well, then just discard that

00:10:35.900 --> 00:10:37.690
and just think of light as energy

00:10:38.650 --> 00:10:40.250
and think of energy as something

00:10:40.250 --> 00:10:43.740
that can impact other
things in its environment.

00:10:43.740 --> 00:10:46.130
Now, the way to imagine light

00:10:46.130 --> 00:10:48.420
or to conceptualize light as energy

00:10:48.420 --> 00:10:50.650
is that all around you light is traveling

00:10:50.650 --> 00:10:51.610
in these little wavelengths.

00:10:51.610 --> 00:10:53.230
And the reason, for those
of you that are watching,

00:10:53.230 --> 00:10:55.050
I'm making a little
wavey motion with my hand

00:10:55.050 --> 00:10:56.010
is that's actually the way

00:10:56.010 --> 00:10:58.060
that light energy moves in little waves.

00:10:58.060 --> 00:10:59.790
Just like sound waves are coming at you

00:10:59.790 --> 00:11:01.050
and impinging on your ears,

00:11:01.050 --> 00:11:02.150
if you can hear me talking right now,

00:11:02.150 --> 00:11:03.300
that is happening,

00:11:03.300 --> 00:11:04.140
those are sound waves,

00:11:04.140 --> 00:11:07.070
meaning the movement of
air particles out there

00:11:07.070 --> 00:11:08.230
impacting your ear drum.

00:11:08.230 --> 00:11:10.770
Well, light energy is just little bits

00:11:10.770 --> 00:11:14.110
of electromagnetic energy
traveling through your environment

00:11:14.110 --> 00:11:16.770
all the time in these little waves

00:11:16.770 --> 00:11:20.770
and impinging on your brain
and body and eyes, et cetera.

00:11:20.770 --> 00:11:22.240
And as I mentioned before,

00:11:22.240 --> 00:11:25.760
energy can change the way
that other things behave.

00:11:25.760 --> 00:11:29.230
It can cause reactions
in cells of your body.

00:11:29.230 --> 00:11:32.040
It can cause reactions in
fruit, for instance, right?

00:11:32.040 --> 00:11:34.350
You see a piece of
fruit and it's not ripe,

00:11:34.350 --> 00:11:36.050
but it gets a lot of
sunlight and it ripens.

00:11:36.050 --> 00:11:39.300
That's because the
electromagnetic energy of sunlight

00:11:39.300 --> 00:11:42.420
had an impact on that plant or that tree,

00:11:42.420 --> 00:11:44.320
or even on the fruit directly.

00:11:44.320 --> 00:11:46.210
As a parallel example of energy

00:11:46.210 --> 00:11:49.410
and its ability to impact other things,

00:11:49.410 --> 00:11:50.800
we are all familiar with food

00:11:50.800 --> 00:11:52.860
and the fact that food has calories.

00:11:52.860 --> 00:11:55.460
Calorie is a measure of energy.

00:11:55.460 --> 00:11:58.560
It has everything to do with
how much heat is generated

00:11:58.560 --> 00:12:00.840
when you burn a particular
article of food,

00:12:00.840 --> 00:12:01.870
believe it or not.

00:12:01.870 --> 00:12:06.720
And it turns out that how hot
a given article of food burns

00:12:06.720 --> 00:12:08.360
gives you a sense of how much energy

00:12:08.360 --> 00:12:09.750
it can provide your body

00:12:09.750 --> 00:12:13.960
in terms of your body's ability
to store or use that energy.

00:12:13.960 --> 00:12:16.910
So again, think of light
as electromagnetic energy,

00:12:16.910 --> 00:12:21.030
but really put that word
energy into capital letters,

00:12:21.030 --> 00:12:22.950
embed that in your mind, going forward,

00:12:22.950 --> 00:12:26.600
and you'll understand most
of the first bullet point

00:12:26.600 --> 00:12:29.910
of what light is in terms
of the physics of light.

00:12:29.910 --> 00:12:31.900
Now, the second thing that
you need to understand

00:12:31.900 --> 00:12:33.130
about the physics of light

00:12:33.130 --> 00:12:35.820
is that light has many
different wavelengths,

00:12:35.820 --> 00:12:37.560
and the simplest way to conceptualize this

00:12:37.560 --> 00:12:39.720
is to imagine that cover
of that Pink Floyd album,

00:12:39.720 --> 00:12:40.790
where there's a prism.

00:12:40.790 --> 00:12:43.720
You have a white beam of
light going into that prism.

00:12:43.720 --> 00:12:46.210
And then the prism
splits that beam of light

00:12:46.210 --> 00:12:48.130
into what looks like a rainbow.

00:12:48.130 --> 00:12:49.690
So you got your reds,
your orange, your greens,

00:12:49.690 --> 00:12:51.870
your blues, your purples, et cetera.

00:12:51.870 --> 00:12:54.110
Anytime we have light in our environment,

00:12:54.110 --> 00:12:55.770
that is so-called white light.

00:12:55.770 --> 00:12:57.710
It includes all those wavelengths,

00:12:57.710 --> 00:13:00.890
but sunlight and other forms of light

00:13:00.890 --> 00:13:05.050
also have other wavelengths
of light that we can't see.

00:13:05.050 --> 00:13:06.420
So when we think about the rainbow,

00:13:06.420 --> 00:13:09.140
that's just the visible spectrum of light.

00:13:09.140 --> 00:13:11.060
There are also wavelengths of light

00:13:11.060 --> 00:13:12.470
that are not visible to us,

00:13:12.470 --> 00:13:15.320
but that are visible
to some other animals,

00:13:15.320 --> 00:13:19.170
and that can still impact
your brain and body

00:13:19.170 --> 00:13:22.950
because there is still
energy at those wavelengths.

00:13:22.950 --> 00:13:24.990
I'll give a few examples of this.

00:13:24.990 --> 00:13:27.460
Humans are not a species that can see

00:13:27.460 --> 00:13:31.430
into the infrared realm of the spectrum.

00:13:31.430 --> 00:13:36.010
A pit viper, meaning a snake
that has infrared sensors,

00:13:36.010 --> 00:13:38.950
however, can sense in the infrared.

00:13:38.950 --> 00:13:41.030
So if you were to walk through a jungle

00:13:41.030 --> 00:13:42.690
and there's a pit viper there,

00:13:42.690 --> 00:13:46.390
it sees you as a cloud of heat emission

00:13:46.390 --> 00:13:51.230
because your body is emitting
infrared energy all the time.

00:13:51.230 --> 00:13:53.790
You're casting off infrared energy.

00:13:53.790 --> 00:13:55.810
The snake can see it, you can't.

00:13:55.810 --> 00:13:58.740
If you were to put on a
particular set of goggles

00:13:58.740 --> 00:14:01.240
that were infrared goggles,

00:14:01.240 --> 00:14:03.620
well, then you would be able
to see the heat emissions

00:14:03.620 --> 00:14:06.060
of any organism, human or otherwise,

00:14:06.060 --> 00:14:08.893
that could emit infrared energy.

00:14:09.790 --> 00:14:11.980
Let's take the opposite
end of the spectrum.

00:14:11.980 --> 00:14:13.350
We are familiar with seeing things

00:14:13.350 --> 00:14:16.380
that are blue or green or very pale blue.

00:14:16.380 --> 00:14:18.780
But as we say below that,

00:14:18.780 --> 00:14:21.460
meaning even shorter
wavelength light is out there.

00:14:21.460 --> 00:14:24.950
Ultraviolet light is a really
good example of light energy

00:14:24.950 --> 00:14:26.987
that's coming from the sun
and is in our environment

00:14:26.987 --> 00:14:29.760
and is being reflected
off surfaces all the time.

00:14:29.760 --> 00:14:30.630
We don't see it.

00:14:30.630 --> 00:14:33.440
And yet, if it's very bright outside,

00:14:33.440 --> 00:14:36.320
that ultraviolet light can burn our skin.

00:14:36.320 --> 00:14:38.140
As you'll learn in today's episode,

00:14:38.140 --> 00:14:42.800
ultraviolet light can
also positively impact us.

00:14:42.800 --> 00:14:44.780
In fact, I will describe a particular set

00:14:44.780 --> 00:14:48.240
of new results that show
that ultraviolet light

00:14:48.240 --> 00:14:51.210
viewed for just a few minutes each day,

00:14:51.210 --> 00:14:53.630
or landing on the skin for
just a few minutes each day,

00:14:53.630 --> 00:14:56.490
can actually offset a lot of pain.

00:14:56.490 --> 00:14:59.370
It actually has the ability
to reduce the amount

00:14:59.370 --> 00:15:00.990
of pain sensed by your body.

00:15:00.990 --> 00:15:03.470
And we now understand the
specific circuits in the brain

00:15:03.470 --> 00:15:05.230
and body that allow that to happen.

00:15:05.230 --> 00:15:06.063
I'll talk about that

00:15:06.063 --> 00:15:08.370
and the related protocols
a little bit later.

00:15:08.370 --> 00:15:09.560
So the important thing to understand

00:15:09.560 --> 00:15:10.880
about the physics of light

00:15:10.880 --> 00:15:13.350
is that there's energy at all
these different wavelengths.

00:15:13.350 --> 00:15:15.660
We only see some of those wavelengths,

00:15:15.660 --> 00:15:19.070
which basically is to
say that light impacts us

00:15:19.070 --> 00:15:21.380
at many different levels.

00:15:21.380 --> 00:15:24.030
And the so-called levels
that I'm referring to

00:15:24.030 --> 00:15:26.100
are the different wavelengths of light.

00:15:26.100 --> 00:15:27.300
And you're welcome to think

00:15:27.300 --> 00:15:29.770
of the different wavelengths
of light as different colors,

00:15:29.770 --> 00:15:33.290
but do understand that there
are truly colors of light

00:15:33.290 --> 00:15:35.240
that you and I can't see,

00:15:35.240 --> 00:15:38.490
and yet that have powerful
impact on your brain and body.

00:15:38.490 --> 00:15:40.500
Now, the third bullet point to understand

00:15:40.500 --> 00:15:41.930
about the physics of light

00:15:41.930 --> 00:15:44.270
is that different wavelengths of light,

00:15:44.270 --> 00:15:47.940
because of the way that
their wave travels,

00:15:47.940 --> 00:15:51.830
can penetrate tissues to different depths.

00:15:51.830 --> 00:15:54.000
This is very, very important.

00:15:54.000 --> 00:15:56.430
Today, we're going to talk a
lot about red light therapies

00:15:56.430 --> 00:15:59.970
and near-infrared light therapies.

00:15:59.970 --> 00:16:02.340
Those are so called longer wavelengths.

00:16:02.340 --> 00:16:03.320
Longer wavelengths, just think

00:16:03.320 --> 00:16:06.380
of a bigger, longer wave, right?

00:16:06.380 --> 00:16:08.710
A bigger curve, as opposed
to short wavelength light,

00:16:08.710 --> 00:16:10.820
which is going to be shorter, right?

00:16:10.820 --> 00:16:12.210
A short wavelength
light would be something

00:16:12.210 --> 00:16:14.763
like blue or green light
or ultraviolet light.

00:16:15.670 --> 00:16:17.890
Blue, green, and ultraviolet light,

00:16:17.890 --> 00:16:19.680
because its shortwave length light,

00:16:19.680 --> 00:16:23.670
doesn't tend to penetrate
tissues very easily.

00:16:23.670 --> 00:16:25.550
It has to do with the way
that the physics of light

00:16:25.550 --> 00:16:27.820
interacts with the physical
properties of your skin

00:16:27.820 --> 00:16:29.250
and other tissues of your body.

00:16:29.250 --> 00:16:31.280
But basically, if you
were to shine UV light

00:16:31.280 --> 00:16:33.030
onto your arm, for instance,

00:16:33.030 --> 00:16:35.310
it could impact the skin
on the surface of the arm,

00:16:35.310 --> 00:16:39.020
maybe some of the cells just
beneath the top layer of skin,

00:16:39.020 --> 00:16:40.920
but it wouldn't penetrate much deeper.

00:16:41.890 --> 00:16:45.980
Long wavelength light like red
light and near-infrared light

00:16:45.980 --> 00:16:50.010
has this amazing ability to
penetrate through tissues,

00:16:50.010 --> 00:16:51.320
including your skin.

00:16:51.320 --> 00:16:53.490
And so if we were to shine red light

00:16:53.490 --> 00:16:56.153
or near-infrared light onto your arm,

00:16:57.140 --> 00:16:59.920
it would pass through
that top layer of skin.

00:16:59.920 --> 00:17:01.490
It might impact it a little bit,

00:17:01.490 --> 00:17:04.820
but it could penetrate
deeper into your skin,

00:17:04.820 --> 00:17:06.030
not just to the skin layers,

00:17:06.030 --> 00:17:07.700
but maybe even down to the bone,

00:17:07.700 --> 00:17:09.570
maybe even down to the bone marrow.

00:17:09.570 --> 00:17:11.860
And for many people, this
will be hard to conceptualize.

00:17:11.860 --> 00:17:13.400
You think, "Well, wait,
I've got the skin there.

00:17:13.400 --> 00:17:14.840
Doesn't the light just bounce off?"

00:17:14.840 --> 00:17:15.980
And the answer is no,

00:17:15.980 --> 00:17:19.570
because of the way that
long wavelength light

00:17:19.570 --> 00:17:22.890
interacts with the absorbance
properties of your skin.

00:17:22.890 --> 00:17:24.090
Absorbance properties are just the way

00:17:24.090 --> 00:17:26.210
that the skin takes light energy

00:17:26.210 --> 00:17:29.330
and converts it into a
different form of energy.

00:17:29.330 --> 00:17:32.810
And your skin is not able to
take long wavelength light,

00:17:32.810 --> 00:17:36.380
like red light and near-infrared
light, and absorb it.

00:17:36.380 --> 00:17:39.670
But the tissues deeper in your body can.

00:17:39.670 --> 00:17:42.220
So if you shine a red light
or near-infrared light

00:17:42.220 --> 00:17:43.540
onto the surface of your skin,

00:17:43.540 --> 00:17:45.140
you'll see a red glow there

00:17:45.140 --> 00:17:47.470
as a reflectance on the
surface of your skin.

00:17:47.470 --> 00:17:49.740
But a lot of the photon energy,

00:17:49.740 --> 00:17:52.030
the light energy in
those longer wavelengths

00:17:52.030 --> 00:17:54.880
is indeed passing through
those top layers of skin,

00:17:54.880 --> 00:17:56.420
into the deeper layers of skin,

00:17:56.420 --> 00:17:59.660
and can even make it into
the deep layers of your arm.

00:17:59.660 --> 00:18:01.930
And as we start to transition
from the physics of light

00:18:01.930 --> 00:18:03.963
to the biological impacts of light,

00:18:04.870 --> 00:18:07.420
just understanding that the
different wavelengths of light

00:18:07.420 --> 00:18:09.330
impact our tissues at different levels,

00:18:09.330 --> 00:18:10.980
literally at different depths,

00:18:10.980 --> 00:18:12.890
will help you better understand

00:18:12.890 --> 00:18:16.260
how light of different colors,
of different intensities

00:18:16.260 --> 00:18:19.660
and how long you are
exposed to those colors

00:18:19.660 --> 00:18:23.530
and intensities of light can
change the way that the cells

00:18:23.530 --> 00:18:25.740
and the organs of your body work.

00:18:25.740 --> 00:18:27.060
And if it didn't sound weird enough

00:18:27.060 --> 00:18:29.840
that you can pass light
through particular tissues

00:18:29.840 --> 00:18:31.790
and have them land and be absorbed

00:18:31.790 --> 00:18:34.510
at tissues deeper in your body,

00:18:34.510 --> 00:18:37.560
well, it turns out that
different wavelengths of light

00:18:37.560 --> 00:18:42.520
are also best absorbed by
particular so-called organelles

00:18:42.520 --> 00:18:43.570
within your cells.

00:18:43.570 --> 00:18:44.403
What are organelles?

00:18:44.403 --> 00:18:46.280
Organelles are the different compartments

00:18:46.280 --> 00:18:48.940
and different functions
within a given cell.

00:18:48.940 --> 00:18:50.460
So for instance, your mitochondria,

00:18:50.460 --> 00:18:52.367
which are responsible for generating ATP

00:18:52.367 --> 00:18:53.903
and energy in your cells,

00:18:54.950 --> 00:18:57.020
those exist at a particular depth,

00:18:57.020 --> 00:18:58.610
at a particular location within a cell.

00:18:58.610 --> 00:18:59.940
They're not all at the cell surface.

00:18:59.940 --> 00:19:01.810
They sit somewhat deeper in the cell.

00:19:01.810 --> 00:19:05.180
The nucleus of your
individual cells contains DNA,

00:19:05.180 --> 00:19:07.160
and that sits at a
particular depth or location

00:19:07.160 --> 00:19:08.043
within your cell.

00:19:09.270 --> 00:19:10.740
Different wavelengths of light

00:19:10.740 --> 00:19:13.520
not only can penetrate
down into different tissues

00:19:13.520 --> 00:19:15.640
and into different cells of your body,

00:19:15.640 --> 00:19:17.460
but they can also penetrate

00:19:17.460 --> 00:19:19.570
and access particular organelles,

00:19:19.570 --> 00:19:22.220
meaning mitochondria or the nucleus

00:19:22.220 --> 00:19:24.070
or the different aspects of your cells

00:19:24.070 --> 00:19:26.450
that are responsible
for different functions.

00:19:26.450 --> 00:19:31.380
This is exquisitely important,
and it's exquisitely powerful

00:19:31.380 --> 00:19:32.830
because as you'll learn today,

00:19:32.830 --> 00:19:34.760
particular wavelengths
of light can be used

00:19:34.760 --> 00:19:37.210
to stimulate the function
of particular organelles

00:19:37.210 --> 00:19:39.040
within particular cells,

00:19:39.040 --> 00:19:41.580
within particular organs of your body.

00:19:41.580 --> 00:19:45.320
I can think of no other
form of energy, not sound,

00:19:45.320 --> 00:19:47.840
not chemical energy, so not drugs,

00:19:47.840 --> 00:19:52.840
not food, not touch, no form of energy

00:19:53.050 --> 00:19:55.750
that can target the particular locations

00:19:55.750 --> 00:19:58.720
in our cells, in our organelles,

00:19:58.720 --> 00:20:00.770
in our organs and in our body,

00:20:00.770 --> 00:20:02.440
to the extent that light can.

00:20:02.440 --> 00:20:04.680
In other words, if you had to imagine

00:20:04.680 --> 00:20:09.680
a real world surgical tool by
which to modulate our biology,

00:20:09.990 --> 00:20:11.670
light would be the sharpest

00:20:11.670 --> 00:20:13.860
and the most precise of those tools.

00:20:13.860 --> 00:20:15.320
Now, let's talk about
how light is converted

00:20:15.320 --> 00:20:17.090
into biological signals.

00:20:17.090 --> 00:20:19.650
There's several ways in
which that is accomplished,

00:20:19.650 --> 00:20:22.220
but the fundamental thing to understand

00:20:22.220 --> 00:20:26.053
is this notion of
absorption of light energy.

00:20:26.950 --> 00:20:30.460
Certain pigments or colors

00:20:30.460 --> 00:20:33.830
in the thing that is
receiving the light energy,

00:20:33.830 --> 00:20:37.410
meaning the thing that
the light energy lands on,

00:20:37.410 --> 00:20:40.510
are going to absorb particular
wavelengths of light.

00:20:40.510 --> 00:20:43.370
Now, I promise you that you
already intuitively know

00:20:43.370 --> 00:20:44.740
how this works.

00:20:44.740 --> 00:20:47.310
For instance, if you were to sit outside

00:20:47.310 --> 00:20:49.190
on a very bright sunny day,

00:20:49.190 --> 00:20:52.560
and you had a table in
front of you that was metal,

00:20:52.560 --> 00:20:55.020
you might find it hard to
look down at that metal table

00:20:55.020 --> 00:20:56.950
because it's reflecting a lot of light

00:20:56.950 --> 00:20:58.250
of particular wavelengths.

00:20:59.260 --> 00:21:02.040
If that table were pitch black, however,

00:21:02.040 --> 00:21:04.570
it wouldn't reflect quite as much,

00:21:04.570 --> 00:21:07.120
and you would be able to
comfortably look at at it.

00:21:08.020 --> 00:21:10.900
If that table were red, it
might be somewhere in between.

00:21:10.900 --> 00:21:12.870
If that table were green,

00:21:12.870 --> 00:21:14.730
it would be also somewhere in between,

00:21:14.730 --> 00:21:16.550
but let's say it were very light blue.

00:21:16.550 --> 00:21:19.760
Well, then it might reflect
almost as much as a table

00:21:19.760 --> 00:21:23.440
that were just metal or
a white table surface.

00:21:23.440 --> 00:21:26.460
So the absorbance properties
of a given surface

00:21:26.460 --> 00:21:30.230
will determine whether or not
light energy goes and stays

00:21:30.230 --> 00:21:33.310
at that location and has
an impact on that location

00:21:33.310 --> 00:21:35.233
or whether or not it bounces off.

00:21:36.370 --> 00:21:38.760
Every biological function of light

00:21:38.760 --> 00:21:43.080
has to do with the absorbance
or the reflectance of light

00:21:43.080 --> 00:21:46.820
or light passing through
that particular thing,

00:21:46.820 --> 00:21:49.983
meaning that particular cell
or compartment within a cell.

00:21:51.910 --> 00:21:53.560
I'd like to make it clear how this works

00:21:53.560 --> 00:21:55.870
by using the three primary examples

00:21:55.870 --> 00:21:58.960
of how you take light in your environment

00:21:58.960 --> 00:22:01.643
and convert it into biological events.

00:22:03.080 --> 00:22:05.990
We have photoreceptors
in the back of our eyes.

00:22:05.990 --> 00:22:08.720
These photoreceptors
come in two major types,

00:22:08.720 --> 00:22:10.380
the so-called rods and the cones.

00:22:10.380 --> 00:22:13.120
The rods are very elongated,
they look like rods.

00:22:13.120 --> 00:22:16.010
And the cones look like little triangles.

00:22:16.010 --> 00:22:20.840
Rods and cones have
within them photopigment.

00:22:20.840 --> 00:22:24.340
They have dark stuff that's
stacked up in little layers.

00:22:24.340 --> 00:22:27.410
Rods absorb light of
essentially any wavelength.

00:22:27.410 --> 00:22:28.760
There's some variation to that,

00:22:28.760 --> 00:22:32.060
but let's just say rods don't care

00:22:32.060 --> 00:22:34.930
about the different colors of light.

00:22:34.930 --> 00:22:37.730
They will absorb light
energy, photon energy,

00:22:37.730 --> 00:22:40.030
if it's red, if it's green, if it's blue,

00:22:40.030 --> 00:22:42.100
if it's yellow, doesn't matter,

00:22:42.100 --> 00:22:44.210
as long as that light is bright enough.

00:22:44.210 --> 00:22:46.530
And it turns out that rods
are very, very sensitive.

00:22:46.530 --> 00:22:50.810
They can detect very, very
small numbers of photons.

00:22:50.810 --> 00:22:53.190
And rods are essentially what you use

00:22:53.190 --> 00:22:55.430
to see in very low light conditions.

00:22:55.430 --> 00:22:57.470
We'll return more to vision later.

00:22:57.470 --> 00:23:00.950
The cones come in three major varieties.

00:23:00.950 --> 00:23:02.990
At least for most people
who aren't colorblind,

00:23:02.990 --> 00:23:05.330
you have so-called red cones,
green cones, and blue cones.

00:23:05.330 --> 00:23:06.850
But they're not really
red, green, and blue

00:23:06.850 --> 00:23:07.940
in the back of your eye.

00:23:07.940 --> 00:23:10.180
They are cones that either absorb

00:23:10.180 --> 00:23:13.020
long wavelength light, red,

00:23:13.020 --> 00:23:16.120
that absorb medium
wavelength light, green,

00:23:16.120 --> 00:23:18.203
or short wavelength light, blue.

00:23:19.720 --> 00:23:21.290
The reason that they can absorb

00:23:21.290 --> 00:23:22.640
different wavelengths of light

00:23:22.640 --> 00:23:24.770
is they have different photopigments.

00:23:24.770 --> 00:23:26.960
So much as the example I gave before,

00:23:26.960 --> 00:23:29.630
where you have different tables outside

00:23:29.630 --> 00:23:30.820
in the sunny environment,

00:23:30.820 --> 00:23:32.700
and some are reflecting
light more than others,

00:23:32.700 --> 00:23:35.500
others are absorbing
light more than others,

00:23:35.500 --> 00:23:39.310
well, so too, the photoreceptors,
meaning the cones,

00:23:39.310 --> 00:23:42.310
are absorbing light of
different wavelengths

00:23:42.310 --> 00:23:43.760
to different extents.

00:23:43.760 --> 00:23:46.600
And in an absolutely incredible way,

00:23:46.600 --> 00:23:49.030
your brain is actually able
to take that information

00:23:49.030 --> 00:23:51.420
and create this perception
that we have of color.

00:23:51.420 --> 00:23:52.870
But that's another story altogether

00:23:52.870 --> 00:23:55.510
that we'll just touch on
a little bit more later,

00:23:55.510 --> 00:23:57.070
but that if you want to learn all about,

00:23:57.070 --> 00:23:59.893
you can go to our episode on vision.

00:24:01.200 --> 00:24:03.790
So that's photoreceptors
in the back of your eye,

00:24:03.790 --> 00:24:07.150
absorbing light of different
wavelengths, rods, and cones.

00:24:07.150 --> 00:24:08.210
The other place, of course,

00:24:08.210 --> 00:24:10.010
where light can impact our body

00:24:10.010 --> 00:24:12.670
is on our surface, on our skin.

00:24:12.670 --> 00:24:14.670
And skin has pigment too.

00:24:14.670 --> 00:24:17.010
We call that pigment melanin.

00:24:17.010 --> 00:24:19.890
We have within our skin
multiple cell types,

00:24:19.890 --> 00:24:23.100
but in the top layer of skin,
which is called the epidermis,

00:24:23.100 --> 00:24:27.400
we have keratinocytes,
and we have melanocytes.

00:24:27.400 --> 00:24:29.740
And the melanocytes are the cells

00:24:29.740 --> 00:24:31.390
that create pigmentation of the skin.

00:24:31.390 --> 00:24:34.800
And of course there is wide
variation in the degree

00:24:34.800 --> 00:24:37.200
to which there is
pigmentation of the skin,

00:24:37.200 --> 00:24:38.630
which has to do with genetics,

00:24:38.630 --> 00:24:41.140
also has to do with where
you were born and raised,

00:24:41.140 --> 00:24:43.760
how much light exposure you
have throughout the year, right?

00:24:43.760 --> 00:24:45.040
So people toward the equator

00:24:45.040 --> 00:24:46.940
tend to have more melanocyte activity

00:24:46.940 --> 00:24:49.410
than people who are
located at the North Pole.

00:24:49.410 --> 00:24:51.300
And of course, people live
at different locations

00:24:51.300 --> 00:24:52.133
throughout the Earth,

00:24:52.133 --> 00:24:53.960
regardless of their genetic background

00:24:53.960 --> 00:24:54.890
or where they were born.

00:24:54.890 --> 00:24:58.420
And so, as you all know,
with light exposure,

00:24:58.420 --> 00:25:02.600
those melanocytes will
turn on genetic programs

00:25:02.600 --> 00:25:04.410
and other biological programs

00:25:04.410 --> 00:25:06.770
that lead to enhanced
pigmentation of the skin,

00:25:06.770 --> 00:25:08.770
which we call tanning.

00:25:08.770 --> 00:25:12.750
The way they do that is by
absorbing UV light specifically.

00:25:12.750 --> 00:25:14.230
So with melanocytes,

00:25:14.230 --> 00:25:16.630
we have a very specific example

00:25:16.630 --> 00:25:20.330
of how a pigment absorbs
light of a particular length,

00:25:20.330 --> 00:25:23.250
in this case, ultraviolet
shortwave length light,

00:25:23.250 --> 00:25:26.910
which in turn creates a
set of biological signals

00:25:26.910 --> 00:25:29.590
within those cells that
in turn creates changes

00:25:29.590 --> 00:25:31.040
in our skin pigmentation.

00:25:31.040 --> 00:25:33.333
So we have photoreceptors,
we have melanocytes.

00:25:33.333 --> 00:25:35.290
And the third example I'd like to provide

00:25:35.290 --> 00:25:37.620
is that of every cell of your body.

00:25:37.620 --> 00:25:40.380
And what I mean by that is
that every cell of your body,

00:25:40.380 --> 00:25:44.590
meaning a cell that is
part of your bone tissue

00:25:44.590 --> 00:25:48.800
or your bone marrow or heart
tissue or liver or spleen,

00:25:48.800 --> 00:25:51.560
if light can access those cells,

00:25:51.560 --> 00:25:53.830
it will change the way
that those cells function

00:25:53.830 --> 00:25:55.363
for better or for worse.

00:25:56.540 --> 00:25:58.680
For many organs within our body

00:25:58.680 --> 00:26:02.110
that reside deep to our skin,

00:26:02.110 --> 00:26:05.360
light never arrives at those cells.

00:26:05.360 --> 00:26:06.690
A really good example of this

00:26:06.690 --> 00:26:09.440
that we'll touch on later is the spleen.

00:26:09.440 --> 00:26:12.590
Unless you have massive
damage to your body surface,

00:26:12.590 --> 00:26:14.790
unless you literally
have a hole in your body,

00:26:14.790 --> 00:26:18.100
light will never land
directly on your spleen,

00:26:18.100 --> 00:26:21.800
but the spleen still
responds to light information

00:26:21.800 --> 00:26:24.140
through indirect pathways.

00:26:24.140 --> 00:26:26.420
And those indirect pathways arise

00:26:26.420 --> 00:26:28.230
through light arriving on the skin

00:26:28.230 --> 00:26:30.650
and light arriving on the eyes.

00:26:30.650 --> 00:26:31.810
So a key principle

00:26:31.810 --> 00:26:34.570
that I'm going to return
to again and again today

00:26:34.570 --> 00:26:38.630
is that the ways in which
light can impact the biology

00:26:38.630 --> 00:26:40.640
of your organelles, your cells,

00:26:40.640 --> 00:26:43.530
your organs, and the tissues,
and indeed your whole body,

00:26:43.530 --> 00:26:45.410
can either be direct,

00:26:45.410 --> 00:26:48.280
so for instance, light onto
your skin impacting skin

00:26:48.280 --> 00:26:49.830
or light onto your photoreceptors

00:26:49.830 --> 00:26:52.100
impacting the photoreceptors of your eye,

00:26:52.100 --> 00:26:53.920
or it can be indirect.

00:26:53.920 --> 00:26:56.950
It can be light arriving
on your photoreceptors,

00:26:56.950 --> 00:26:59.910
the photoreceptors then
informing another cell type,

00:26:59.910 --> 00:27:01.780
which informs another cell type,

00:27:01.780 --> 00:27:03.220
which then relays a signal

00:27:03.220 --> 00:27:05.620
in kind of a bucket brigade manner

00:27:05.620 --> 00:27:07.577
off to the spleen and says to the spleen,

00:27:07.577 --> 00:27:10.400
"Hey, there's a lot of UV light out here.

00:27:10.400 --> 00:27:12.110
We're actually under stress.

00:27:12.110 --> 00:27:13.730
In fact, there's so much UV light

00:27:13.730 --> 00:27:16.750
that you need to activate
an immune program

00:27:16.750 --> 00:27:18.150
to protect the skin."

00:27:18.150 --> 00:27:19.210
And in response to that,

00:27:19.210 --> 00:27:21.960
the spleen can deploy certain
signals in certain cell types

00:27:21.960 --> 00:27:23.640
to go out and start repairing skin

00:27:23.640 --> 00:27:25.440
that's being damaged by UV light.

00:27:25.440 --> 00:27:28.420
So we have direct signals
and we have indirect signals,

00:27:28.420 --> 00:27:31.000
but in every case,

00:27:31.000 --> 00:27:34.310
it starts with light of
particular wavelengths

00:27:34.310 --> 00:27:37.920
being absorbed by particular
pigments or properties

00:27:37.920 --> 00:27:42.330
of the surfaces that
those light waves land on.

00:27:42.330 --> 00:27:43.870
And as you recall from our discussion

00:27:43.870 --> 00:27:45.640
about the physics of light, remember,

00:27:45.640 --> 00:27:47.500
it's not just about light impinging

00:27:47.500 --> 00:27:48.590
on the surface of your body.

00:27:48.590 --> 00:27:51.980
Light can actually
penetrate deep to the skin

00:27:51.980 --> 00:27:55.830
and access at least certain
tissues and cells of your body.

00:27:55.830 --> 00:27:58.430
Even though you can't see
those wavelengths of light,

00:27:58.430 --> 00:28:01.020
they are getting into you all the time.

00:28:01.020 --> 00:28:04.110
So perhaps the best way
to wrap this discussion

00:28:04.110 --> 00:28:06.150
about the physics and the biology of light

00:28:06.150 --> 00:28:07.850
with a bit of a bow

00:28:07.850 --> 00:28:11.270
is to think about light as a transducer,

00:28:11.270 --> 00:28:13.930
meaning a communicator of what's going on

00:28:13.930 --> 00:28:15.760
in the environment around you

00:28:15.760 --> 00:28:19.150
and that some of those signals
are arriving at the surface

00:28:19.150 --> 00:28:21.270
and impacting the surface of your body.

00:28:21.270 --> 00:28:24.100
But many of those signals
are being taken by cells

00:28:24.100 --> 00:28:25.830
at the surface of your body,

00:28:25.830 --> 00:28:27.630
meaning your melanocytes in your skin

00:28:27.630 --> 00:28:29.480
and the photoreceptors of your eyes,

00:28:29.480 --> 00:28:33.350
and then being passed off
as a set of instructions

00:28:33.350 --> 00:28:35.760
to the other organs and
tissues of your body.

00:28:35.760 --> 00:28:38.980
Light can impact our biology in very fast,

00:28:38.980 --> 00:28:41.520
moderately fast, and slow ways.

00:28:41.520 --> 00:28:45.230
But even the slow ways in which
light can impact our biology

00:28:45.230 --> 00:28:48.370
can be very powerful
and very long-lasting.

00:28:48.370 --> 00:28:51.770
Just as a quick example
of the rapid effects

00:28:51.770 --> 00:28:53.950
of light on our biology,

00:28:53.950 --> 00:28:58.360
if you were to go from a room
that is dimly lit or dark

00:28:58.360 --> 00:29:01.110
into a very brightly lit room,

00:29:01.110 --> 00:29:04.600
you would immediately feel very alert.

00:29:04.600 --> 00:29:05.990
You might say, "No, that's the not true.

00:29:05.990 --> 00:29:09.080
Sometimes I wake up and it's
dark, and I kind of stumble out

00:29:09.080 --> 00:29:11.030
and it's lighter out in the next room.

00:29:11.030 --> 00:29:12.530
And it takes me a while to wake up."

00:29:12.530 --> 00:29:15.080
Ah, but if we were to move you from a room

00:29:15.080 --> 00:29:17.100
that was very dark to very bright,

00:29:17.100 --> 00:29:19.890
a signal conveyed from your eyes

00:29:19.890 --> 00:29:23.170
to an area of your brain stem
called the locus coeruleus

00:29:23.170 --> 00:29:25.780
would cause the release of adrenaline

00:29:25.780 --> 00:29:27.520
similar to the release of adrenaline

00:29:27.520 --> 00:29:30.010
if you were to be dropped
into very, very cold water

00:29:30.010 --> 00:29:30.910
all of a sudden.

00:29:30.910 --> 00:29:33.550
Just an immediate wake-up
signal to your brain and body.

00:29:33.550 --> 00:29:35.900
So that's an example of a rapid effect

00:29:35.900 --> 00:29:37.550
of light on your biology,

00:29:37.550 --> 00:29:39.760
not a very typical one, but nonetheless,

00:29:39.760 --> 00:29:42.543
one that has a hardwired
biological mechanism.

00:29:43.530 --> 00:29:45.560
At the other end of the spectrum

00:29:45.560 --> 00:29:48.490
are what we call slow
integrating effects of light

00:29:48.490 --> 00:29:49.980
on our biology.

00:29:49.980 --> 00:29:53.320
So what I mean by that are
ways in which your body

00:29:53.320 --> 00:29:56.640
is taking information about
light in the environment,

00:29:56.640 --> 00:29:59.760
not in the sort of snapshot, acute sense,

00:29:59.760 --> 00:30:02.540
but averaging the amount of
light in your environment.

00:30:02.540 --> 00:30:05.280
And that average light information

00:30:05.280 --> 00:30:07.970
is changing the way
that your biology works.

00:30:07.970 --> 00:30:10.650
But even though this is a slow process,

00:30:10.650 --> 00:30:13.490
as I mentioned before,
it's a very powerful one.

00:30:13.490 --> 00:30:15.530
The primary example of this

00:30:15.530 --> 00:30:18.340
are so-called circannual rhythms.

00:30:18.340 --> 00:30:21.880
Circannual rhythms are
literally a calendar

00:30:21.880 --> 00:30:26.450
that exists within your
body that uses not numbers,

00:30:26.450 --> 00:30:28.870
but amounts of hormone that are released

00:30:28.870 --> 00:30:31.870
into your brain and body
each day and each night

00:30:31.870 --> 00:30:34.130
as a way of knowing where you are

00:30:34.130 --> 00:30:37.560
in the 365-day calendar year.

00:30:37.560 --> 00:30:40.960
Now that might seem kind of
crazy, but it's not crazy.

00:30:40.960 --> 00:30:45.070
The Earth travels around
the sun once every 365 days.

00:30:45.070 --> 00:30:49.143
And depending on where you are
on the Earth, where you live,

00:30:50.270 --> 00:30:54.920
you are going to get more or
less light each day on average,

00:30:54.920 --> 00:30:56.560
depending on the time of year.

00:30:56.560 --> 00:30:58.850
So if you're in the Northern Hemisphere,

00:30:58.850 --> 00:31:02.940
in the winter months, days are
shorter, nights are longer.

00:31:02.940 --> 00:31:06.040
In the summer months, days are
longer, nights are shorter.

00:31:06.040 --> 00:31:09.100
And of course, things
change whether or not you're

00:31:09.100 --> 00:31:11.700
in the Northern Hemisphere
or the Southern Hemisphere,

00:31:11.700 --> 00:31:16.640
but nonetheless in short
days you have more darkness,

00:31:16.640 --> 00:31:17.880
that's obvious.

00:31:17.880 --> 00:31:22.680
And if you understand that
light arriving on the eyes

00:31:23.880 --> 00:31:26.190
is absorbed by a particular cell type

00:31:26.190 --> 00:31:29.050
called the intrinsically
photosensitive ganglion cell.

00:31:29.050 --> 00:31:29.883
It's just a name.

00:31:29.883 --> 00:31:31.190
You don't need to know
the name, but if you want,

00:31:31.190 --> 00:31:32.350
it's the so-called intrinsically

00:31:32.350 --> 00:31:33.480
photosensitive ganglion cell,

00:31:33.480 --> 00:31:35.330
also called the melanopsin cell

00:31:35.330 --> 00:31:36.810
because it contains an opsin,

00:31:36.810 --> 00:31:41.810
a photopigment that absorbs
shortwave length light

00:31:41.960 --> 00:31:43.730
that arrives through sunlight.

00:31:43.730 --> 00:31:47.510
Those cells communicate to
particular stations in the brain

00:31:47.510 --> 00:31:51.680
that in turn connect to
your so-called pineal gland,

00:31:51.680 --> 00:31:53.430
which is this little pea-sized gland

00:31:53.430 --> 00:31:54.700
in the middle of your brain

00:31:54.700 --> 00:31:57.650
that releases a hormone called melatonin.

00:31:57.650 --> 00:32:00.450
And the only thing you
need to know is that light

00:32:00.450 --> 00:32:02.210
activates these particular cells,

00:32:02.210 --> 00:32:04.550
the intrinsically
photosensitive melanopsin cells,

00:32:04.550 --> 00:32:08.460
which in turn shuts down
the production of melatonin

00:32:08.460 --> 00:32:09.653
from the pineal gland.

00:32:11.020 --> 00:32:13.910
If you think about this
in terms of the travel

00:32:13.910 --> 00:32:16.980
of the Earth around the
sun across the year,

00:32:16.980 --> 00:32:19.600
what it means is that in short days,

00:32:19.600 --> 00:32:22.080
because there's very
little light on average

00:32:22.080 --> 00:32:23.713
landing on these cells,

00:32:24.730 --> 00:32:28.170
the duration of melatonin
release will be much longer

00:32:28.170 --> 00:32:30.860
because as I mentioned
before, light inhibits,

00:32:30.860 --> 00:32:32.580
it shuts down melatonin.

00:32:32.580 --> 00:32:36.100
Whereas in the summer months,
much more light on average

00:32:36.100 --> 00:32:37.580
will land on your eyes, right?

00:32:37.580 --> 00:32:39.010
Because days are longer.

00:32:39.010 --> 00:32:40.450
Even if you're spending more time indoors,

00:32:40.450 --> 00:32:43.540
on average, you're going to get more light

00:32:43.540 --> 00:32:45.160
to activate these cells.

00:32:45.160 --> 00:32:48.207
And because light shuts
down melatonin production,

00:32:48.207 --> 00:32:51.320
what you'll find is that the
duration of melatonin release

00:32:51.320 --> 00:32:54.420
for the pineal is much shorter.

00:32:54.420 --> 00:32:57.510
So melatonin is a transducer.

00:32:57.510 --> 00:33:00.630
It's a communicator of
how much light on average

00:33:00.630 --> 00:33:03.000
is in your physical environment.

00:33:03.000 --> 00:33:05.090
What this means is

00:33:05.090 --> 00:33:07.460
for people living in
the Northern Hemisphere,

00:33:07.460 --> 00:33:10.580
you're getting more melatonin
release in the winter months

00:33:10.580 --> 00:33:12.680
than you are in the summer months.

00:33:12.680 --> 00:33:17.680
So you have a calendar system
that is based in a hormone,

00:33:18.720 --> 00:33:22.159
and that hormone is using
light in order to determine

00:33:22.159 --> 00:33:26.200
where you are in that
journey around the sun.

00:33:26.200 --> 00:33:27.100
Now, this is beautiful.

00:33:27.100 --> 00:33:28.540
At least to me, it's beautiful

00:33:28.540 --> 00:33:31.900
because what it means is that
the environment around us

00:33:31.900 --> 00:33:33.870
is converted into a signal

00:33:33.870 --> 00:33:36.900
that changes the environment within us.

00:33:36.900 --> 00:33:38.820
That signal is melatonin,

00:33:38.820 --> 00:33:41.220
and melatonin is well known for its role

00:33:41.220 --> 00:33:42.950
in making us sleepy each night

00:33:42.950 --> 00:33:44.810
and allowing us to fall asleep.

00:33:44.810 --> 00:33:46.250
Many of you have probably heard before,

00:33:46.250 --> 00:33:48.930
I am not a big fan of
melatonin supplementation

00:33:48.930 --> 00:33:52.200
for a number of reasons,
but just as a quick aside,

00:33:52.200 --> 00:33:54.390
the levels of melatonin
that are in most supplements

00:33:54.390 --> 00:33:57.520
are far too high to really
be considered physiological.

00:33:57.520 --> 00:34:00.210
They are indeed super
physiological in most cases,

00:34:00.210 --> 00:34:02.960
and melatonin can have a
number of different effects,

00:34:02.960 --> 00:34:05.170
not just related to sleep,

00:34:05.170 --> 00:34:07.730
but that's supplemented melatonin.

00:34:07.730 --> 00:34:10.400
Here, I'm talking about
our natural production

00:34:10.400 --> 00:34:11.500
and release of melatonin

00:34:11.500 --> 00:34:15.793
according to where we are in
the 365-day calendar year.

00:34:17.130 --> 00:34:19.080
Endogenous melatonin,
meaning the melatonin

00:34:19.080 --> 00:34:20.960
that we make within our bodies naturally,

00:34:20.960 --> 00:34:23.610
not melatonin that's supplemented,

00:34:23.610 --> 00:34:26.800
has two general categories of effects.

00:34:26.800 --> 00:34:29.180
The first set of effects are
so called regulatory effects

00:34:29.180 --> 00:34:30.940
and the others are protective effects.

00:34:30.940 --> 00:34:32.830
The regulatory effects are for instance,

00:34:32.830 --> 00:34:37.580
that melatonin can
positively impact bone mass.

00:34:37.580 --> 00:34:39.350
So melatonin can, for instance,

00:34:39.350 --> 00:34:41.850
turn on the production of osteoblasts,

00:34:41.850 --> 00:34:45.870
which are essentially stem
cells that make more bone for us

00:34:45.870 --> 00:34:47.460
that make our bones stronger

00:34:47.460 --> 00:34:50.093
and that can replace
damaged aspects of our bone.

00:34:51.100 --> 00:34:53.250
Melatonin is also involved in maturation

00:34:53.250 --> 00:34:56.890
of the gonads during puberty,
the ovaries and the testes.

00:34:56.890 --> 00:34:58.650
Although there, the effects of melatonin

00:34:58.650 --> 00:35:01.330
tend to be suppressive on maturation

00:35:01.330 --> 00:35:02.820
of the ovaries and testes,

00:35:02.820 --> 00:35:04.660
meaning high levels of melatonin

00:35:04.660 --> 00:35:07.560
tend to reduce testicle volume

00:35:07.560 --> 00:35:10.920
and reduce certain
functions within the testes,

00:35:10.920 --> 00:35:13.630
including sperm production
and testosterone production.

00:35:13.630 --> 00:35:16.730
And within the ovaries,
melatonin can suppress

00:35:16.730 --> 00:35:18.630
the maturation of eggs, et cetera.

00:35:18.630 --> 00:35:20.080
Now, I don't want anyone to get scared

00:35:20.080 --> 00:35:21.590
if you've been taking melatonin.

00:35:21.590 --> 00:35:22.830
Most of the effects of melatonin

00:35:22.830 --> 00:35:25.220
on those functions are reversible,

00:35:25.220 --> 00:35:27.310
but I should point out
that one of the reasons

00:35:27.310 --> 00:35:30.670
why children don't go into
puberty until a particular age

00:35:30.670 --> 00:35:32.080
is that young children

00:35:32.080 --> 00:35:34.790
tend to have chronically
high endogenous melatonin.

00:35:34.790 --> 00:35:37.410
And that is healthy to
keep them out of puberty

00:35:37.410 --> 00:35:40.740
until it's the right time
for puberty to happen.

00:35:40.740 --> 00:35:43.440
So melatonin can increase bone mass,

00:35:43.440 --> 00:35:46.890
but reduces gonad mass, so to speak.

00:35:46.890 --> 00:35:48.770
It's going to have varying effects

00:35:48.770 --> 00:35:51.247
depending on the ratios and
levels of other hormones

00:35:51.247 --> 00:35:53.260
and other biological events in the body.

00:35:53.260 --> 00:35:54.170
But as you can see,

00:35:54.170 --> 00:35:57.570
melatonin has these powerful
regulatory on other tissues.

00:35:57.570 --> 00:36:00.910
I should also mention that
melatonin is a powerful modulator

00:36:00.910 --> 00:36:02.160
of placental development.

00:36:02.160 --> 00:36:03.520
So for anyone that's pregnant,

00:36:03.520 --> 00:36:05.880
if you're considering
melatonin supplementation,

00:36:05.880 --> 00:36:08.560
please, please, please
talk to your OB/GYN,

00:36:08.560 --> 00:36:11.010
talk to your other doctor as well.

00:36:11.010 --> 00:36:12.410
You want to be very, very cautious

00:36:12.410 --> 00:36:14.690
because of the powerful
effects that melatonin can have

00:36:14.690 --> 00:36:16.950
on the developing fetus and placenta.

00:36:16.950 --> 00:36:20.050
For people that are not
pregnant, in fact, all people,

00:36:20.050 --> 00:36:22.690
melatonin has a powerful effect

00:36:22.690 --> 00:36:24.990
on the central nervous system as a whole.

00:36:24.990 --> 00:36:27.020
Your brain and spinal cord
are the major components

00:36:27.020 --> 00:36:28.480
of your central nervous system ,

00:36:28.480 --> 00:36:32.193
and melatonin, because it's
associated with darkness,

00:36:33.070 --> 00:36:34.240
which is just another way of saying

00:36:34.240 --> 00:36:35.897
that light suppresses melatonin,

00:36:35.897 --> 00:36:39.230
melatonin is thereby
associated with the dark phase

00:36:39.230 --> 00:36:40.693
of each 24-hour cycle,

00:36:41.840 --> 00:36:43.890
it can have a number of different effects

00:36:44.750 --> 00:36:48.380
in terms of waking up or making
our body feel more sleepy.

00:36:48.380 --> 00:36:50.460
And it does that by way of impacting cells

00:36:50.460 --> 00:36:51.490
within our nervous system,

00:36:51.490 --> 00:36:54.090
literally turning on certain brain areas,

00:36:54.090 --> 00:36:55.620
turning off other brain areas.

00:36:55.620 --> 00:36:57.110
And it does that through a whole cascade

00:36:57.110 --> 00:36:58.520
of biological mechanisms,

00:36:58.520 --> 00:37:00.510
a bit too detailed to get into today.

00:37:00.510 --> 00:37:03.040
So melatonin is regulating
how awake or asleep we are.

00:37:03.040 --> 00:37:05.980
It tends to make us more
asleep, incidentally.

00:37:05.980 --> 00:37:08.880
It's regulating our timing of puberty,

00:37:08.880 --> 00:37:11.340
and it's regulating how our gonads,

00:37:11.340 --> 00:37:12.940
the testes and ovaries, function,

00:37:12.940 --> 00:37:14.830
even in adulthood, to some extent.

00:37:14.830 --> 00:37:17.460
And it's regulating bone mass.

00:37:17.460 --> 00:37:18.293
As I mentioned before,

00:37:18.293 --> 00:37:20.510
melatonin also has protective effects.

00:37:20.510 --> 00:37:22.210
It can activate our immune system.

00:37:22.210 --> 00:37:25.550
It is among the most potent antioxidants.

00:37:25.550 --> 00:37:28.180
So it is known to have
certain anti-cancer properties

00:37:28.180 --> 00:37:29.670
and things of that sort,

00:37:29.670 --> 00:37:32.170
which is not to say that you
simply want more melatonin.

00:37:32.170 --> 00:37:33.900
I think a lot of people get misled

00:37:33.900 --> 00:37:35.180
when they hear something like,

00:37:35.180 --> 00:37:38.010
melatonin has anti-cancer properties.

00:37:38.010 --> 00:37:40.500
That doesn't mean that cranking
up the levels of melatonin

00:37:40.500 --> 00:37:43.670
by supplementing it, or by
spending time in darkness

00:37:43.670 --> 00:37:44.560
and not getting any light,

00:37:44.560 --> 00:37:45.823
which would, of course, inhibit melatonin,

00:37:45.823 --> 00:37:48.540
is going to be beneficial
for combating cancer.

00:37:48.540 --> 00:37:50.590
That's not the way it works.

00:37:50.590 --> 00:37:54.050
It is actually the rise
and fall of melatonin

00:37:54.050 --> 00:37:55.690
every 24-hour cycle

00:37:55.690 --> 00:37:58.510
and the changes in the duration
of that melatonin signal

00:37:58.510 --> 00:37:59.870
throughout the seasons

00:37:59.870 --> 00:38:03.340
that has these anti-cancer
and antioxidant effects.

00:38:03.340 --> 00:38:07.140
So when we think about
light impacting our biology,

00:38:07.140 --> 00:38:08.700
the reason I bring up melatonin

00:38:08.700 --> 00:38:10.340
as the primary example of that

00:38:10.340 --> 00:38:12.953
is A, because melatonin impacts
so many important functions

00:38:12.953 --> 00:38:14.460
within our brain and body,

00:38:14.460 --> 00:38:17.870
but also because hormones
in general, not always,

00:38:17.870 --> 00:38:19.270
but in general, are responsible

00:38:19.270 --> 00:38:22.730
for these slow modulatory
effects on our biology.

00:38:22.730 --> 00:38:24.840
And so I'm using this as an example

00:38:24.840 --> 00:38:26.780
of how light throughout the year

00:38:26.780 --> 00:38:29.290
is changing the way
that the different cells

00:38:29.290 --> 00:38:31.100
and tissues and organs
of your body are working,

00:38:31.100 --> 00:38:34.340
and that melatonin is the
transducer of that signal.

00:38:34.340 --> 00:38:35.173
So at this point,

00:38:35.173 --> 00:38:38.090
we can say light powerfully
modulates melatonin,

00:38:38.090 --> 00:38:39.720
meaning it shuts down melatonin.

00:38:39.720 --> 00:38:42.470
Melatonin is both beneficial
for certain tissues

00:38:42.470 --> 00:38:46.280
and suppressive for other
tissues and functions.

00:38:46.280 --> 00:38:47.820
What should we do with this information?

00:38:47.820 --> 00:38:50.890
Well, it's very well established now

00:38:50.890 --> 00:38:53.130
that one of the best
things that we can all do

00:38:53.130 --> 00:38:56.630
is to get the proper amount
of sunlight each day.

00:38:56.630 --> 00:39:00.370
And by proper, I mean appropriate
for that time of year.

00:39:00.370 --> 00:39:03.600
So in the summer months
where the days are longer

00:39:03.600 --> 00:39:04.820
and nights are shorter,

00:39:04.820 --> 00:39:08.250
we would all do well to get
more sunlight in our eyes.

00:39:08.250 --> 00:39:10.140
And again, it's going to be to our eyes

00:39:10.140 --> 00:39:11.980
because as you recall,

00:39:11.980 --> 00:39:14.270
the pineal sits deep in the brain,

00:39:14.270 --> 00:39:16.360
and light can't access
the pineal directly,

00:39:16.360 --> 00:39:17.870
at least not in humans.

00:39:17.870 --> 00:39:22.650
So in order to get light
information to the pineal

00:39:22.650 --> 00:39:25.200
and thereby get the
proper levels of melatonin

00:39:26.160 --> 00:39:27.610
according to the time of year,

00:39:27.610 --> 00:39:30.960
we should all try and get
outside as much as possible

00:39:30.960 --> 00:39:34.200
during the long days of summer and spring.

00:39:34.200 --> 00:39:36.060
And in the winter months, it makes sense

00:39:36.060 --> 00:39:37.760
to spend more time indoors.

00:39:37.760 --> 00:39:38.840
For those of you that suffer

00:39:38.840 --> 00:39:40.200
from seasonal effective disorder,

00:39:40.200 --> 00:39:41.490
which is a seasonal depression,

00:39:41.490 --> 00:39:44.220
or feel low during the
fall and winter months,

00:39:44.220 --> 00:39:45.340
there are ways to offset this.

00:39:45.340 --> 00:39:47.920
We did an entire episode on
mood and circadian rhythms

00:39:47.920 --> 00:39:49.150
where we described this.

00:39:49.150 --> 00:39:50.500
So it does make sense for some people

00:39:50.500 --> 00:39:52.970
to get more bright light in
their eyes early in the morning

00:39:52.970 --> 00:39:56.050
and throughout the day during
the winter months as well.

00:39:56.050 --> 00:39:59.420
But nonetheless, changes in melatonin,

00:39:59.420 --> 00:40:01.090
meaning changes in the duration

00:40:01.090 --> 00:40:04.120
of melatonin release across the
year are normal and healthy.

00:40:04.120 --> 00:40:06.800
So provided that you're not
suffering from depression,

00:40:06.800 --> 00:40:09.570
it's going to be healthy to
somewhat modulate your amount

00:40:09.570 --> 00:40:11.690
of indoor and outdoor
time across the year.

00:40:11.690 --> 00:40:13.270
The other thing to understand

00:40:13.270 --> 00:40:15.080
is this very firmly established fact,

00:40:15.080 --> 00:40:18.173
which is light powerfully
inhibits melatonin.

00:40:19.310 --> 00:40:21.010
If you wake up in the middle of the night,

00:40:21.010 --> 00:40:23.360
and you go into the bathroom
and you flip on the lights,

00:40:23.360 --> 00:40:26.520
and those are very bright,
overhead, fluorescent lights,

00:40:26.520 --> 00:40:28.090
your melatonin levels,

00:40:28.090 --> 00:40:30.210
which would ordinarily be quite high

00:40:30.210 --> 00:40:31.043
in the middle of the night

00:40:31.043 --> 00:40:33.850
because you've been eyes
closed in the dark, presumably,

00:40:33.850 --> 00:40:37.040
will immediately plummet
to near zero or zero.

00:40:37.040 --> 00:40:40.080
We would all do well
regardless of time of year

00:40:40.080 --> 00:40:44.410
to not destroy our melatonin
in the middle of the night

00:40:44.410 --> 00:40:45.420
in this way.

00:40:45.420 --> 00:40:46.990
So if you need to get up
in the middle of the night

00:40:46.990 --> 00:40:47.823
and use the restroom,

00:40:47.823 --> 00:40:50.920
which is a perfectly normal
behavior for many people,

00:40:50.920 --> 00:40:53.990
use the minimum amount of light required

00:40:53.990 --> 00:40:57.120
in order to safely move
through the environment

00:40:57.120 --> 00:40:58.620
that you need to move through.

00:40:59.780 --> 00:41:02.480
Melatonin needs to come
on early in the night.

00:41:02.480 --> 00:41:06.330
It actually starts rising in
the evening and towards sleep.

00:41:06.330 --> 00:41:08.250
But then as you close your
eyes and you go to sleep,

00:41:08.250 --> 00:41:10.720
melatonin levels are
going to continue to rise

00:41:10.720 --> 00:41:13.630
at least for several hours into the night.

00:41:13.630 --> 00:41:15.250
Again, if you get up in
the middle of the night,

00:41:15.250 --> 00:41:17.950
really try hard not to flip
on a lot of bright lights.

00:41:17.950 --> 00:41:19.500
If you do that every once in a while,

00:41:19.500 --> 00:41:21.060
it's not going to be a problem.

00:41:21.060 --> 00:41:23.010
But if you're doing
that night after night,

00:41:23.010 --> 00:41:27.490
you are really disrupting
this fundamental signal

00:41:27.490 --> 00:41:29.030
that occurs every night,

00:41:29.030 --> 00:41:31.130
regardless of winter,
spring, summer, et cetera.

00:41:31.130 --> 00:41:33.530
And that is communicating information

00:41:33.530 --> 00:41:37.100
about where your brain and
body should be in time.

00:41:37.100 --> 00:41:38.920
And I know that's a little
bit of a tricky concept,

00:41:38.920 --> 00:41:41.880
but really our body is
not meant to function

00:41:41.880 --> 00:41:43.380
in the same way during the winter months,

00:41:43.380 --> 00:41:44.570
as the summer months.

00:41:44.570 --> 00:41:48.030
There are functions that
are specifically optimal

00:41:48.030 --> 00:41:49.670
for the shorter days of winter.

00:41:49.670 --> 00:41:51.840
And there are functions that
are specifically optimal

00:41:51.840 --> 00:41:53.840
for the longer or days of summer.

00:41:53.840 --> 00:41:58.560
So again, try to avoid bright
light exposure to your eyes

00:41:58.560 --> 00:41:59.610
in the middle of the night.

00:41:59.610 --> 00:42:01.610
And for those of you that
are doing shift work,

00:42:01.610 --> 00:42:04.140
what I can say is try and
avoid getting bright light

00:42:04.140 --> 00:42:06.100
in your eyes in the middle
of your sleep cycle.

00:42:06.100 --> 00:42:07.470
So even if you're sleeping
in the middle of the day,

00:42:07.470 --> 00:42:08.770
because you have to work at night,

00:42:08.770 --> 00:42:11.210
if you wake up during that about of sleep,

00:42:11.210 --> 00:42:13.390
really try hard to limit
the amount of light,

00:42:13.390 --> 00:42:15.610
which is going to be harder
for shift workers, right?

00:42:15.610 --> 00:42:17.450
Because there are generally
a lot more lights on

00:42:17.450 --> 00:42:18.540
and bright lights outside,

00:42:18.540 --> 00:42:20.100
so you would want to close the blinds

00:42:20.100 --> 00:42:22.420
and limit artificial light inside.

00:42:22.420 --> 00:42:25.980
One way to bypass some
of the inhibitory effects

00:42:25.980 --> 00:42:27.450
of light on melatonin

00:42:27.450 --> 00:42:30.010
is to change your physical environment

00:42:30.010 --> 00:42:32.040
by, for instance, dimming the lights.

00:42:32.040 --> 00:42:34.030
That's one simple way, very low-cost way.

00:42:34.030 --> 00:42:35.810
In fact, you'll save money
by dimming the lights

00:42:35.810 --> 00:42:36.750
or turning them off.

00:42:36.750 --> 00:42:39.170
The other is if you
are going to use light,

00:42:39.170 --> 00:42:41.870
using long wavelength light,
because, as you recall,

00:42:41.870 --> 00:42:44.090
these intrinsically
photosensitive melanopsin cells

00:42:44.090 --> 00:42:46.770
within your retina that convey the signal

00:42:46.770 --> 00:42:49.070
about bright light in your environment

00:42:49.070 --> 00:42:51.780
to impact melatonin,
to shut down melatonin,

00:42:51.780 --> 00:42:53.890
respond to short wavelengths of light.

00:42:53.890 --> 00:42:55.900
So red light is long wavelength light.

00:42:55.900 --> 00:42:57.800
You now understand that
from our discussion

00:42:57.800 --> 00:42:59.660
about the physics of light.

00:42:59.660 --> 00:43:03.450
And if you were to use
amber-colored light or red light

00:43:03.450 --> 00:43:06.730
and even better, dim
amber or dim red light

00:43:06.730 --> 00:43:07.563
in the middle of the night,

00:43:07.563 --> 00:43:11.130
well, then you would probably
not reduce melatonin at all

00:43:11.130 --> 00:43:13.070
unless those red lights and amber lights

00:43:13.070 --> 00:43:14.120
are very, very bright.

00:43:14.120 --> 00:43:16.310
Any light, provided it's bright enough,

00:43:16.310 --> 00:43:18.900
will shut down melatonin production.

00:43:18.900 --> 00:43:20.757
One final point about melatonin,

00:43:20.757 --> 00:43:24.010
and this relates to melatonin
supplementation as well,

00:43:24.010 --> 00:43:25.960
is that now that you understand

00:43:25.960 --> 00:43:28.110
how potently melatonin can impact things

00:43:28.110 --> 00:43:29.950
like cardiovascular
function, immune function,

00:43:29.950 --> 00:43:32.280
anti-cancer properties, bone mass,

00:43:32.280 --> 00:43:34.370
gonad function, et cetera,

00:43:34.370 --> 00:43:36.710
you can understand why it would make sense

00:43:36.710 --> 00:43:39.010
to be cautious about
melatonin supplementation,

00:43:39.010 --> 00:43:41.530
because supplementation
tends to be pretty static.

00:43:41.530 --> 00:43:44.490
It's X number of milligrams per night,

00:43:44.490 --> 00:43:48.350
whereas normally endogenously
the amount of melatonin

00:43:48.350 --> 00:43:49.450
that you're releasing each night

00:43:49.450 --> 00:43:52.170
is changing according to time of year,

00:43:52.170 --> 00:43:54.370
or if you happen to live in an area

00:43:54.370 --> 00:43:56.970
where there isn't much change
in day length across the year,

00:43:56.970 --> 00:43:59.190
so for instance, if you
live near the equator,

00:43:59.190 --> 00:44:02.730
well, then your body is accustomed
to having regular amounts

00:44:02.730 --> 00:44:04.050
of melatonin each night.

00:44:04.050 --> 00:44:06.210
When you start supplementing melatonin,

00:44:06.210 --> 00:44:09.280
you start changing the total
amount of melatonin, obviously,

00:44:09.280 --> 00:44:12.280
but you're also changing
the normal rhythms

00:44:12.280 --> 00:44:15.030
in how much melatonin is being released

00:44:15.030 --> 00:44:16.360
into your brain and body

00:44:16.360 --> 00:44:19.050
across the 365-day calendar year.

00:44:19.050 --> 00:44:20.230
So while I'm somebody

00:44:20.230 --> 00:44:24.020
who readily embraces
supplementation in various forms,

00:44:24.020 --> 00:44:26.410
for things like sleep
and focus, et cetera,

00:44:26.410 --> 00:44:30.100
when it comes to melatonin,
I'm extremely cautious.

00:44:30.100 --> 00:44:32.610
And I think it's also
one of the few examples

00:44:32.610 --> 00:44:35.860
where a hormone is available
without prescription,

00:44:35.860 --> 00:44:36.693
over the counter.

00:44:36.693 --> 00:44:38.700
You just go into a pharmacy
or drugstore or order online,

00:44:38.700 --> 00:44:40.410
this hormone, which is known

00:44:40.410 --> 00:44:41.910
to have all these powerful effects.

00:44:41.910 --> 00:44:43.358
So I get very, very concerned

00:44:43.358 --> 00:44:45.720
when I hear about people taking melatonin,

00:44:45.720 --> 00:44:47.430
especially at the levels that are present

00:44:47.430 --> 00:44:48.600
in most supplements.

00:44:48.600 --> 00:44:50.630
It's been recognized for a very long time,

00:44:50.630 --> 00:44:53.590
and in fact, there are now
data to support the fact

00:44:53.590 --> 00:44:56.630
that animals of all
kinds, including humans,

00:44:56.630 --> 00:44:59.890
will seek out mates and
engage in mating behavior

00:44:59.890 --> 00:45:04.190
more frequently during the
long days of spring and summer.

00:45:04.190 --> 00:45:06.860
That's right, in
seasonally-breeding animals,

00:45:06.860 --> 00:45:08.700
of course, this is the case,

00:45:08.700 --> 00:45:10.900
but in humans as well,

00:45:10.900 --> 00:45:13.830
there is more seeking out
of mates and mating behavior

00:45:13.830 --> 00:45:16.890
in longer day times of year.

00:45:16.890 --> 00:45:19.570
Now, you could imagine
at least two mechanisms

00:45:19.570 --> 00:45:21.560
by which this occurs.

00:45:21.560 --> 00:45:25.410
The first mechanism we could
easily map to melatonin

00:45:25.410 --> 00:45:28.040
and the fact that melatonin is suppressive

00:45:28.040 --> 00:45:31.470
to various aspects of the
so-called gonadal axis,

00:45:31.470 --> 00:45:34.050
which is basically a fancy way of saying

00:45:34.050 --> 00:45:37.730
that melatonin inhibits
testosterone and estrogen output

00:45:37.730 --> 00:45:40.150
from the testes and from the ovaries.

00:45:40.150 --> 00:45:42.320
I just want to remind people
that both males and females

00:45:42.320 --> 00:45:44.720
make testosterone and estrogen,

00:45:44.720 --> 00:45:46.330
although in different ratios, typically,

00:45:46.330 --> 00:45:47.640
in males versus females,

00:45:47.640 --> 00:45:50.610
and that both testosterone and estrogen

00:45:50.610 --> 00:45:55.610
are critical for the desire to
mate and for mating behavior.

00:45:55.900 --> 00:45:58.609
There's a broad misconception
that testosterone

00:45:58.609 --> 00:46:00.320
is involved in mating behavior

00:46:00.320 --> 00:46:02.020
and estrogen's involved
in other behaviors,

00:46:02.020 --> 00:46:04.680
but having enough estrogen is critical

00:46:04.680 --> 00:46:05.960
for both males and females

00:46:05.960 --> 00:46:08.080
in order to maintain the desire to mate,

00:46:08.080 --> 00:46:10.210
and indeed the ability to mate.

00:46:10.210 --> 00:46:12.000
I discuss this on the episode

00:46:12.000 --> 00:46:14.360
on optimizing testosterone and estrogen.

00:46:14.360 --> 00:46:15.980
So if you'd like more details on that,

00:46:15.980 --> 00:46:19.340
please see that episode of
the Huberman Lab Podcast.

00:46:19.340 --> 00:46:22.880
Okay, so if melatonin is suppressive

00:46:22.880 --> 00:46:27.410
to the so-called gonadal axis
and reduces overall levels

00:46:27.410 --> 00:46:30.030
of testosterone and estrogen
in males and females

00:46:30.030 --> 00:46:33.560
and a light inhibits melatonin,

00:46:33.560 --> 00:46:36.750
then when there's more light,
then there's less melatonin

00:46:36.750 --> 00:46:39.570
and more hormone output from the gonads.

00:46:39.570 --> 00:46:41.730
And indeed that's how the system works,

00:46:41.730 --> 00:46:43.750
but that's not the entire story.

00:46:43.750 --> 00:46:46.120
It turns out that there is a second

00:46:46.120 --> 00:46:47.820
so-called parallel pathway,

00:46:47.820 --> 00:46:50.550
meaning a different biological pathway

00:46:50.550 --> 00:46:52.340
that operates in parallel

00:46:52.340 --> 00:46:55.500
to the light suppression
of melatonin pathway

00:46:55.500 --> 00:46:58.730
that provides a basis for longer days,

00:46:58.730 --> 00:47:02.080
inspiring more desire to mate
and more mating behavior.

00:47:02.080 --> 00:47:05.270
So if we think of the first
pathway involving melatonin

00:47:05.270 --> 00:47:08.520
as sort of a break on these
reproductive hormones,

00:47:08.520 --> 00:47:11.120
the second mechanism is
more like an accelerator

00:47:11.120 --> 00:47:12.090
on those hormones.

00:47:12.090 --> 00:47:15.110
And yet it still involve light.

00:47:15.110 --> 00:47:18.770
As I'm about to tell you,
in animals such as mice,

00:47:18.770 --> 00:47:20.640
but also in humans,

00:47:20.640 --> 00:47:24.070
exposure to light, in
particular UV blue light,

00:47:24.070 --> 00:47:26.170
so short wavelengths of light,

00:47:26.170 --> 00:47:29.220
can trigger increases in
testosterone and estrogen

00:47:29.220 --> 00:47:30.820
and the desire to mate.

00:47:30.820 --> 00:47:33.670
Now what's especially important
about this accelerator

00:47:33.670 --> 00:47:36.850
on the desire to mate and
mating behavior and hormones

00:47:36.850 --> 00:47:40.950
is that it is driven by exposure to light,

00:47:40.950 --> 00:47:44.770
but it is not the exposure
of light to the eyes.

00:47:44.770 --> 00:47:48.170
It turns out that it is
the exposure of your skin

00:47:48.170 --> 00:47:50.250
to particular wavelengths of light

00:47:50.250 --> 00:47:52.890
that is triggering
increases in the hormones,

00:47:52.890 --> 00:47:54.510
testosterone, and estrogen,

00:47:54.510 --> 00:47:57.060
leading to increased desire to mate.

00:47:57.060 --> 00:47:59.270
As it turns out, your skin,

00:47:59.270 --> 00:48:01.440
which most of us just think of as a way

00:48:01.440 --> 00:48:03.390
to protect the organs of our body

00:48:03.390 --> 00:48:06.340
or something to hang
clothes on or ornaments on,

00:48:06.340 --> 00:48:09.480
if you're somebody who
has earrings and so forth,

00:48:09.480 --> 00:48:12.380
your skin is actually an endocrine organ,

00:48:12.380 --> 00:48:15.220
meaning it is a hormone-producing

00:48:15.220 --> 00:48:17.393
and hormone-influencing organ.

00:48:18.240 --> 00:48:19.700
I promise what I'm about to tell you next

00:48:19.700 --> 00:48:21.560
will forever change the way that you think

00:48:21.560 --> 00:48:25.800
about your skin and light
and the desire to mate,

00:48:25.800 --> 00:48:28.440
and indeed even mating behavior.

00:48:28.440 --> 00:48:30.600
I think the results are best understood

00:48:30.600 --> 00:48:33.030
by simply going through the primary data,

00:48:33.030 --> 00:48:35.400
meaning the actual research on this topic.

00:48:35.400 --> 00:48:37.590
And to do so, I'm going
to review a recent paper

00:48:37.590 --> 00:48:40.000
that was published in
the Journal Cell Reports,

00:48:40.000 --> 00:48:42.120
Cell Press Journal, excellent journal.

00:48:42.120 --> 00:48:44.463
This is a paper that came out in 2021,

00:48:45.420 --> 00:48:48.680
entitled "Skin Exposure to UVB light

00:48:48.680 --> 00:48:53.130
induces a skin, brain, gonad
axis, and sexual behavior.

00:48:53.130 --> 00:48:55.810
And I want to emphasize
that this was a paper

00:48:55.810 --> 00:48:58.220
that focused on mice

00:48:58.220 --> 00:49:00.600
in order to address specific mechanisms,

00:49:00.600 --> 00:49:01.650
because in mice,

00:49:01.650 --> 00:49:04.280
you can so-called knock
out particular genes.

00:49:04.280 --> 00:49:06.640
You can remove particular
genes to understand mechanism.

00:49:06.640 --> 00:49:08.190
You just can't do that in humans

00:49:08.190 --> 00:49:10.000
in any kind of controlled way,

00:49:10.000 --> 00:49:12.200
at least not at this point in time.

00:49:12.200 --> 00:49:15.420
And this study also explores humans

00:49:15.420 --> 00:49:18.323
and looked at human
subjects, both men and women.

00:49:19.380 --> 00:49:22.430
The basic finding of this study was that

00:49:22.430 --> 00:49:26.330
when mice or humans were exposed to UVB,

00:49:26.330 --> 00:49:28.530
meaning ultraviolet blue light,
so shortwave length light

00:49:28.530 --> 00:49:31.010
of the sort that comes
through in sunshine,

00:49:31.010 --> 00:49:34.323
but is also available through
various artificial sources.

00:49:35.950 --> 00:49:38.590
If they received enough exposure

00:49:38.590 --> 00:49:40.093
of that light to their skin,

00:49:41.320 --> 00:49:44.870
there were increases in
testosterone that were observed

00:49:44.870 --> 00:49:47.030
within a very brief period of time,

00:49:47.030 --> 00:49:50.120
also increases in the hormone estrogen.

00:49:50.120 --> 00:49:52.577
And I should point out that
the proper ratios of estrogen

00:49:52.577 --> 00:49:55.850
and testosterone were maintained
in both males and females,

00:49:55.850 --> 00:49:58.760
at least as far as these data indicate,

00:49:58.760 --> 00:50:03.760
and mice tended to seek out
mating more and mate more.

00:50:04.480 --> 00:50:07.630
There were also increases
in gonadal weight,

00:50:07.630 --> 00:50:11.300
literally increases in testy
size and in ovarian size

00:50:11.300 --> 00:50:14.080
when mice were exposed to this UVB light

00:50:14.080 --> 00:50:15.540
past a certain threshold.

00:50:15.540 --> 00:50:18.200
Now, as I mentioned before, the
study also looked at humans.

00:50:18.200 --> 00:50:21.980
They did not look at
testy size or ovarian size

00:50:21.980 --> 00:50:23.300
in the human subjects.

00:50:23.300 --> 00:50:25.280
However, because they're humans,

00:50:25.280 --> 00:50:29.000
they did address the psychology
of these human beings

00:50:29.000 --> 00:50:31.470
and addressed whether or
not they had increases

00:50:31.470 --> 00:50:34.840
in, for instance, aggressiveness
or in passionate feelings

00:50:34.840 --> 00:50:38.700
and how their perception
of other people changed

00:50:38.700 --> 00:50:42.360
when they were getting a
lot of UVB light exposure

00:50:42.360 --> 00:50:43.240
to the skin.

00:50:43.240 --> 00:50:45.390
So before I get into some of
the more important details

00:50:45.390 --> 00:50:47.080
of the study and how it was done

00:50:47.080 --> 00:50:49.790
and how you can leverage this
information for yourself,

00:50:49.790 --> 00:50:51.133
if you desire,

00:50:52.040 --> 00:50:55.650
I just want to highlight some
of the basic findings overall.

00:50:55.650 --> 00:50:59.410
UVB exposure increased these
so-called sex steroid levels

00:50:59.410 --> 00:51:00.720
in mice and humans.

00:51:00.720 --> 00:51:03.810
The sex steroid hormones,
when we say steroids,

00:51:03.810 --> 00:51:06.530
we don't mean anabolic
steroids taken exogenously.

00:51:06.530 --> 00:51:07.920
I think when people
hear the word steroids,

00:51:07.920 --> 00:51:10.955
they always think steroid
abuse or use, rather.

00:51:10.955 --> 00:51:13.930
Steroid hormones, such as
testosterone and estrogen,

00:51:13.930 --> 00:51:18.002
went up when mice or humans
had a lot of UVB exposure

00:51:18.002 --> 00:51:19.250
to their skin.

00:51:19.250 --> 00:51:22.810
Second of all, UVB light
exposure to the skin

00:51:22.810 --> 00:51:24.660
enhanced female attractiveness,

00:51:24.660 --> 00:51:27.820
so the perceived attractiveness
of females by males,

00:51:27.820 --> 00:51:30.120
and increased the receptiveness

00:51:30.120 --> 00:51:33.390
or the desire to mate in both sexes.

00:51:33.390 --> 00:51:36.680
UVB light exposure also
changed various aspects

00:51:36.680 --> 00:51:39.050
of female biology related to fertility,

00:51:39.050 --> 00:51:41.530
in particular follicle growth.

00:51:41.530 --> 00:51:43.800
Follicle and egg maturation

00:51:43.800 --> 00:51:47.310
are well-known indices of fertility,

00:51:47.310 --> 00:51:49.760
and of course, correlate
with the menstrual cycle

00:51:49.760 --> 00:51:52.900
in adult humans and is related overall

00:51:52.900 --> 00:51:56.367
to the propensity to become pregnant.

00:51:56.367 --> 00:51:59.390
UVB light exposure enhanced
maturation of the follicle,

00:51:59.390 --> 00:52:02.580
which just meant that more
healthy eggs were being produced.

00:52:02.580 --> 00:52:03.940
These are impressive effects.

00:52:03.940 --> 00:52:05.900
First of all, they
looked at a large number

00:52:05.900 --> 00:52:07.200
of variables in the study.

00:52:07.200 --> 00:52:08.120
And the fact that they looked

00:52:08.120 --> 00:52:10.190
at mice and humans is terrific.

00:52:10.190 --> 00:52:13.100
I think that oftentimes we
find it hard to translate data

00:52:13.100 --> 00:52:13.950
from mice to humans.

00:52:13.950 --> 00:52:16.310
So the fact that they
looked at both in parallel

00:52:16.310 --> 00:52:17.193
is wonderful.

00:52:18.490 --> 00:52:20.300
In the mice and in the humans,

00:52:20.300 --> 00:52:22.800
they established a protocol

00:52:22.800 --> 00:52:26.950
that essentially involved
exposing the skin to UV light

00:52:26.950 --> 00:52:29.440
that was equivalent to
about 20 to 30 minutes

00:52:29.440 --> 00:52:31.380
of midday sun exposure.

00:52:31.380 --> 00:52:33.090
Now, of course, where
you live in the world

00:52:33.090 --> 00:52:34.730
will dictate whether
or not that midday sun

00:52:34.730 --> 00:52:37.670
is very, very bright and
intense or is less bright.

00:52:37.670 --> 00:52:39.400
Maybe there's cloud cover, et cetera.

00:52:39.400 --> 00:52:43.130
But since I'm imagining that
most people are interested

00:52:43.130 --> 00:52:45.730
in the ways to increase testosterone

00:52:45.730 --> 00:52:47.750
and/or estrogen in humans

00:52:47.750 --> 00:52:48.870
and are not so much interested

00:52:48.870 --> 00:52:51.100
in increasing testosterone in mice,

00:52:51.100 --> 00:52:52.850
I'm going to just review what they did

00:52:52.850 --> 00:52:55.940
in the human population
or the human subjects.

00:52:55.940 --> 00:52:58.280
What they did is they had people,

00:52:58.280 --> 00:53:00.070
first of all, establish a baseline.

00:53:00.070 --> 00:53:01.360
And the way they established a baseline

00:53:01.360 --> 00:53:02.670
was a little bit unusual,

00:53:02.670 --> 00:53:04.560
but will make perfect sense to you.

00:53:04.560 --> 00:53:06.020
They had people wear long sleeves

00:53:06.020 --> 00:53:08.820
and essentially cover up and
avoid sunlight for a few days

00:53:08.820 --> 00:53:10.750
so they could measure
their baseline hormones

00:53:10.750 --> 00:53:14.378
in the absence of getting
a lot of UVB light exposure

00:53:14.378 --> 00:53:16.630
from the sun or from other sources.

00:53:16.630 --> 00:53:18.880
Now, of course, these people had access

00:53:18.880 --> 00:53:20.020
to artificial lights,

00:53:20.020 --> 00:53:22.060
but as I've pointed out
on this podcast before,

00:53:22.060 --> 00:53:25.450
it's pretty unusual that
you'll get enough UVB exposure

00:53:25.450 --> 00:53:28.030
from artificial lights throughout the day.

00:53:28.030 --> 00:53:30.710
And in the morning you
need a lot of UVB exposure,

00:53:30.710 --> 00:53:34.110
or we should be getting a lot
of UVB exposure to our eyes

00:53:34.110 --> 00:53:36.050
and to our face and to our
skin throughout the day,

00:53:36.050 --> 00:53:37.740
provided we're not getting sunburnt.

00:53:37.740 --> 00:53:40.220
This is actually a healthy
thing for mood and for energy

00:53:40.220 --> 00:53:41.220
throughout the day.

00:53:41.220 --> 00:53:43.940
It's only at night,
basically between the hours

00:53:43.940 --> 00:53:46.310
of about 10:00 pm and 4:00 am,

00:53:46.310 --> 00:53:49.740
that even a tiny bit of UVB
exposure from artificial sources

00:53:49.740 --> 00:53:51.667
can mess us up in terms of our sleep

00:53:51.667 --> 00:53:53.460
and our energy levels, and so on.

00:53:53.460 --> 00:53:55.880
And that's because of
the potent effect of UVB

00:53:55.880 --> 00:53:58.080
on suppressing melatonin.

00:53:58.080 --> 00:54:01.220
So the point here is that
they establish a baseline

00:54:01.220 --> 00:54:04.230
whereby people were getting
some artificial light exposure

00:54:04.230 --> 00:54:05.063
throughout the day,

00:54:05.063 --> 00:54:06.690
but they weren't getting outside a lot.

00:54:06.690 --> 00:54:08.380
They weren't getting a lot of sunlight.

00:54:08.380 --> 00:54:11.750
And then they had people receive a dose

00:54:11.750 --> 00:54:14.060
of UVB light exposure

00:54:14.060 --> 00:54:16.860
that was about 20 to 30 minutes outdoors.

00:54:16.860 --> 00:54:20.350
They had people wear short
sleeves, no hat, no sunglasses.

00:54:20.350 --> 00:54:21.920
Some people wore sleeveless shirts.

00:54:21.920 --> 00:54:23.610
They encouraged people to wear shorts.

00:54:23.610 --> 00:54:26.010
So they were indeed wearing clothing.

00:54:26.010 --> 00:54:27.400
They were not naked.

00:54:27.400 --> 00:54:30.310
And they were wearing
clothing that was culturally

00:54:30.310 --> 00:54:31.470
and situationally appropriate,

00:54:31.470 --> 00:54:32.980
at least for the part of the world

00:54:32.980 --> 00:54:34.390
where this study was done.

00:54:34.390 --> 00:54:37.150
And they had people do that
two or three times a week.

00:54:37.150 --> 00:54:38.370
So in terms of a protocol

00:54:38.370 --> 00:54:39.890
that you might export from this study,

00:54:39.890 --> 00:54:42.040
basically getting outside
for about 30 minutes,

00:54:42.040 --> 00:54:44.730
two or three times a week
in a minimum of clothing,

00:54:44.730 --> 00:54:47.220
and yet still wearing enough clothing

00:54:47.220 --> 00:54:48.970
that is culturally appropriate.

00:54:48.970 --> 00:54:51.210
They were outside, they
weren't sun bathing,

00:54:51.210 --> 00:54:52.620
flipping over on their back and front.

00:54:52.620 --> 00:54:53.870
They were just moving about doing things.

00:54:53.870 --> 00:54:55.440
They could read, they could talk,

00:54:55.440 --> 00:54:57.560
they could go about other activities,

00:54:57.560 --> 00:54:59.590
but they weren't wearing a broad brim hat

00:54:59.590 --> 00:55:00.580
or a hat of any kind,

00:55:00.580 --> 00:55:03.750
just getting a lot of sun
exposure to their skin.

00:55:03.750 --> 00:55:08.030
They did this for a total
of 10 to 12 UVB treatments.

00:55:08.030 --> 00:55:10.100
So this took several weeks, right?

00:55:10.100 --> 00:55:11.450
It took about a month,
if you think about it,

00:55:11.450 --> 00:55:12.380
two or three times per week

00:55:12.380 --> 00:55:15.810
for a total of 10 to 12 UVB treatments.

00:55:15.810 --> 00:55:17.330
These treatments, of course,
are just being outside

00:55:17.330 --> 00:55:18.420
in the sun.

00:55:18.420 --> 00:55:20.900
And then they measured hormones,

00:55:20.900 --> 00:55:22.120
and they measured the psychology

00:55:22.120 --> 00:55:24.950
of these male and female adult subjects.

00:55:24.950 --> 00:55:27.110
Let's first look at the
psychological changes

00:55:27.110 --> 00:55:28.980
that these human subjects experienced

00:55:28.980 --> 00:55:33.090
after getting 10 to 12 of
these UVB light exposure

00:55:33.090 --> 00:55:35.410
outdoor and sunlight type treatments.

00:55:35.410 --> 00:55:37.120
They did this by collecting blood samples

00:55:37.120 --> 00:55:38.430
throughout the study,

00:55:38.430 --> 00:55:40.990
and they saw significant increases

00:55:40.990 --> 00:55:42.730
in the hormones, beta-estradiol,

00:55:42.730 --> 00:55:45.270
which is one of the
major forms of estrogen,

00:55:45.270 --> 00:55:48.450
progesterone, another
important steroid hormone,

00:55:48.450 --> 00:55:51.720
and testosterone in both men and women.

00:55:51.720 --> 00:55:55.240
Now, an important point is
that the testosterone increases

00:55:55.240 --> 00:55:59.330
were significantly higher in
men that happened to originate

00:55:59.330 --> 00:56:03.030
from countries that had low UV exposure

00:56:04.080 --> 00:56:05.560
compared to individuals from countries

00:56:05.560 --> 00:56:07.970
with high UV exposure.

00:56:07.970 --> 00:56:10.110
Now, this ought to make sense

00:56:10.110 --> 00:56:11.370
if we understand a little bit

00:56:11.370 --> 00:56:14.010
about how the skin functions
as an endocrine organ.

00:56:14.010 --> 00:56:17.070
Many of you have probably
heard of vitamin D3,

00:56:17.070 --> 00:56:19.730
which is a vitamin that we all make.

00:56:19.730 --> 00:56:21.210
Many people supplement it as well

00:56:21.210 --> 00:56:23.133
if they need additional vitamin D3.

00:56:24.320 --> 00:56:28.840
We all require sunlight in
order to allow vitamin D3

00:56:28.840 --> 00:56:32.200
to be synthesized and perform
its roles in the body.

00:56:32.200 --> 00:56:35.150
And it turns out that
people who have darker skin

00:56:35.150 --> 00:56:39.730
actually need more vitamin D3
and/or more sunlight exposure

00:56:39.730 --> 00:56:42.220
in order to activate that D3 pathway,

00:56:42.220 --> 00:56:45.300
than do people with paler skin.

00:56:45.300 --> 00:56:47.490
And this should make sense to all of you

00:56:47.490 --> 00:56:50.190
given what you now
understand about melanocytes,

00:56:50.190 --> 00:56:51.760
that cell type that we discussed earlier,

00:56:51.760 --> 00:56:54.840
because melanocytes have
pigment within them.

00:56:54.840 --> 00:56:56.670
And if you have darker skin,

00:56:56.670 --> 00:56:59.300
it means that you have more melanocytes

00:56:59.300 --> 00:57:00.580
or that you have melanocytes

00:57:00.580 --> 00:57:03.990
that are more efficient
at creating pigment.

00:57:03.990 --> 00:57:06.010
And as a consequence,

00:57:06.010 --> 00:57:06.909
the light that lands on your skin

00:57:06.909 --> 00:57:09.420
will be absorbed by those melanocytes,

00:57:09.420 --> 00:57:12.750
and less of it is able
to impact the D3 pathway.

00:57:12.750 --> 00:57:14.070
Whereas if you have pale skin,

00:57:14.070 --> 00:57:16.110
more of the light that lands on your skin

00:57:16.110 --> 00:57:18.200
can trigger the synthesis

00:57:18.200 --> 00:57:22.370
and assist the actions of vitamin D3.

00:57:22.370 --> 00:57:24.350
Similarly, in this study, they found

00:57:24.350 --> 00:57:25.183
that people who had paler skin

00:57:25.183 --> 00:57:28.210
and/or who originated from countries

00:57:28.210 --> 00:57:32.300
where they had less UVB light
exposure across the year

00:57:32.300 --> 00:57:35.530
had greater, meaning more
significant, increases

00:57:35.530 --> 00:57:36.970
in testosterone overall

00:57:36.970 --> 00:57:39.640
than did people who
already were getting a lot

00:57:39.640 --> 00:57:41.120
of UVB exposure.

00:57:41.120 --> 00:57:43.850
This led them to explore
so-called seasonal changes

00:57:43.850 --> 00:57:46.440
in testosterone that occurred normally

00:57:46.440 --> 00:57:49.710
in the absence of any
light exposure treatment.

00:57:49.710 --> 00:57:52.660
So up until now, I've been
talking about the aspects

00:57:52.660 --> 00:57:55.180
of this study involving
people getting outside

00:57:55.180 --> 00:57:58.010
for about 20 to 30 minutes
per day in sunlight,

00:57:58.010 --> 00:57:59.470
in a minimum of clothing.

00:57:59.470 --> 00:58:01.870
There was an increase
in testosterone observed

00:58:01.870 --> 00:58:03.300
in both men and women.

00:58:03.300 --> 00:58:05.320
The increases in testosterone were greater

00:58:05.320 --> 00:58:08.560
for people that had paler
skin than darker skin.

00:58:08.560 --> 00:58:10.270
So the data I'm about to describe

00:58:10.270 --> 00:58:13.030
also come from this same
paper, but do not involve

00:58:13.030 --> 00:58:16.130
20 to 30 minute daily
sun exposure protocols.

00:58:16.130 --> 00:58:19.680
It's simply addressing whether
or not testosterone levels

00:58:19.680 --> 00:58:22.563
change as a function of time of year.

00:58:24.010 --> 00:58:27.860
They measure testosterone
across the 12-month calendar.

00:58:27.860 --> 00:58:29.700
This study was done on subjects living

00:58:29.700 --> 00:58:32.230
in the Northern Hemisphere
for the entire year.

00:58:32.230 --> 00:58:35.540
And so in the months of
January, February, and March,

00:58:35.540 --> 00:58:38.650
of course, the length of days is shortest

00:58:38.650 --> 00:58:41.100
and the length of nights is longest.

00:58:41.100 --> 00:58:43.640
And, of course, in the
spring and summer months,

00:58:43.640 --> 00:58:45.750
June, July, August, September, and so on,

00:58:45.750 --> 00:58:47.780
the days are much longer
and the nights are shorter.

00:58:47.780 --> 00:58:50.603
And what they observed was very obvious.

00:58:51.610 --> 00:58:54.620
They observed that testosterone levels

00:58:54.620 --> 00:58:56.820
were lowest in the winter months

00:58:56.820 --> 00:58:58.420
and were highest in the months

00:58:58.420 --> 00:59:01.280
of June, July, August, and September.

00:59:01.280 --> 00:59:03.080
Now, these are very important data.

00:59:03.080 --> 00:59:03.913
At least to my knowledge,

00:59:03.913 --> 00:59:07.490
these are the first data
systematically exploring the levels

00:59:07.490 --> 00:59:10.000
of sex steroid hormones in humans

00:59:10.000 --> 00:59:11.620
as a function of time of year

00:59:11.620 --> 00:59:13.450
and thereby as a function

00:59:13.450 --> 00:59:16.000
of how much sunlight
exposure they're getting.

00:59:16.000 --> 00:59:17.950
And what's remarkable about these data

00:59:17.950 --> 00:59:20.680
is that they map very
well to the data in mice

00:59:20.680 --> 00:59:23.600
and the other data in
this paper on humans,

00:59:23.600 --> 00:59:28.040
which illustrate that if you're
getting more UVB exposure,

00:59:28.040 --> 00:59:29.810
your testosterone levels are higher.

00:59:29.810 --> 00:59:31.090
This study went a step further

00:59:31.090 --> 00:59:33.780
and explored whether or not
the amount of sunlight exposure

00:59:33.780 --> 00:59:35.270
that one is getting to their skin

00:59:35.270 --> 00:59:37.420
influences their psychology

00:59:37.420 --> 00:59:40.130
in terms of whether or not
they have increased desire

00:59:40.130 --> 00:59:41.600
to mate and so on.

00:59:41.600 --> 00:59:43.970
It's well known that sunlight exposure

00:59:43.970 --> 00:59:46.810
to the eyes can increase mood.

00:59:46.810 --> 00:59:48.775
And I talked about this
in the podcast episode

00:59:48.775 --> 00:59:50.684
with my guest, Dr. Samer Hattar,

00:59:50.684 --> 00:59:52.800
who's the director of
the chronobiology unit

00:59:52.800 --> 00:59:54.430
at the National Institutes
of Mental Health.

00:59:54.430 --> 00:59:55.720
And Samer's recommendation

00:59:55.720 --> 00:59:57.550
is that people get as
much bright light exposure

00:59:57.550 --> 01:00:00.230
as they safely can in the
morning and throughout the day

01:00:00.230 --> 01:00:02.450
for sake of both sleep and energy,

01:00:02.450 --> 01:00:06.900
but also for enhancing mood
and regulating appetite.

01:00:06.900 --> 01:00:08.840
In this study, it was found

01:00:08.840 --> 01:00:12.030
that both males and
females had higher levels

01:00:12.030 --> 01:00:16.180
of romantic passion after
getting the UV treatment.

01:00:16.180 --> 01:00:18.760
In fact, some of them reported
increases in romantic passion

01:00:18.760 --> 01:00:21.490
from just one or two
of these UV treatments.

01:00:21.490 --> 01:00:24.580
So they didn't have to go
through all 10 or 12 in order

01:00:24.580 --> 01:00:28.430
to get a statistically
significant increase in passion.

01:00:28.430 --> 01:00:30.060
Now, when we talk about passion,

01:00:30.060 --> 01:00:32.030
as the authors of this paper acknowledge,

01:00:32.030 --> 01:00:32.990
there's really two forms.

01:00:32.990 --> 01:00:34.690
There is emotional and sexual,

01:00:34.690 --> 01:00:36.440
and they parse this pretty finely.

01:00:36.440 --> 01:00:37.980
I don't want to go into all the details,

01:00:37.980 --> 01:00:39.760
and we can provide a reference
and link to this study

01:00:39.760 --> 01:00:41.760
if you'd like to look at those details.

01:00:41.760 --> 01:00:44.610
But what they found was that women

01:00:44.610 --> 01:00:46.710
receiving this UVB light exposure

01:00:47.700 --> 01:00:49.550
focused more on increases

01:00:49.550 --> 01:00:51.780
in physical arousal and sexual passion,

01:00:51.780 --> 01:00:53.750
whereas the men actually scored higher

01:00:53.750 --> 01:00:55.620
on the cognitive dimensions of passion,

01:00:55.620 --> 01:00:58.260
such as obsessive thoughts
about their partner and so on.

01:00:58.260 --> 01:01:00.680
Regardless, both males and females

01:01:01.560 --> 01:01:05.450
experienced and reported a
increase in sexual passion

01:01:05.450 --> 01:01:06.920
and desire to mate.

01:01:06.920 --> 01:01:08.660
And we now know there were increases

01:01:08.660 --> 01:01:10.520
in testosterone and estrogen,

01:01:10.520 --> 01:01:13.230
which of course could be driving
the psychological changes,

01:01:13.230 --> 01:01:15.860
although I'm sure that those
interact in both directions,

01:01:15.860 --> 01:01:19.430
meaning the hormones no
doubt affect psychology

01:01:19.430 --> 01:01:21.240
and no doubt the psychology,

01:01:21.240 --> 01:01:22.990
these changes in passionate feelings,

01:01:22.990 --> 01:01:24.910
no doubt also increased

01:01:24.910 --> 01:01:26.950
or changed the hormone levels as well.

01:01:26.950 --> 01:01:29.410
And I want to reemphasize

01:01:29.410 --> 01:01:31.430
that there was a component of the study

01:01:31.430 --> 01:01:35.320
that had no deliberate
daylight, sunlight exposure

01:01:35.320 --> 01:01:36.380
for 20 or 30 minutes,

01:01:36.380 --> 01:01:39.630
but rather just looked at hormone
levels throughout the year

01:01:39.630 --> 01:01:44.020
and found that the increase in day length

01:01:44.020 --> 01:01:45.460
correlated with increases

01:01:45.460 --> 01:01:47.450
in testosterone and sexual passion.

01:01:47.450 --> 01:01:50.180
Now, in my opinion, this
is a very noteworthy study

01:01:50.180 --> 01:01:54.360
because it really illustrates
that sunlight and day length

01:01:54.360 --> 01:01:56.430
can impact the melatonin pathway

01:01:56.430 --> 01:01:59.640
and thereby take the foot off the brake,

01:01:59.640 --> 01:02:02.617
so to speak, on testosterone, estrogen,

01:02:02.617 --> 01:02:04.010
and the desire to mate.

01:02:04.010 --> 01:02:08.250
It also emphasizes that
sunlight, UVB light,

01:02:08.250 --> 01:02:11.400
can directly trigger hormone pathways

01:02:11.400 --> 01:02:14.130
and desire to mate and mating behavior.

01:02:14.130 --> 01:02:15.530
Now, this study went a step further

01:02:15.530 --> 01:02:17.540
in defining the precise mechanism

01:02:17.540 --> 01:02:19.537
by which light can
impact all these hormones

01:02:19.537 --> 01:02:21.600
and this desire to mate.

01:02:21.600 --> 01:02:23.700
And here, understanding
the mechanism is key

01:02:23.700 --> 01:02:26.170
if you want to export
a particular protocol

01:02:26.170 --> 01:02:27.853
or tool that you might apply.

01:02:29.340 --> 01:02:32.470
We talked earlier about how
UVB light exposure to the eyes

01:02:32.470 --> 01:02:34.610
triggers activation of
these particular neurons

01:02:34.610 --> 01:02:35.670
within the eye,

01:02:35.670 --> 01:02:37.940
and then with centers deeper in the brain,

01:02:37.940 --> 01:02:39.820
and eventually the pineal gland

01:02:39.820 --> 01:02:42.200
to suppress the output of melatonin

01:02:42.200 --> 01:02:45.070
and thereby to allow
testosterone and estrogen

01:02:45.070 --> 01:02:46.230
to exist at higher levels

01:02:46.230 --> 01:02:50.620
because melatonin can inhibit
testosterone and estrogen.

01:02:50.620 --> 01:02:51.700
In this study,

01:02:51.700 --> 01:02:54.140
they were able to very clearly establish

01:02:54.140 --> 01:02:57.490
that it is sunlight exposure to our skin

01:02:57.490 --> 01:02:59.470
that is causing these hormone increases

01:02:59.470 --> 01:03:02.150
that they observed in mice and humans.

01:03:02.150 --> 01:03:03.610
And the way they did that

01:03:03.610 --> 01:03:06.690
was to use the so-called
knockout technology,

01:03:06.690 --> 01:03:08.730
the ability to remove specific genes

01:03:08.730 --> 01:03:11.110
within specific tissues of the body.

01:03:11.110 --> 01:03:13.650
And what they found is that UVB light,

01:03:13.650 --> 01:03:16.483
meaning sunlight-exposed skin,

01:03:17.420 --> 01:03:19.530
upregulated, meaning
increased the activity

01:03:19.530 --> 01:03:21.030
of something called p53,

01:03:21.030 --> 01:03:24.060
which is involved in
the maturation of cells

01:03:24.060 --> 01:03:26.030
and various aspects of cellular function.

01:03:26.030 --> 01:03:29.890
And the cells they were focused
on were the keratinocytes,

01:03:29.890 --> 01:03:32.560
which you are now familiar with
from our earlier discussion

01:03:32.560 --> 01:03:35.140
about the fact that the
epidermis of your skin

01:03:35.140 --> 01:03:38.013
contains mainly keratinocytes
and melanocytes.

01:03:38.950 --> 01:03:43.060
Sunlight exposure increased
p53 activity in the skin.

01:03:43.060 --> 01:03:48.060
And p53 activity was required
for the downstream increases

01:03:48.060 --> 01:03:51.030
in ovarian size, in testicular size,

01:03:51.030 --> 01:03:55.580
in testosterone increases,
in the estrogen increases,

01:03:55.580 --> 01:03:57.310
and the various other changes

01:03:57.310 --> 01:04:00.020
that they observed at
the physiological level

01:04:00.020 --> 01:04:03.110
when animals or humans
were exposed to sunlight.

01:04:03.110 --> 01:04:04.960
So these data are important
because what they mean

01:04:04.960 --> 01:04:06.950
is that not only is it important

01:04:06.950 --> 01:04:09.870
that we get sunlight
exposure early in the day

01:04:09.870 --> 01:04:11.690
and throughout the day to our eyes,

01:04:11.690 --> 01:04:14.050
at least as much as is safely possible,

01:04:14.050 --> 01:04:16.750
but that we also need to
get UVB sunlight exposure

01:04:16.750 --> 01:04:21.070
onto our skin if we want to
activate this p53 pathway

01:04:21.070 --> 01:04:25.690
in keratinocytes and the
testosterone and estrogen increases

01:04:25.690 --> 01:04:28.430
that are downstream of that p53 pathway.

01:04:28.430 --> 01:04:33.270
So even though the gene knockout
studies were done on mice,

01:04:33.270 --> 01:04:36.630
they clearly show that if
you remove p53 from the skin,

01:04:36.630 --> 01:04:39.240
that these effects simply do not occur.

01:04:39.240 --> 01:04:41.640
So in terms of thinking about a protocol

01:04:41.640 --> 01:04:43.560
to increase testosterone and estrogen,

01:04:43.560 --> 01:04:45.900
mood and feelings of passion,

01:04:45.900 --> 01:04:47.800
the idea is that you would want

01:04:47.800 --> 01:04:51.600
to get these two to
three exposures per week,

01:04:51.600 --> 01:04:55.510
minimum of 20 to 30 minutes
of sunlight exposure

01:04:55.510 --> 01:04:57.490
onto as much of your body

01:04:57.490 --> 01:04:59.840
as you can reasonably expose it to.

01:04:59.840 --> 01:05:02.080
And when I say reasonably, I mean,

01:05:02.080 --> 01:05:04.410
of course you have to
obey cultural constraints,

01:05:04.410 --> 01:05:05.710
decency constraints.

01:05:05.710 --> 01:05:08.690
And of course you have
to also obey the fact

01:05:08.690 --> 01:05:10.660
that sunlight can burn your skin.

01:05:10.660 --> 01:05:12.967
So many people are probably going to ask,

01:05:12.967 --> 01:05:15.250
"What happens if you wear sunscreen?"

01:05:15.250 --> 01:05:19.730
Well, in theory, because
sunscreen has UV protection,

01:05:19.730 --> 01:05:21.880
it would block some of these effects.

01:05:21.880 --> 01:05:22.713
Now I'm not suggesting

01:05:22.713 --> 01:05:24.620
that people do away
with sunscreen entirely.

01:05:24.620 --> 01:05:27.810
I do hope to do an episode all
about sunscreen in the future

01:05:27.810 --> 01:05:31.000
because sunscreen is a bit
of a controversial topic.

01:05:31.000 --> 01:05:32.760
Skin cancers are a real thing,

01:05:32.760 --> 01:05:35.660
and many people are especially
prone to skin cancer,

01:05:35.660 --> 01:05:37.570
so you need to take that seriously.

01:05:37.570 --> 01:05:39.840
Some people are not very
prone to skin cancers

01:05:39.840 --> 01:05:42.660
and can tolerate much more sun exposure.

01:05:42.660 --> 01:05:44.510
You're probably familiar
with the simple fact

01:05:44.510 --> 01:05:47.930
that if you've gone outside
on the beach with friends,

01:05:47.930 --> 01:05:49.990
some people get burned
very easily, others don't.

01:05:49.990 --> 01:05:52.470
So you really should prioritize the health

01:05:52.470 --> 01:05:54.880
and the avoidance of sunburn on your skin.

01:05:54.880 --> 01:05:58.780
However, these data and
other data point to the fact

01:05:58.780 --> 01:06:01.540
that we should all probably be striving

01:06:01.540 --> 01:06:04.210
to get more sunlight
exposure onto our skin

01:06:04.210 --> 01:06:05.710
during the winter months

01:06:05.710 --> 01:06:08.970
and still getting sunlight
exposure onto our skin

01:06:08.970 --> 01:06:10.050
in the summer months,

01:06:10.050 --> 01:06:12.800
provided we can do that
without damaging our skin.

01:06:12.800 --> 01:06:15.690
Another set of very impressive
effects of UVB light,

01:06:15.690 --> 01:06:16.780
whether or not it comes from sunlight

01:06:16.780 --> 01:06:18.950
or from an artificial source,

01:06:18.950 --> 01:06:22.440
is the effect of UVB light
on our tolerance for pain.

01:06:22.440 --> 01:06:24.350
It turns out that our tolerance for pain

01:06:24.350 --> 01:06:26.190
varies across the year

01:06:26.190 --> 01:06:29.390
and that our pain tolerance is increased

01:06:29.390 --> 01:06:32.160
in longer day conditions.

01:06:32.160 --> 01:06:34.850
And as we saw with the effects of UVB

01:06:34.850 --> 01:06:36.683
on hormones and mating,

01:06:37.840 --> 01:06:42.237
again, this is occurring
via UVB exposure to the skin

01:06:42.237 --> 01:06:45.160
and UVB exposure to the eyes.

01:06:45.160 --> 01:06:46.750
I want to just describe two studies

01:06:46.750 --> 01:06:49.520
that really capture the
essence of these results.

01:06:49.520 --> 01:06:52.540
I'm going to discuss these in
kind of a top contour fashion.

01:06:52.540 --> 01:06:54.260
I won't go into it as quite as much depth

01:06:54.260 --> 01:06:55.460
as I did the last study,

01:06:55.460 --> 01:06:57.960
but I will provide links
to these studies as well.

01:06:59.010 --> 01:07:00.910
The first study is entitled

01:07:00.910 --> 01:07:04.600
Skin Exposure to Ultraviolet
B Rapidly Activates

01:07:04.600 --> 01:07:07.570
Systemic, Neuroendocrine, and
Immunosuppressive Responses.

01:07:07.570 --> 01:07:08.403
And you might hear that and think,

01:07:08.403 --> 01:07:09.900
"Oh, immunosuppressive that's bad."

01:07:09.900 --> 01:07:14.890
But basically what they observed
is that even one exposure

01:07:14.890 --> 01:07:18.490
to UVB light changed the
output of particular hormones

01:07:18.490 --> 01:07:20.100
and neurochemicals in the body,

01:07:20.100 --> 01:07:23.670
such as corticotropin
hormone and beta-endorphins,

01:07:23.670 --> 01:07:25.750
which are these endogenous opioids.

01:07:25.750 --> 01:07:27.330
We've all heard of the opioid crisis,

01:07:27.330 --> 01:07:29.500
which is people getting
addicted to opioids

01:07:29.500 --> 01:07:32.830
that they are taking in
drug form, pharmaceuticals.

01:07:32.830 --> 01:07:35.090
But here I'm referring to endorphins

01:07:35.090 --> 01:07:37.570
that our body naturally
manufactures and releases

01:07:37.570 --> 01:07:39.110
in order to counter pain

01:07:39.110 --> 01:07:44.110
and act as somewhat of a
psychological soother also,

01:07:44.130 --> 01:07:46.410
because, of course, physical
pain and emotional pain

01:07:46.410 --> 01:07:48.740
are intimately linked
in the brain and body.

01:07:48.740 --> 01:07:51.980
What they found was that
exposure to UVB light

01:07:51.980 --> 01:07:55.080
increased the release of
these beta-endorphins.

01:07:55.080 --> 01:07:56.640
It caused essentially the release

01:07:56.640 --> 01:07:59.410
of an endogenous pain killer.

01:07:59.410 --> 01:08:03.040
Now, a second study that
came out very recently,

01:08:03.040 --> 01:08:04.810
just this last week, in fact,

01:08:04.810 --> 01:08:06.070
published in the journal Neuron,

01:08:06.070 --> 01:08:08.290
Cell Press journal, excellent journal,

01:08:08.290 --> 01:08:10.290
is entitled A Visual Circuit Related

01:08:10.290 --> 01:08:12.420
to the Periaqueductal Gray Area

01:08:12.420 --> 01:08:15.330
for the Antinociceptive Effects
of Bright Light Treatment.

01:08:15.330 --> 01:08:18.010
I'll translate a little
bit of that for you.

01:08:18.010 --> 01:08:21.650
The periaqueductal gray is
a region of the mid-brain

01:08:21.650 --> 01:08:24.180
that contains a lot of neurons

01:08:24.180 --> 01:08:26.890
that can release endogenous opioids,

01:08:26.890 --> 01:08:30.850
things like beta-enkephalin,
things like enkephalin,

01:08:30.850 --> 01:08:32.250
things like mu opioid.

01:08:32.250 --> 01:08:34.360
These are all names of chemicals

01:08:34.360 --> 01:08:35.670
that your body can manufacture

01:08:35.670 --> 01:08:37.650
that act as endogenous pain killers

01:08:37.650 --> 01:08:39.520
and increase your tolerance for pain.

01:08:39.520 --> 01:08:41.930
They actually make you
feel less pain overall

01:08:41.930 --> 01:08:43.780
by shutting down some of the neurons

01:08:43.780 --> 01:08:47.150
that perceive pain or by
reducing their activity.

01:08:47.150 --> 01:08:48.690
Not to a dangerous level, right?

01:08:48.690 --> 01:08:50.170
They're not going to
block the pain response

01:08:50.170 --> 01:08:52.660
so that you burn yourself unnecessarily

01:08:52.660 --> 01:08:54.200
or harm yourself unnecessarily,

01:08:54.200 --> 01:08:57.563
but they act a bit of a
pain killer from the inside.

01:08:59.010 --> 01:09:01.310
If you heard the word antinociceptive,

01:09:01.310 --> 01:09:06.310
nociception is basically
the perception or the way

01:09:06.490 --> 01:09:09.460
in which neurons respond
to painful stimuli.

01:09:09.460 --> 01:09:11.730
So you can think of nociceptive events

01:09:11.730 --> 01:09:14.090
in your nervous system as painful events.

01:09:14.090 --> 01:09:15.760
And there I'm using a broad brush.

01:09:15.760 --> 01:09:17.557
I realized that the
experts in pain will say,

01:09:17.557 --> 01:09:19.550
"Oh, it's not really a pain circuit,"

01:09:19.550 --> 01:09:20.500
et cetera, et cetera.

01:09:20.500 --> 01:09:22.890
But for sake of today's discussion,

01:09:22.890 --> 01:09:26.520
it's fair to say that nociception
is the perception of pain.

01:09:26.520 --> 01:09:29.550
So if this title is A
Visual Circuit Related

01:09:29.550 --> 01:09:31.360
to the Periaqueductal Gray,

01:09:31.360 --> 01:09:34.240
which is this area that releases
these endogenous opioids

01:09:34.240 --> 01:09:36.870
for the antinociceptive,
the anti-pain effects

01:09:36.870 --> 01:09:38.383
of bright light treatment,

01:09:40.180 --> 01:09:41.570
the key finding of this study

01:09:41.570 --> 01:09:46.570
is that it is light landing
on the eyes and captured

01:09:46.780 --> 01:09:49.020
by the specific cells I
was talking about earlier,

01:09:49.020 --> 01:09:51.827
those intrinsically photosensitive
melanopsin ganglion cells

01:09:51.827 --> 01:09:53.100
is the long name for them,

01:09:53.100 --> 01:09:54.950
but these particular neurons in your eye,

01:09:54.950 --> 01:09:57.320
and in my eye incidentally,

01:09:57.320 --> 01:10:00.640
that communicate with
particular brain areas.

01:10:00.640 --> 01:10:01.750
These brain areas have names.

01:10:01.750 --> 01:10:04.090
If you want to know
them, for you aficionados

01:10:04.090 --> 01:10:05.760
or for you ultra curious folks,

01:10:05.760 --> 01:10:08.150
they have names like the ventral
lateral geniculate nucleus

01:10:08.150 --> 01:10:09.620
and the intergeniculate leaflet.

01:10:09.620 --> 01:10:10.820
The names don't matter.

01:10:10.820 --> 01:10:13.430
The point is that light
landing on the eyes

01:10:13.430 --> 01:10:16.740
is captured by these melanopsin cells.

01:10:16.740 --> 01:10:18.040
They absorb that light,

01:10:18.040 --> 01:10:20.250
translate that light
into electrical signals

01:10:20.250 --> 01:10:22.040
that are handed off to areas of the brain,

01:10:22.040 --> 01:10:24.490
such as the ventral geniculate.

01:10:24.490 --> 01:10:27.690
And then the ventral
geniculate communicates

01:10:27.690 --> 01:10:30.320
with this periaqueductal gray area

01:10:30.320 --> 01:10:34.000
to evoke the release of
these endogenous opioids

01:10:34.000 --> 01:10:38.020
that soothe you and lead
to less perception of pain.

01:10:38.020 --> 01:10:39.410
This is a really important study

01:10:39.410 --> 01:10:43.390
because it's long been
known that in longer days

01:10:43.390 --> 01:10:44.670
or in bright light environments,

01:10:44.670 --> 01:10:47.403
we tolerate emotional
and physical pain better.

01:10:48.600 --> 01:10:50.030
Previous studies had shown

01:10:50.030 --> 01:10:53.030
that it is light landing on our skin

01:10:53.030 --> 01:10:55.500
that mediates that
effect, but only in part.

01:10:55.500 --> 01:10:57.440
It couldn't explain the entire effect.

01:10:57.440 --> 01:10:59.420
This very recent study indicates

01:10:59.420 --> 01:11:02.390
that it's also light arriving at the eyes,

01:11:02.390 --> 01:11:05.200
and in this case, again, UVB
light, ultraviolet blue light

01:11:05.200 --> 01:11:07.283
of the sort that comes from sunlight,

01:11:08.400 --> 01:11:11.020
that is triggering these anti-pain

01:11:11.020 --> 01:11:12.740
or pain-relieving pathways.

01:11:12.740 --> 01:11:15.230
So once again, we have
two parallel pathways.

01:11:15.230 --> 01:11:16.360
This is a theme you're going to hear

01:11:16.360 --> 01:11:19.080
over and over and over again,
not just in this episode,

01:11:19.080 --> 01:11:21.510
but in all episodes of
the Huberman Lab Podcast,

01:11:21.510 --> 01:11:24.810
because this is the way that
your brain and body are built.

01:11:24.810 --> 01:11:27.960
Nature rarely relies on one mechanism

01:11:27.960 --> 01:11:30.230
in order to create an
important phenomenon,

01:11:30.230 --> 01:11:32.460
and pain relief is an
important phenomenon.

01:11:32.460 --> 01:11:35.950
So we now have at least two
examples of the potent effects

01:11:35.950 --> 01:11:39.610
of UVB light exposure to
the skin and to the eyes.

01:11:39.610 --> 01:11:44.008
One involving activation of testosterone

01:11:44.008 --> 01:11:46.380
and estrogen pathways,
as it relates to mating,

01:11:46.380 --> 01:11:50.250
and another that relates to
reducing the total amount

01:11:50.250 --> 01:11:51.660
of pain that we experience

01:11:51.660 --> 01:11:54.240
in response to any painful stimuli.

01:11:54.240 --> 01:11:57.190
So for those of you that are
thinking tools and protocols,

01:11:57.190 --> 01:12:00.170
if you're somebody who's
experiencing chronic pain,

01:12:00.170 --> 01:12:01.800
provided you can do it safely,

01:12:01.800 --> 01:12:06.220
try to get some UVB exposure,
ideally from sunlight.

01:12:06.220 --> 01:12:07.960
I think the 20 to 30-minute protocol,

01:12:07.960 --> 01:12:10.570
two or three times per
week is an excellent one,

01:12:10.570 --> 01:12:14.320
seems like a fairly low
dose of UVB light exposure.

01:12:14.320 --> 01:12:17.380
It's hard to imagine getting
much damage to the skin.

01:12:17.380 --> 01:12:19.780
Of course, if you have
very sensitive skin,

01:12:19.780 --> 01:12:22.070
or if you live in an area of the world

01:12:22.070 --> 01:12:23.347
that is very, very bright

01:12:23.347 --> 01:12:26.420
and has intense sunlight at
particular times of year,

01:12:26.420 --> 01:12:27.870
you'll want to be cautious.

01:12:27.870 --> 01:12:30.490
Heed the warnings and
considerations about sunscreen

01:12:30.490 --> 01:12:32.920
that I talked about earlier,
or about wearing a hat.

01:12:32.920 --> 01:12:34.900
But the point is very clear.

01:12:34.900 --> 01:12:38.300
Most of us should be
getting more UVB exposure

01:12:38.300 --> 01:12:39.163
from sunlight.

01:12:40.140 --> 01:12:42.580
I can already hear the
screams within the comments

01:12:42.580 --> 01:12:44.577
or rather the questions
within the comments, saying,

01:12:44.577 --> 01:12:46.260
"Well, what if I live
in a part of the world

01:12:46.260 --> 01:12:48.790
where I don't get much UVB exposure?"

01:12:48.790 --> 01:12:50.989
And I want to emphasize something
that I've also emphasized

01:12:50.989 --> 01:12:53.160
in the many discussions on this podcast

01:12:53.160 --> 01:12:55.500
related to sleep and circadian
rhythms and alertness,

01:12:55.500 --> 01:12:59.410
which is even on a cloud-covered day,

01:12:59.410 --> 01:13:03.190
you are going to get far
more light energy, photons

01:13:03.190 --> 01:13:06.670
through cloud cover than
you are going to get

01:13:06.670 --> 01:13:10.180
from an indoor light source,
an artificial light source.

01:13:10.180 --> 01:13:12.730
I can't emphasize this enough.

01:13:12.730 --> 01:13:14.090
If you look outside in the morning

01:13:14.090 --> 01:13:16.130
and you see some sunlight,

01:13:16.130 --> 01:13:17.780
if you see some sunlight
throughout the day,

01:13:17.780 --> 01:13:20.410
you would do yourself a great favor

01:13:20.410 --> 01:13:22.140
to try and chase some of that sunlight

01:13:22.140 --> 01:13:25.130
and get into that sunlight
to expose your eyes

01:13:25.130 --> 01:13:28.170
and your skin to that sunlight
as much as you safely can.

01:13:28.170 --> 01:13:30.610
And when I say as much as you safely can,

01:13:30.610 --> 01:13:31.940
never ever look at any light,

01:13:31.940 --> 01:13:33.670
artificial, sunlight, or otherwise,

01:13:33.670 --> 01:13:35.340
that's so bright that
it's painful to look at.

01:13:35.340 --> 01:13:36.730
It's fine to get that light

01:13:36.730 --> 01:13:38.860
arriving on your eyes indirectly.

01:13:38.860 --> 01:13:41.380
It's fine to wear eyeglasses
or contact lenses.

01:13:41.380 --> 01:13:43.640
In fact, if you think about
the biology of the eye

01:13:43.640 --> 01:13:45.130
and the way that those lenses work,

01:13:45.130 --> 01:13:47.520
that you will just serve
to focus that light

01:13:47.520 --> 01:13:50.296
onto the very cells that
you want those light beams

01:13:50.296 --> 01:13:51.950
to be delivered to,

01:13:51.950 --> 01:13:54.220
whereas sunglasses that
are highly reflective

01:13:54.220 --> 01:13:56.050
or trying to get your sunlight exposure

01:13:56.050 --> 01:13:58.010
through a windshield of a car

01:13:58.010 --> 01:14:00.980
or through a window simply won't work.

01:14:00.980 --> 01:14:02.100
I'm sorry to tell you,

01:14:02.100 --> 01:14:06.250
but most windows are designed
to filter out the UVB light.

01:14:06.250 --> 01:14:09.050
And if you're somebody who's
really keen on blue blockers

01:14:09.050 --> 01:14:11.040
and you're wearing your
blue blockers all day,

01:14:11.040 --> 01:14:13.060
well, don't wear them outside.

01:14:13.060 --> 01:14:15.950
And in fact, you're probably
doing yourself a disservice

01:14:15.950 --> 01:14:18.310
by wearing them in the
morning and in the daytime.

01:14:18.310 --> 01:14:20.140
There certainly is a
place for blue blockers

01:14:20.140 --> 01:14:21.350
in the evening and nighttime,

01:14:21.350 --> 01:14:24.170
if you're having issues with
falling and staying asleep.

01:14:24.170 --> 01:14:26.070
But if you think about it, blue blockers,

01:14:26.070 --> 01:14:26.903
what they're really doing

01:14:26.903 --> 01:14:30.920
is blocking those short wavelength,
UVB wavelengths of light

01:14:30.920 --> 01:14:33.860
that you so desperately need
to arrive at your retina

01:14:33.860 --> 01:14:35.990
and of course, also onto your skin

01:14:35.990 --> 01:14:38.660
in order to get these
powerful biological effects

01:14:38.660 --> 01:14:40.733
on hormones and on pain reduction.

01:14:42.090 --> 01:14:44.370
And in terms of skin exposure,

01:14:44.370 --> 01:14:46.590
these data also might make
you think a little bit

01:14:46.590 --> 01:14:48.160
about whether or not you
should wear short sleeves

01:14:48.160 --> 01:14:48.993
or long sleeves,

01:14:48.993 --> 01:14:51.830
whether or not you want to wear
shorts or a skirt or pants.

01:14:51.830 --> 01:14:53.960
It's all going to depend
on the context of your life

01:14:53.960 --> 01:14:56.320
and the social and other variables

01:14:56.320 --> 01:14:57.850
that are important, of course.

01:14:57.850 --> 01:15:00.590
I don't know each and every
one of your circumstances,

01:15:00.590 --> 01:15:05.480
so I can't tell you to do
X or Y or Z, nor would I,

01:15:05.480 --> 01:15:07.220
but you might take into consideration

01:15:07.220 --> 01:15:10.330
that it is the total
amount of skin exposure

01:15:10.330 --> 01:15:14.210
that is going to allow you to
capture more or fewer photons,

01:15:14.210 --> 01:15:16.230
depending on, for instance,

01:15:16.230 --> 01:15:18.030
if you're completely cloaked in clothing

01:15:18.030 --> 01:15:21.460
and you're just exposed in
the hands, neck, and face

01:15:21.460 --> 01:15:22.640
such as I am now,

01:15:22.640 --> 01:15:25.150
or whether or not you're
outside in shorts and a T-shirt,

01:15:25.150 --> 01:15:28.360
you're going to get very,
very different patterns

01:15:28.360 --> 01:15:30.840
of biological signaling activation

01:15:30.840 --> 01:15:32.190
in those two circumstances.

01:15:32.190 --> 01:15:33.930
Many of you I'm guessing are wondering

01:15:33.930 --> 01:15:37.170
whether or not you should
seek out UVB exposure

01:15:37.170 --> 01:15:39.660
throughout the entire year
or only in the summer months.

01:15:39.660 --> 01:15:41.680
And that's sort of going to depend

01:15:41.680 --> 01:15:45.260
on whether or not you
experience depression

01:15:45.260 --> 01:15:46.530
in the winter months,

01:15:46.530 --> 01:15:48.920
so called seasonal effective disorder.

01:15:48.920 --> 01:15:51.190
Some people have mild, some
people have severe forms

01:15:51.190 --> 01:15:52.310
of seasonal effective disorder.

01:15:52.310 --> 01:15:55.190
Some people love the fall and
winter and the shorter days.

01:15:55.190 --> 01:15:56.920
They love bundling up.
They love the leaves.

01:15:56.920 --> 01:15:58.210
They love the snow, they love the cold,

01:15:58.210 --> 01:16:00.170
and they don't experience
those psychological lows.

01:16:00.170 --> 01:16:01.960
So it varies tremendously.

01:16:01.960 --> 01:16:03.840
And there are genetic differences

01:16:03.840 --> 01:16:07.830
and birthplace origin differences
that relate to all this,

01:16:07.830 --> 01:16:11.453
but really it has to be considered
on a case-by-case basis.

01:16:12.680 --> 01:16:15.840
I personally believe,
and this was reinforced

01:16:15.840 --> 01:16:19.080
by the director of the chronobiology unit

01:16:19.080 --> 01:16:21.620
at the National Institutes of
Mental Health, Samer Hattar,

01:16:21.620 --> 01:16:25.120
that we would all do well
to get more UVB exposure

01:16:25.120 --> 01:16:27.770
from sunlight throughout the entire year,

01:16:27.770 --> 01:16:29.260
provided we aren't burning our skin

01:16:29.260 --> 01:16:31.423
or damaging our eyes in some way.

01:16:32.470 --> 01:16:34.800
In addition to that,
during the winter months,

01:16:34.800 --> 01:16:38.060
if you do experience some drop in energy

01:16:38.060 --> 01:16:43.030
or increase in depression
or psychological lows,

01:16:43.030 --> 01:16:47.500
it can be very beneficial
to access a SAD lamp.

01:16:47.500 --> 01:16:49.350
Or if you don't want to buy a SAD lamp,

01:16:49.350 --> 01:16:51.270
'cause oftentimes they
can be very expensive,

01:16:51.270 --> 01:16:55.120
you might do well to simply
get a LED lighting panel.

01:16:55.120 --> 01:16:56.490
I've described one before.

01:16:56.490 --> 01:17:00.170
And I want to emphasize that I
have no affiliation whatsoever

01:17:00.170 --> 01:17:01.870
to these commercial sources,

01:17:01.870 --> 01:17:03.720
but I've described one before
and I'll describe it again.

01:17:03.720 --> 01:17:06.160
And we can provide a link to
a couple examples of these

01:17:06.160 --> 01:17:09.450
in the show, in the show
note captions, excuse me.

01:17:09.450 --> 01:17:14.450
This is a 930 to 1,000
lux, L-U-X, light source

01:17:14.840 --> 01:17:16.400
that's designed for drawing.

01:17:16.400 --> 01:17:17.740
It's literally a drawing box.

01:17:17.740 --> 01:17:20.510
It's a thin panel. It's
about the size of a laptop.

01:17:20.510 --> 01:17:22.730
Very inexpensive compared
to the typical SAD lamp.

01:17:22.730 --> 01:17:23.563
I actually have one,

01:17:23.563 --> 01:17:25.550
and I position it on my desk all day long.

01:17:25.550 --> 01:17:27.780
I also happen to have
skylights above my desk.

01:17:27.780 --> 01:17:29.910
I'm fairly sensitive to
the effects of light.

01:17:29.910 --> 01:17:31.290
So in longer days I feel much better

01:17:31.290 --> 01:17:32.230
than I do in shorter days.

01:17:32.230 --> 01:17:33.150
I've never suffered

01:17:33.150 --> 01:17:35.180
from full-blown seasonal
effective disorder,

01:17:35.180 --> 01:17:38.730
but I keep that light
source on throughout the day

01:17:38.730 --> 01:17:39.690
throughout the year.

01:17:39.690 --> 01:17:42.110
But I also make it a point to
get outside and get sunlight

01:17:42.110 --> 01:17:44.550
early in the morning and several
times throughout the day.

01:17:44.550 --> 01:17:47.270
And if it's particularly overcast outside

01:17:47.270 --> 01:17:49.670
or there just doesn't seem
to be a lot of sunlight

01:17:49.670 --> 01:17:50.960
coming through those clouds,

01:17:50.960 --> 01:17:53.540
I will try to look at that light source

01:17:53.540 --> 01:17:54.880
a little bit more each day

01:17:54.880 --> 01:17:57.420
in order to trigger these mechanisms.

01:17:57.420 --> 01:18:00.160
Now, some people may
desire to get UVB exposure

01:18:00.160 --> 01:18:02.160
to their skin and they want to do that

01:18:02.160 --> 01:18:03.920
through sources other than sunlight.

01:18:03.920 --> 01:18:06.150
And there it's a little
bit more complicated.

01:18:06.150 --> 01:18:08.330
There are, of course, tanning salons,

01:18:08.330 --> 01:18:10.700
which basically are beds of UVB light.

01:18:10.700 --> 01:18:12.160
That's really all they are.

01:18:12.160 --> 01:18:13.130
I've never been to one.

01:18:13.130 --> 01:18:14.630
I know people do frequent them

01:18:14.630 --> 01:18:16.130
in certain parts of the world.

01:18:17.070 --> 01:18:19.320
There, of course, people
are covering their eyes.

01:18:19.320 --> 01:18:21.780
They are only getting UVB
exposure to their skin, typically

01:18:21.780 --> 01:18:24.920
because the UVB exposure,
or intensities rather,

01:18:24.920 --> 01:18:26.560
tend to be very, very high.

01:18:26.560 --> 01:18:27.920
And so you can actually damage your eyes.

01:18:27.920 --> 01:18:29.940
If you're looking at a very, very bright

01:18:29.940 --> 01:18:32.530
artificial UVB source up close.

01:18:32.530 --> 01:18:35.190
So you really have to explore
these options for yourself.

01:18:35.190 --> 01:18:37.150
Sunlight of course, being the original

01:18:37.150 --> 01:18:40.780
and still the best way
to get UVB exposure.

01:18:40.780 --> 01:18:44.510
So without knowing your particular
circumstances, finances,

01:18:44.510 --> 01:18:47.390
genetics, or place of origin, et cetera,

01:18:47.390 --> 01:18:48.590
I can't know whether or not

01:18:48.590 --> 01:18:49.890
you need to use artificial sources.

01:18:49.890 --> 01:18:51.650
You're going to have to gauge that.

01:18:51.650 --> 01:18:55.250
Meanwhile, getting outside, looking at

01:18:55.250 --> 01:18:59.330
and getting some exposure
of UVB onto your skin

01:18:59.330 --> 01:19:00.550
is going to be beneficial

01:19:00.550 --> 01:19:03.380
for the vast majority of people out there.

01:19:03.380 --> 01:19:05.530
And in fact, it's even
going to be beneficial

01:19:05.530 --> 01:19:07.123
for people that are blind.

01:19:08.130 --> 01:19:10.770
People that are blind,
provided they still have eyes,

01:19:10.770 --> 01:19:14.230
often maintain these melanopsin cells.

01:19:14.230 --> 01:19:16.240
So even if you're low vision or no vision,

01:19:16.240 --> 01:19:18.110
getting UVB exposure to your eyes

01:19:18.110 --> 01:19:21.060
can be very beneficial for sake of mood,

01:19:21.060 --> 01:19:24.400
hormone pathways, pain
reduction, and so forth.

01:19:24.400 --> 01:19:28.820
A cautionary note, people who
have retinitis pigmentosa,

01:19:28.820 --> 01:19:31.390
macular degeneration, or glaucoma,

01:19:31.390 --> 01:19:34.730
as well as people who are
especially prone to skin cancers

01:19:34.730 --> 01:19:37.660
should definitely consult
with your ophthalmologist

01:19:37.660 --> 01:19:40.130
and dermatologist before
you start increasing

01:19:40.130 --> 01:19:41.970
the total amount of UVB exposure

01:19:41.970 --> 01:19:44.990
that you're getting from any
source, sunlight or otherwise.

01:19:44.990 --> 01:19:47.780
There are additional, very
interesting and powerful effects

01:19:47.780 --> 01:19:52.483
of UVB light, in particular
on immune function.

01:19:54.060 --> 01:19:57.330
All the organs of our
body are inside our skin.

01:19:57.330 --> 01:20:00.500
And so information about
external conditions,

01:20:00.500 --> 01:20:02.250
meaning the environment that we're in,

01:20:02.250 --> 01:20:06.410
need to be communicated to the
various organs of our body.

01:20:06.410 --> 01:20:08.700
Some of them have more direct access

01:20:08.700 --> 01:20:10.630
to what's going on outside.

01:20:10.630 --> 01:20:13.830
So for instance, the cells in your brain

01:20:13.830 --> 01:20:15.640
that reside right over
the roof of your mouth,

01:20:15.640 --> 01:20:17.468
your hypothalamus, they
control hormone output,

01:20:17.468 --> 01:20:20.600
and they control the biological functions

01:20:20.600 --> 01:20:21.980
that we call circadian functions,

01:20:21.980 --> 01:20:24.470
the ones that change every 24 hours.

01:20:24.470 --> 01:20:27.100
Well, those are just
one or two connections,

01:20:27.100 --> 01:20:29.800
meaning synapses away from
those cells in your eye

01:20:29.800 --> 01:20:32.563
that perceive UB, UVB light, excuse me.

01:20:33.440 --> 01:20:36.010
Other organs of your
body, such as your spleen,

01:20:36.010 --> 01:20:38.920
which is involved in the
creation of molecules

01:20:38.920 --> 01:20:41.260
and cells that combat infection,

01:20:41.260 --> 01:20:42.990
well, those are a long ways away

01:20:42.990 --> 01:20:43.823
from those cell in your eye.

01:20:43.823 --> 01:20:47.280
And in fact, they're a long
ways away from your skin.

01:20:47.280 --> 01:20:48.940
There are beautiful studies showing

01:20:48.940 --> 01:20:53.260
that if we get more UVB
exposure from sunlight

01:20:53.260 --> 01:20:55.993
or from appropriate artificial sources,

01:20:57.260 --> 01:21:00.830
that spleen and immune
function are enhanced,

01:21:00.830 --> 01:21:03.160
and there's a very logical,

01:21:03.160 --> 01:21:06.070
well-established circuit
as to how that happens.

01:21:06.070 --> 01:21:09.350
Your brain actually
connects to your spleen.

01:21:09.350 --> 01:21:12.047
Now, it's not the case
that you can simply think,

01:21:12.047 --> 01:21:14.720
"Okay, spleen, turn on,
release killer cells,

01:21:14.720 --> 01:21:16.410
go out and combat infection."

01:21:16.410 --> 01:21:20.730
However, UVB light arriving on the eyes

01:21:20.730 --> 01:21:23.560
is known to trigger
activation of the neurons

01:21:23.560 --> 01:21:26.070
within the so-called
sympathetic nervous system.

01:21:26.070 --> 01:21:28.400
These neurons are part of the larger thing

01:21:28.400 --> 01:21:30.140
that we call the autonomic nervous system,

01:21:30.140 --> 01:21:34.210
meaning it's below or not
accessible by conscious control.

01:21:34.210 --> 01:21:35.590
It's the thing that
controls your heartbeat,

01:21:35.590 --> 01:21:38.060
controls your breathing
and that also activates

01:21:38.060 --> 01:21:41.040
or flips on the switch
of your immune system.

01:21:41.040 --> 01:21:43.600
When we get a lot of
UVB light in our eyes,

01:21:43.600 --> 01:21:46.900
or I should say sufficient
UVB light in our eyes,

01:21:46.900 --> 01:21:50.150
a particular channel, a
particular set of connections

01:21:50.150 --> 01:21:53.260
within the sympathetic
nervous system is activated,

01:21:53.260 --> 01:21:57.380
and our spleen deploys
immune cells and molecules

01:21:57.380 --> 01:22:00.330
that scavenge for and combat infection.

01:22:00.330 --> 01:22:03.900
So if you've noticed that
you get fewer colds and flus

01:22:03.900 --> 01:22:06.830
and other forms of illness
in the summer months,

01:22:06.830 --> 01:22:10.470
part of that could be because
of the increase in temperature

01:22:10.470 --> 01:22:11.303
in your environment,

01:22:11.303 --> 01:22:13.310
because typically longer
days are associated

01:22:13.310 --> 01:22:15.660
with more warmth in your environment

01:22:15.660 --> 01:22:16.850
as opposed to winter days,

01:22:16.850 --> 01:22:19.590
which are short when it
tends to be colder out.

01:22:19.590 --> 01:22:24.000
Well, that's true, but it's also the case

01:22:24.000 --> 01:22:26.480
that people around you
have fewer colds and flus

01:22:26.480 --> 01:22:29.680
and that you will get infected
with fewer colds and flus

01:22:29.680 --> 01:22:34.520
and other infections,
because if those infections,

01:22:34.520 --> 01:22:36.940
whether or not they're bacterial or viral,

01:22:36.940 --> 01:22:39.430
arrive in your body,
right, if you inhale them

01:22:39.430 --> 01:22:42.000
or they get into your
mouth or on your skin,

01:22:42.000 --> 01:22:45.550
your spleen meets those
infections with a greater output.

01:22:45.550 --> 01:22:47.580
In other words, the soldiers
of your immune system,

01:22:47.580 --> 01:22:50.240
the chemicals and cell
types of your immune system

01:22:50.240 --> 01:22:52.150
that combat infection

01:22:52.150 --> 01:22:56.490
are in a more ready,
deployed stance, if you will.

01:22:56.490 --> 01:22:58.210
If you want to know more
about the immune system

01:22:58.210 --> 01:22:59.070
and immune function,

01:22:59.070 --> 01:23:01.470
I did an entire episode
about the immune system

01:23:01.470 --> 01:23:04.850
and the brain, you can find
that at hubermanlab.com.

01:23:04.850 --> 01:23:05.950
We talk about cytokines,

01:23:05.950 --> 01:23:08.060
we talk about killer cells,
B cells, T cells, et cetera,

01:23:08.060 --> 01:23:09.540
a lot of detail there.

01:23:09.540 --> 01:23:11.760
So we often think about the summer months

01:23:11.760 --> 01:23:15.350
and the spring months as fewer
infections floating around.

01:23:15.350 --> 01:23:18.890
But in fact, there aren't fewer
infections floating around.

01:23:18.890 --> 01:23:21.500
We are simply better at
combating those infections,

01:23:21.500 --> 01:23:25.130
and therefore there's less
infection floating around.

01:23:25.130 --> 01:23:27.670
So we are still confronted
with a lot of infections.

01:23:27.670 --> 01:23:29.960
We're just able to combat them better.

01:23:29.960 --> 01:23:31.410
What does this mean in terms of a tool?

01:23:31.410 --> 01:23:33.880
What it means is that
during the winter months,

01:23:33.880 --> 01:23:38.610
we should be especially
conscious of accessing UVB light

01:23:38.610 --> 01:23:41.350
to enhance our spleen function,

01:23:41.350 --> 01:23:43.750
to make sure that our
sympathetic nervous system

01:23:43.750 --> 01:23:46.210
is activated to a sufficient level

01:23:46.210 --> 01:23:49.550
to keep our immune system
deploying all those killer T cells

01:23:49.550 --> 01:23:51.130
and B cells and cytokines

01:23:51.130 --> 01:23:52.890
so that when we encounter the infections,

01:23:52.890 --> 01:23:54.800
as we inevitably will, right,

01:23:54.800 --> 01:23:57.780
we're constantly being bombarded
with potential infections,

01:23:57.780 --> 01:24:00.280
that we can combat those infections well.

01:24:00.280 --> 01:24:02.020
And as just a brief aside,

01:24:02.020 --> 01:24:03.650
but I should mention, a brief aside

01:24:03.650 --> 01:24:07.290
that's related to tens of
thousands of quality studies,

01:24:07.290 --> 01:24:10.580
it is well known that
wound healing is faster

01:24:10.580 --> 01:24:13.940
when we are getting
sufficient UVB exposure.

01:24:13.940 --> 01:24:16.360
Typically, that's associated
with the longer days

01:24:16.360 --> 01:24:17.740
of spring and summer.

01:24:17.740 --> 01:24:21.430
It is known that turnover of hair cells,

01:24:21.430 --> 01:24:23.610
the very cells that
give rise to hair cells

01:24:23.610 --> 01:24:24.443
are called stem cells.

01:24:24.443 --> 01:24:27.080
They live in little
so-called niches in our skin

01:24:27.080 --> 01:24:28.510
with these hair stem cells,

01:24:28.510 --> 01:24:31.040
and your hair grows faster in longer days.

01:24:31.040 --> 01:24:34.490
That too is triggered by UVB exposure,

01:24:34.490 --> 01:24:37.560
not just to the skin, but to the eyes.

01:24:37.560 --> 01:24:38.480
That's right.

01:24:38.480 --> 01:24:39.670
There was a study published

01:24:39.670 --> 01:24:42.310
in the Proceedings of the
National Academy of Sciences

01:24:42.310 --> 01:24:46.260
a couple of years ago that
showed that the exposure

01:24:46.260 --> 01:24:48.590
of those melanopsin
ganglion cells in your eyes

01:24:48.590 --> 01:24:52.230
is absolutely critical for
triggering the turnover

01:24:52.230 --> 01:24:56.380
of stem cells in both the skin and hair,

01:24:56.380 --> 01:24:58.690
and also turns out in nails.

01:24:58.690 --> 01:25:00.810
So if you've noticed that your skin,

01:25:00.810 --> 01:25:03.630
your hair and your nails look
better and turn over more,

01:25:03.630 --> 01:25:06.610
meaning grow faster in longer days,

01:25:06.610 --> 01:25:08.100
that is not on a coincidence.

01:25:08.100 --> 01:25:09.830
That is not just your perception.

01:25:09.830 --> 01:25:13.550
In fact, hair grows more,
skin turns over more,

01:25:13.550 --> 01:25:14.920
meaning it's going to look more youthful.

01:25:14.920 --> 01:25:18.007
You're going to essentially
remove older skin cells

01:25:18.007 --> 01:25:20.360
and replace them with new cells,

01:25:20.360 --> 01:25:23.700
and all the renewing cells
and tissues of our body

01:25:23.700 --> 01:25:24.820
are going to proliferate,

01:25:24.820 --> 01:25:27.450
are going to recreate themselves more

01:25:27.450 --> 01:25:30.290
when we're getting sufficient
UVB light to our eyes

01:25:30.290 --> 01:25:32.010
and also to our skin.

01:25:32.010 --> 01:25:36.230
And so while some of you
may think of light therapies

01:25:36.230 --> 01:25:38.600
such as red light
therapies or UVB therapies

01:25:38.600 --> 01:25:41.050
as kind of new agey, or just biohacking,

01:25:41.050 --> 01:25:42.950
again, a phrase I don't particularly like,

01:25:42.950 --> 01:25:44.250
this notion of biohacking,

01:25:44.250 --> 01:25:47.470
'cause it implies using
one thing for a purpose

01:25:47.470 --> 01:25:49.743
that it was never tended to have,

01:25:50.700 --> 01:25:54.300
well, it turns out that
UVB exposure and red light,

01:25:54.300 --> 01:25:57.550
as we'll soon see, is a very potent form

01:25:57.550 --> 01:26:00.190
of increasing things like
wound healing and skin health

01:26:00.190 --> 01:26:04.710
for very logical
mechanistically backed reasons.

01:26:04.710 --> 01:26:07.520
So while I can't account for everything

01:26:07.520 --> 01:26:08.670
that's being promoted out there

01:26:08.670 --> 01:26:09.900
in terms of this light source

01:26:09.900 --> 01:26:11.310
will help your skin look more youthful

01:26:11.310 --> 01:26:13.463
or will help heal your scars,

01:26:14.400 --> 01:26:17.610
the mechanistic basis for
light having those effects

01:26:17.610 --> 01:26:19.190
makes total sense.

01:26:19.190 --> 01:26:21.290
But what you should consider, however,

01:26:21.290 --> 01:26:23.750
is that if the particular light therapy

01:26:23.750 --> 01:26:27.620
that you're considering
involves very local application

01:26:27.620 --> 01:26:32.610
rather than illuminating
broad swaths of skin,

01:26:32.610 --> 01:26:35.050
and if it has no
involvement with the eyes,

01:26:35.050 --> 01:26:38.900
meaning there's no delivery
of UVB or red light

01:26:38.900 --> 01:26:41.440
or the other light therapy to the eyes,

01:26:41.440 --> 01:26:44.980
it's probably not going to
be as potent a treatment

01:26:44.980 --> 01:26:48.160
as would a more systemic activation

01:26:48.160 --> 01:26:51.130
of larger areas of skin and the eyes.

01:26:51.130 --> 01:26:52.850
Now, again, a cautionary note,

01:26:52.850 --> 01:26:55.090
I don't want people taking
technologies that were designed

01:26:55.090 --> 01:26:58.300
for local application and
beaming those into the eyes.

01:26:58.300 --> 01:26:59.500
That could be very, very bad

01:26:59.500 --> 01:27:02.550
and damaging to your
retinal and other tissues.

01:27:02.550 --> 01:27:05.220
Certainly, wouldn't want
you taking bright light

01:27:05.220 --> 01:27:07.480
of very high intensity of any kind

01:27:07.480 --> 01:27:09.600
and getting cavalier about that.

01:27:09.600 --> 01:27:12.970
Typically, the local
illumination of say a wound

01:27:12.970 --> 01:27:14.410
or a particular patch of acne

01:27:14.410 --> 01:27:17.050
or some other form of skin treatment

01:27:17.050 --> 01:27:18.870
involves very high intensity light.

01:27:18.870 --> 01:27:20.470
And if the intensity is too high,

01:27:20.470 --> 01:27:22.410
you can actually damage that skin.

01:27:22.410 --> 01:27:24.910
And so as we'll talk
about in a few moments,

01:27:24.910 --> 01:27:27.410
most of those therapies for modifying skin

01:27:27.410 --> 01:27:31.640
involve actually burning
off a small, very thin layer

01:27:31.640 --> 01:27:33.510
at the top of the epidermis

01:27:33.510 --> 01:27:35.670
in efforts to trigger the renewal

01:27:35.670 --> 01:27:37.050
or the activation of stem cells

01:27:37.050 --> 01:27:39.890
that will replenish that with new cells.

01:27:39.890 --> 01:27:44.370
So there's a fine line to be
had between light therapies

01:27:44.370 --> 01:27:46.010
that are very localized and intense,

01:27:46.010 --> 01:27:48.190
which are designed to damage skin

01:27:48.190 --> 01:27:52.540
and cause reactivation of new stem cells,

01:27:52.540 --> 01:27:54.780
whether that's hair cells
or skin cells, et cetera,

01:27:54.780 --> 01:27:57.597
versus systemic activation
across broad swaths of skin

01:27:57.597 --> 01:27:58.430
and the eyes.

01:27:58.430 --> 01:28:00.910
You really have to consider
this on a case-by-case basis,

01:28:00.910 --> 01:28:03.740
but at least for now just consider

01:28:03.740 --> 01:28:08.240
that increases in
hormones, reduction in pain

01:28:08.240 --> 01:28:10.680
by way of increases in enkephalin

01:28:10.680 --> 01:28:12.720
and other endogenous opioids,

01:28:12.720 --> 01:28:15.720
improving immune status
by activating the spleen,

01:28:15.720 --> 01:28:17.410
and so on, and so on

01:28:17.410 --> 01:28:19.580
really are all the downstream consequence

01:28:19.580 --> 01:28:21.950
of illuminating large swaths of skin

01:28:21.950 --> 01:28:23.980
and making sure that those
neurons within the eye

01:28:23.980 --> 01:28:26.280
get their adequate UVB exposure

01:28:26.280 --> 01:28:28.270
or other light wavelength exposure,

01:28:28.270 --> 01:28:31.380
not simply beaming a
particular wavelength of light

01:28:31.380 --> 01:28:33.010
at a particular location on the body

01:28:33.010 --> 01:28:35.410
and hoping that that
particular illumination

01:28:35.410 --> 01:28:37.150
at a particular location on the body

01:28:37.150 --> 01:28:40.500
is going to somehow change
the biology at that location.

01:28:40.500 --> 01:28:43.350
Our biology just really
doesn't work that way.

01:28:43.350 --> 01:28:45.270
It's possible, but in general,

01:28:45.270 --> 01:28:48.450
systemic effects through
broad scale illumination

01:28:48.450 --> 01:28:49.730
and illumination to the eye,

01:28:49.730 --> 01:28:52.320
combined with local
treatments are very likely

01:28:52.320 --> 01:28:54.400
to be the ones that have the most success.

01:28:54.400 --> 01:28:55.730
Now, I'd like to shift our attention

01:28:55.730 --> 01:28:58.030
to the effects of light
on mood more specifically.

01:28:58.030 --> 01:28:58.863
We talked about this

01:28:58.863 --> 01:29:00.720
in terms of seasonal effective disorder,

01:29:00.720 --> 01:29:01.890
but many of us don't suffer

01:29:01.890 --> 01:29:03.410
from seasonal effective disorder.

01:29:03.410 --> 01:29:04.880
So I'd like to drill a little deeper

01:29:04.880 --> 01:29:07.670
into how light impacts mood.

01:29:07.670 --> 01:29:11.790
And here, I want to, again,
paraphrase the statements

01:29:11.790 --> 01:29:14.500
of Dr. Samer Hattar

01:29:14.500 --> 01:29:16.810
at the National Institutes
of Mental Health,

01:29:16.810 --> 01:29:18.960
I should mention the director
of the chronobiology unit

01:29:18.960 --> 01:29:20.700
at the National Institutes
of Mental Health

01:29:20.700 --> 01:29:25.700
and perhaps one of the top one
to two to three world experts

01:29:26.040 --> 01:29:28.230
in how light can impact mood, appetite,

01:29:28.230 --> 01:29:29.830
circadian rhythms, and so forth.

01:29:31.690 --> 01:29:33.070
Samer stated on the podcast,

01:29:33.070 --> 01:29:36.240
and he said in various
other venues as well,

01:29:36.240 --> 01:29:40.600
that getting as much UVB light
in our eyes and on our skin

01:29:40.600 --> 01:29:42.410
in the early day and throughout the day

01:29:42.410 --> 01:29:45.530
as is safely possible is going
to be beneficial for mood.

01:29:45.530 --> 01:29:48.140
There's also another time of day,

01:29:48.140 --> 01:29:49.850
or rather I should say a time of night

01:29:49.850 --> 01:29:54.450
in which UVB can be leveraged
in order to improve mood,

01:29:54.450 --> 01:29:56.370
but it's actually the
inverse of everything

01:29:56.370 --> 01:29:58.293
we've been talking about up until now.

01:29:59.410 --> 01:30:02.520
We have a particular neural
circuit that originates

01:30:02.520 --> 01:30:04.940
with those melanopsin cells in our eye

01:30:04.940 --> 01:30:07.250
that bypass all the areas of the brain

01:30:07.250 --> 01:30:09.150
associated with circadian clocks,

01:30:09.150 --> 01:30:11.590
so everything related to
sleep and wakefulness,

01:30:11.590 --> 01:30:14.800
that's specifically
dedicated to the pathways

01:30:14.800 --> 01:30:17.010
involving the release of
molecules like dopamine,

01:30:17.010 --> 01:30:19.940
the neuromodulator that's
associated with motivation,

01:30:19.940 --> 01:30:23.870
with feeling good, with feeling
like there's possibility

01:30:23.870 --> 01:30:25.800
in the world, and so on and so forth,

01:30:25.800 --> 01:30:26.960
and other molecules as well,

01:30:26.960 --> 01:30:29.750
including serotonin and some
of those endogenous opioids

01:30:29.750 --> 01:30:31.150
that we talked about before.

01:30:32.270 --> 01:30:34.390
That particular pathway
involves a brain structure

01:30:34.390 --> 01:30:36.750
called the perihabenular nucleus.

01:30:36.750 --> 01:30:39.740
The perihabenular nucleus gets input

01:30:39.740 --> 01:30:43.440
from the cells in the eye
that respond to UVB light,

01:30:43.440 --> 01:30:46.790
and frankly, to bright light
of other wavelengths as well,

01:30:46.790 --> 01:30:49.490
'cause as you recall, if
a light is bright enough,

01:30:49.490 --> 01:30:51.360
even if it's not UV or blue light,

01:30:51.360 --> 01:30:53.870
it can activate those cells in the eye.

01:30:53.870 --> 01:30:54.770
Those cells in the eye

01:30:54.770 --> 01:30:57.410
communicate to the perihabenular nucleus.

01:30:57.410 --> 01:31:01.110
And as it turns out, if
this pathway is activated

01:31:01.110 --> 01:31:04.780
at the wrong time of each 24-hour cycle,

01:31:04.780 --> 01:31:09.780
mood gets worse, dopamine
output gets worse,

01:31:09.780 --> 01:31:13.210
molecules that are there
specifically to make us feel good,

01:31:13.210 --> 01:31:16.103
actually are reduced in their output.

01:31:17.040 --> 01:31:20.460
So while UVB exposure in the
morning and throughout the day

01:31:21.400 --> 01:31:24.410
is going to be very
important for elevating

01:31:24.410 --> 01:31:26.530
and maintaining elevated mood,

01:31:26.530 --> 01:31:30.870
avoiding UVB light at
night is actually a way

01:31:30.870 --> 01:31:33.700
in which we can prevent activation

01:31:33.700 --> 01:31:36.920
of this eye to perihabenular pathway

01:31:36.920 --> 01:31:38.930
that can actually turn on depression.

01:31:38.930 --> 01:31:41.680
To be very direct and succinct about this,

01:31:41.680 --> 01:31:45.000
avoid exposure to UVB light
from artificial sources

01:31:45.850 --> 01:31:48.480
between the hours of 10:00 pm and 4:00 am.

01:31:48.480 --> 01:31:51.820
And if you're somebody
who suffers from low mood

01:31:51.820 --> 01:31:54.710
and overall has a kind of mild depression

01:31:54.710 --> 01:31:55.980
or even severe depression,

01:31:55.980 --> 01:31:57.930
of course, please see a psychiatrist,

01:31:57.930 --> 01:32:00.970
see a trained psychologist,
get that treated,

01:32:00.970 --> 01:32:05.970
but you would do especially
well to avoid UVB exposure

01:32:06.510 --> 01:32:09.610
from artificial sources, not
just from 10:00 pm to 4:00 am,

01:32:09.610 --> 01:32:13.000
but really be careful about
getting too much exposure to UVB

01:32:13.000 --> 01:32:14.410
even in the late evening,

01:32:14.410 --> 01:32:17.220
so 8:00 pm perhaps to 4:00 am.

01:32:17.220 --> 01:32:19.570
I can't emphasize this enough,

01:32:19.570 --> 01:32:21.790
that if you view UVB light,

01:32:21.790 --> 01:32:24.570
you activate those neurons
in your eye very potently.

01:32:24.570 --> 01:32:28.100
And if those cells communicate
to the perihabenular nucleus,

01:32:28.100 --> 01:32:28.933
which they do,

01:32:28.933 --> 01:32:31.650
you will truncate or reduce
the amount of dopamine

01:32:31.650 --> 01:32:32.500
that you release.

01:32:33.410 --> 01:32:36.160
So if you want to keep your mood elevated,

01:32:36.160 --> 01:32:38.920
get a lot of light, UVB
light, throughout the day,

01:32:38.920 --> 01:32:42.870
and at night, really be cautious
about getting UVB exposure

01:32:42.870 --> 01:32:44.160
from artificial sources.

01:32:44.160 --> 01:32:46.960
Now let's say you're somebody
who has no issues with mood.

01:32:46.960 --> 01:32:49.540
You're just the happiest
person all year long,

01:32:49.540 --> 01:32:52.560
or maybe you just have subtle
variations in your mood.

01:32:52.560 --> 01:32:53.860
You feel great about that.

01:32:55.030 --> 01:32:57.940
Turns out that you still
want to be very careful

01:32:57.940 --> 01:32:59.570
about light exposure

01:32:59.570 --> 01:33:02.870
between the hours of 10:00
pm or so, and 4:00 am,

01:33:02.870 --> 01:33:05.030
in fact, even during sleep.

01:33:05.030 --> 01:33:06.840
There's a recent study that just came out

01:33:06.840 --> 01:33:09.750
in the Proceedings of the
National Academy of Sciences,

01:33:09.750 --> 01:33:12.760
and it's entitled Light
Exposure During Sleep

01:33:12.760 --> 01:33:15.840
Impairs Cardiometabolic Function.

01:33:15.840 --> 01:33:17.690
This is a very interesting study

01:33:17.690 --> 01:33:20.220
where they took human
subjects, young adults,

01:33:20.220 --> 01:33:22.650
and having them sleep in rooms

01:33:22.650 --> 01:33:24.040
that had different lighting conditions,

01:33:24.040 --> 01:33:27.920
either dim light or slightly bright light.

01:33:27.920 --> 01:33:30.490
Now, many people can't fall
asleep in brightly lit rooms,

01:33:30.490 --> 01:33:31.978
so they acknowledge this.

01:33:31.978 --> 01:33:33.960
These were not very brightly lit rooms.

01:33:33.960 --> 01:33:36.260
These were rooms that
had just a little bit

01:33:36.260 --> 01:33:39.260
of overhead room lighting, a hundred lux,

01:33:39.260 --> 01:33:41.290
which is not very bright at all.

01:33:41.290 --> 01:33:43.920
Or they had them sleep in a
room that had very dim light,

01:33:43.920 --> 01:33:45.800
which is less than three lux.

01:33:45.800 --> 01:33:49.160
If you want to get a sense
of how bright three lux is

01:33:49.160 --> 01:33:50.780
versus a hundred lux,

01:33:50.780 --> 01:33:53.910
I would encourage you to download
the free app Light Meter.

01:33:53.910 --> 01:33:55.420
I have no relationship to the app.

01:33:55.420 --> 01:33:56.580
It's a pretty cool app, however.

01:33:56.580 --> 01:33:57.580
I've used it for a long time,

01:33:57.580 --> 01:34:00.080
where you can basically point your phone

01:34:00.080 --> 01:34:02.360
at a particular light
source, sun or otherwise,

01:34:02.360 --> 01:34:03.290
and you just press the button

01:34:03.290 --> 01:34:05.620
and it'll give you an
approximate readout of lux,

01:34:05.620 --> 01:34:07.720
which is the light intensity

01:34:07.720 --> 01:34:10.170
that the phone happens
to be staring out at

01:34:10.170 --> 01:34:11.003
at that location.

01:34:11.003 --> 01:34:14.080
It's not exact, but it's a pretty good

01:34:14.080 --> 01:34:17.460
back-of-the-envelope
measure of light intensity.

01:34:17.460 --> 01:34:20.410
So these subjects were either
sleeping in a very dim room,

01:34:20.410 --> 01:34:22.180
three lux is very, very dim,

01:34:22.180 --> 01:34:25.930
or a somewhat dim room, a hundred lux.

01:34:25.930 --> 01:34:29.210
In this study, they measured
things like melatonin levels.

01:34:29.210 --> 01:34:30.960
They looked at heart rate,

01:34:30.960 --> 01:34:35.940
they looked at measures of
insulin and glucose management.

01:34:35.940 --> 01:34:38.070
Now, in previous episodes,

01:34:38.070 --> 01:34:40.430
I've talked about how
glucose, blood sugar,

01:34:40.430 --> 01:34:42.430
is regulated by insulin

01:34:42.430 --> 01:34:43.960
because you don't want your glucose levels

01:34:43.960 --> 01:34:47.580
to be too high, hyperglycemia,
or too low, hypoglycemia.

01:34:47.580 --> 01:34:50.400
And the hormone insulin is
involved in sequestering

01:34:50.400 --> 01:34:52.520
and shuttling glucose in the bloodstream.

01:34:52.520 --> 01:34:55.410
Basically, how well you manage
glucose in the bloodstream

01:34:55.410 --> 01:34:58.490
can be indirectly measured
by your insulin levels.

01:34:58.490 --> 01:35:01.490
And it's well known that sleep deprivation

01:35:01.490 --> 01:35:06.490
can disrupt glucose regulation by insulin.

01:35:07.000 --> 01:35:09.400
However, in this study,
subjects were sleeping

01:35:09.400 --> 01:35:10.233
the whole night through.

01:35:10.233 --> 01:35:12.170
It just so happens that some
of the subjects were sleeping

01:35:12.170 --> 01:35:13.937
in this very dimly lit room, three lux,

01:35:13.937 --> 01:35:15.410
and other subjects were sleeping

01:35:15.410 --> 01:35:19.760
in a somewhat dimly lit
room, a hundred lux.

01:35:19.760 --> 01:35:21.730
What's incredible about this study

01:35:21.730 --> 01:35:24.180
is that both rooms were
sufficiently dimmed

01:35:24.180 --> 01:35:27.510
that melatonin levels were
not altered in either case.

01:35:27.510 --> 01:35:28.343
This is really key.

01:35:28.343 --> 01:35:31.500
It's not as if one group
experienced a lot of bright light

01:35:31.500 --> 01:35:33.640
through their eyelids and others did not.

01:35:33.640 --> 01:35:35.570
Melatonin levels were not disrupted.

01:35:35.570 --> 01:35:38.280
And given how potently
light can inhibit melatonin,

01:35:38.280 --> 01:35:39.790
this speaks to the fact

01:35:39.790 --> 01:35:42.520
that this very dim condition of three lux

01:35:42.520 --> 01:35:44.890
and the somewhat dim
condition of a hundred lux

01:35:44.890 --> 01:35:48.070
was not actually perceived by the subjects

01:35:48.070 --> 01:35:50.760
nor was it disrupting
these hormone pathways.

01:35:50.760 --> 01:35:53.290
They also looked at glucose responses.

01:35:53.290 --> 01:35:56.480
They had people essentially
take a fasting glucose test

01:35:56.480 --> 01:35:57.380
in different conditions.

01:35:57.380 --> 01:35:58.950
I won't go into all the details,

01:35:58.950 --> 01:36:00.410
but here's what they found.

01:36:00.410 --> 01:36:04.440
In healthy adults, even
just one night of sleeping

01:36:04.440 --> 01:36:06.920
in a moderately lit environment,

01:36:06.920 --> 01:36:10.930
this hundred lux
environment, caused changes,

01:36:10.930 --> 01:36:12.630
increases in nighttime heart rate,

01:36:12.630 --> 01:36:14.440
which means that the
sympathetic nervous system

01:36:14.440 --> 01:36:16.620
was overly active as compared to people

01:36:16.620 --> 01:36:18.340
that slept in a completely dark

01:36:18.340 --> 01:36:20.900
or in a very, very dimly lit room.

01:36:20.900 --> 01:36:23.490
Decreases in heart rate variability,

01:36:23.490 --> 01:36:25.760
and here I should point out
that heart rate variability

01:36:25.760 --> 01:36:28.710
or HRV is a good thing, we
want heart rate variability.

01:36:28.710 --> 01:36:30.380
So they saw increases in heart rate,

01:36:30.380 --> 01:36:32.480
decreases in heart rate variability,

01:36:32.480 --> 01:36:36.360
and increases in next
morning insulin resistance,

01:36:36.360 --> 01:36:40.790
which is an indication that
glucose management is suffering.

01:36:40.790 --> 01:36:43.330
So this is powerful.

01:36:43.330 --> 01:36:45.860
The results of this study
essentially indicate

01:36:45.860 --> 01:36:49.140
that even just one night of
sleeping the whole night through

01:36:49.140 --> 01:36:52.490
in a dimly lit environment
is disrupting the way

01:36:52.490 --> 01:36:54.970
that our autonomic nervous
system is functioning,

01:36:54.970 --> 01:36:57.550
altering so called autonomic tone,

01:36:57.550 --> 01:37:00.300
making us less relaxed
is probably the best way

01:37:00.300 --> 01:37:01.500
to describe it,

01:37:01.500 --> 01:37:03.130
even though we are asleep,

01:37:03.130 --> 01:37:04.460
disrupting the way

01:37:04.460 --> 01:37:07.570
that our cardiometabolic
function operates,

01:37:07.570 --> 01:37:09.860
such that we have lower
heart rate variability

01:37:09.860 --> 01:37:12.910
and increased insulin resistance.

01:37:12.910 --> 01:37:15.690
This is not a good thing
for any of us to experience.

01:37:15.690 --> 01:37:17.630
So while we've mainly been talking

01:37:17.630 --> 01:37:19.480
about the positive effects of UVB light

01:37:19.480 --> 01:37:21.210
and other forms of light,

01:37:21.210 --> 01:37:22.950
now we have two examples.

01:37:22.950 --> 01:37:25.270
One from the work of Hattar and colleagues

01:37:25.270 --> 01:37:28.800
showing that UVB exposure
via the perihabenula

01:37:28.800 --> 01:37:31.370
can diminish the output of dopamine

01:37:31.370 --> 01:37:33.090
and other molecules that make us feel good

01:37:33.090 --> 01:37:35.380
if that UVB exposure is
in the middle of the night

01:37:35.380 --> 01:37:36.810
or late evening.

01:37:36.810 --> 01:37:39.330
And now we have yet
another study performed,

01:37:39.330 --> 01:37:40.840
in this case, in humans,

01:37:40.840 --> 01:37:42.957
indicating that even if we fall asleep

01:37:42.957 --> 01:37:44.580
and sleep the whole night through,

01:37:44.580 --> 01:37:47.540
if the room that we're
sleeping in has too many lux,

01:37:47.540 --> 01:37:48.860
too much light energy,

01:37:48.860 --> 01:37:51.860
that light energy is no doubt
going through the eyelids,

01:37:51.860 --> 01:37:54.930
which it can, activating
the particular cells

01:37:54.930 --> 01:37:57.930
in the eye that trigger an increase

01:37:57.930 --> 01:37:59.910
in sympathetic nervous system activation

01:37:59.910 --> 01:38:02.370
and disrupting our metabolism.

01:38:02.370 --> 01:38:05.660
And this study rests on a
number of other recent studies

01:38:05.660 --> 01:38:07.960
published in Cell, which
is a superb journal,

01:38:07.960 --> 01:38:10.210
and other journals, showing
that during the course

01:38:10.210 --> 01:38:13.090
of a healthy, deep night's sleep,

01:38:13.090 --> 01:38:14.187
our body actually transitions

01:38:14.187 --> 01:38:16.800
through various forms
of metabolic function.

01:38:16.800 --> 01:38:20.420
We actually experience
ketosis-like states.

01:38:20.420 --> 01:38:23.000
We experience glucogenesis.

01:38:23.000 --> 01:38:25.240
We experience different
forms of metabolism

01:38:25.240 --> 01:38:27.230
associated with different stages of sleep,

01:38:27.230 --> 01:38:29.080
not something that we're
going into in depth

01:38:29.080 --> 01:38:31.800
in this podcast, we will
in a future podcast.

01:38:31.800 --> 01:38:35.970
What this study shows is that
light exposure even in sleep

01:38:35.970 --> 01:38:38.530
is disrupting our autonomic, in this case,

01:38:38.530 --> 01:38:40.950
the sympathetic arm of the
autonomic nervous system

01:38:40.950 --> 01:38:44.280
in ways that are disrupting
metabolism, probably in sleep,

01:38:44.280 --> 01:38:47.030
but certainly outside of
sleep so that we wake up

01:38:47.030 --> 01:38:48.450
and have our first meal of the day.

01:38:48.450 --> 01:38:49.700
Or even if you're intermittent fasting,

01:38:49.700 --> 01:38:51.320
you eat that first meal of the day,

01:38:51.320 --> 01:38:54.310
if your sleep is taking
place in an environment

01:38:54.310 --> 01:38:56.590
that's overly illuminated,

01:38:56.590 --> 01:38:59.310
well, that's disrupting
your cardiac function

01:38:59.310 --> 01:39:00.720
and your metabolism.

01:39:00.720 --> 01:39:02.480
I've been talking a lot about UVB light,

01:39:02.480 --> 01:39:04.150
which is short wavelength light.

01:39:04.150 --> 01:39:05.270
So UV light, blue light,

01:39:05.270 --> 01:39:07.180
maybe even some blue green light,

01:39:07.180 --> 01:39:08.680
that's going to be short wavelength light.

01:39:08.680 --> 01:39:09.800
Now, I'd like to shift our attention

01:39:09.800 --> 01:39:11.160
to the other end of the spectrum,

01:39:11.160 --> 01:39:12.350
meaning the light spectrum,

01:39:12.350 --> 01:39:14.360
to talk about red light
and infrared light,

01:39:14.360 --> 01:39:16.250
which is long wavelength light.

01:39:16.250 --> 01:39:19.970
Many so-called low level light therapies,

01:39:19.970 --> 01:39:23.990
the acronym is LLLT, low
level light therapies,

01:39:23.990 --> 01:39:27.600
involve the use of red
light and infrared light.

01:39:27.600 --> 01:39:31.100
Sometimes, low level light
therapies involve the use of UVB,

01:39:31.100 --> 01:39:32.760
but more often than not these days,

01:39:32.760 --> 01:39:35.760
when we hear LLLT, low
level light therapy,

01:39:35.760 --> 01:39:37.540
it's referring to red light

01:39:37.540 --> 01:39:40.003
and near-infrared light therapies.

01:39:41.950 --> 01:39:44.610
Low level light therapies have
been shown to be effective

01:39:44.610 --> 01:39:47.640
for a huge number of biological phenomenon

01:39:47.640 --> 01:39:49.420
and medical treatments.

01:39:49.420 --> 01:39:50.940
I can't summarize all of those now.

01:39:50.940 --> 01:39:52.770
It would take me many, many hours.

01:39:52.770 --> 01:39:55.410
It would be an effective
episode for curing insomnia,

01:39:55.410 --> 01:39:58.810
but it wouldn't inform you
properly about the use of light

01:39:58.810 --> 01:39:59.800
for your health.

01:39:59.800 --> 01:40:01.380
Rather, I'd like to just emphasize

01:40:01.380 --> 01:40:03.550
some of the top contour of those studies

01:40:03.550 --> 01:40:06.010
and point out that for instance,

01:40:06.010 --> 01:40:08.160
low level light therapy
with infrared light

01:40:08.160 --> 01:40:10.960
has been shown to be effective
for the treatment of acne

01:40:10.960 --> 01:40:13.000
and other sorts of skin lesions.

01:40:13.000 --> 01:40:15.200
There have been some really
nice studies actually

01:40:15.200 --> 01:40:18.010
where they use subjects as
their own internal control.

01:40:18.010 --> 01:40:19.150
So people, believe it or not,

01:40:19.150 --> 01:40:20.820
agreed to have half of their face

01:40:20.820 --> 01:40:23.910
illuminated with red light
or near-infrared light,

01:40:23.910 --> 01:40:26.050
and the other half of their
face serve as a control,

01:40:26.050 --> 01:40:28.330
and to do that for
several weeks at a time.

01:40:28.330 --> 01:40:31.100
And you can see pretty
impressive reductions

01:40:31.100 --> 01:40:35.220
in skin lesions, reductions
in scars from acne,

01:40:35.220 --> 01:40:38.220
and reduction in acne lesions themselves,

01:40:38.220 --> 01:40:41.020
meaning the accumulation of new acne cysts

01:40:41.020 --> 01:40:42.570
with low level light therapy,

01:40:42.570 --> 01:40:44.600
using red light and infrared light.

01:40:44.600 --> 01:40:49.280
Sometimes however, there is
a resistance of that acne

01:40:49.280 --> 01:40:50.620
to the low level light therapy,

01:40:50.620 --> 01:40:52.800
such that people will get
an initial improvement,

01:40:52.800 --> 01:40:55.700
and then it'll go away despite
continuing the treatment.

01:40:55.700 --> 01:40:58.270
So you're probably asking, or
at least you should be asking,

01:40:58.270 --> 01:41:00.770
how is it that shining
red light on our skin

01:41:00.770 --> 01:41:04.640
can impact things like acne
and wound healing, et cetera?

01:41:04.640 --> 01:41:06.760
Well, to understand that,
we have to think back

01:41:06.760 --> 01:41:08.370
to the beginning of the episode

01:41:08.370 --> 01:41:10.730
where I described how
long wavelength light,

01:41:10.730 --> 01:41:13.100
such as red light and near-infrared light,

01:41:13.100 --> 01:41:15.160
which is even longer than red light,

01:41:15.160 --> 01:41:19.180
can pass through certain
surfaces, including our skin.

01:41:19.180 --> 01:41:21.830
So our skin has an epidermis,
which is on the outside,

01:41:21.830 --> 01:41:24.440
and the dermis, which
is in the deeper layers.

01:41:24.440 --> 01:41:27.150
Red light and infrared light can pass down

01:41:27.150 --> 01:41:29.060
into the deeper layers of our skin,

01:41:29.060 --> 01:41:31.437
where it can change the metabolic function

01:41:31.437 --> 01:41:32.900
of particular cells.

01:41:32.900 --> 01:41:35.650
So let's just take acne as an example.

01:41:35.650 --> 01:41:37.930
Within the dermis, the
deep layers of our skin,

01:41:37.930 --> 01:41:39.730
we have what are called sebaceous glands

01:41:39.730 --> 01:41:43.740
that actually make the oil
that is present in our skin.

01:41:43.740 --> 01:41:46.710
Those sebaceous glands are
often nearby hair follicles.

01:41:46.710 --> 01:41:49.313
So if you've ever had a
infected hair follicle,

01:41:50.150 --> 01:41:51.520
that's not a coincidence

01:41:51.520 --> 01:41:53.120
that hair follicles tend to get infected.

01:41:53.120 --> 01:41:56.270
Part of it is because there's
actually a portal down

01:41:56.270 --> 01:41:57.128
and around the hair follicle,

01:41:57.128 --> 01:42:00.550
but the sebaceous gland is
where the oil is created.

01:42:00.550 --> 01:42:03.800
That is going to give rise to,
for instance, acne lesions.

01:42:03.800 --> 01:42:06.940
Also, in the dermis, in the
deep layers of the skin,

01:42:06.940 --> 01:42:08.020
are the melanocytes.

01:42:08.020 --> 01:42:08.960
They're not just in the epidermis,

01:42:08.960 --> 01:42:11.820
they're also in the
deeper layers of the skin.

01:42:11.820 --> 01:42:14.780
And you have the stem cells that give rise

01:42:14.780 --> 01:42:16.860
to additional skin cells.

01:42:16.860 --> 01:42:19.240
If the top layers of the
epidermis are damaged,

01:42:19.240 --> 01:42:21.300
those stem cells can become activated.

01:42:21.300 --> 01:42:23.570
And you also have the stem cells

01:42:23.570 --> 01:42:25.380
that give rise to hair follicles.

01:42:25.380 --> 01:42:29.680
So by shining red light
or near-infrared light

01:42:29.680 --> 01:42:31.630
on a localized patch of skin,

01:42:31.630 --> 01:42:34.980
provided that red light is
not of such high intensity

01:42:34.980 --> 01:42:36.550
that it burns the skin,

01:42:36.550 --> 01:42:39.150
but is of sufficient
intensity that it provides

01:42:39.150 --> 01:42:42.480
just a little bit of damage to
the upper layers of the skin,

01:42:42.480 --> 01:42:43.860
the epidermis,

01:42:43.860 --> 01:42:47.240
and that it triggers
certain biological pathways

01:42:47.240 --> 01:42:49.540
within the cells of the sebaceous gland

01:42:49.540 --> 01:42:52.510
and the stem cells within
the hair cell niche

01:42:52.510 --> 01:42:54.050
and the stem cells in skin,

01:42:54.050 --> 01:42:56.690
what happens is the top layers of the skin

01:42:56.690 --> 01:43:00.510
are basically burned off
by a very low level of burn

01:43:00.510 --> 01:43:02.460
and/or the cells in the deeper layer

01:43:02.460 --> 01:43:03.930
start to churn out new cells,

01:43:03.930 --> 01:43:07.400
which go and rescue the lesion,

01:43:07.400 --> 01:43:10.670
essentially clear out the
lesion and replace that lesion

01:43:10.670 --> 01:43:12.033
with healthy skin cells.

01:43:13.620 --> 01:43:17.470
This does work in the
context of wound healing,

01:43:17.470 --> 01:43:19.050
getting scars to disappear.

01:43:19.050 --> 01:43:23.010
It also works to remove certain
patches of pigmentation.

01:43:23.010 --> 01:43:24.010
There are sometimes cases

01:43:24.010 --> 01:43:26.900
where people will get a red blotchiness

01:43:26.900 --> 01:43:28.610
due to certain skin conditions

01:43:28.610 --> 01:43:31.370
or some darker pigmentation
that they want remove,

01:43:31.370 --> 01:43:32.610
or that they need removed,

01:43:32.610 --> 01:43:36.070
because it's a potential
skin cancer threat.

01:43:36.070 --> 01:43:37.880
Now, how is red light actually doing it

01:43:37.880 --> 01:43:39.550
within the cells of the sebaceous gland,

01:43:39.550 --> 01:43:41.040
the stem cells, et cetera?

01:43:41.040 --> 01:43:43.220
Well, long wavelength light

01:43:43.220 --> 01:43:45.220
can actually get deep into the skin,

01:43:45.220 --> 01:43:46.053
I mentioned that before,

01:43:46.053 --> 01:43:49.800
but can also get into individual cells

01:43:49.800 --> 01:43:51.760
and can access the so-called organelles,

01:43:51.760 --> 01:43:53.380
which I described at the
beginning of the episode.

01:43:53.380 --> 01:43:55.830
In particular, they can
access the mitochondria,

01:43:55.830 --> 01:43:58.560
which are responsible for producing ATP.

01:43:58.560 --> 01:44:00.580
Now, the simple way to think about this

01:44:00.580 --> 01:44:05.580
for sake of this discussion
is that as cells age,

01:44:06.160 --> 01:44:09.143
and in particular, in very
metabolically active cells,

01:44:10.360 --> 01:44:12.830
they accumulate what are called ROSs,

01:44:12.830 --> 01:44:15.010
reactive oxygen species.

01:44:15.010 --> 01:44:18.810
And as reactive oxygen species go up,

01:44:18.810 --> 01:44:22.390
ATP energy production in
those cells tends to go down.

01:44:22.390 --> 01:44:23.440
It's a general statement,

01:44:23.440 --> 01:44:27.210
but it's a general statement
that in most cases is true.

01:44:27.210 --> 01:44:29.910
There are some minor exceptions
that don't concern us

01:44:29.910 --> 01:44:31.963
that have to do with cell
types different than the ones

01:44:31.963 --> 01:44:33.570
that I'm talking about now.

01:44:33.570 --> 01:44:35.900
So the way to think about
this is that red light passes

01:44:35.900 --> 01:44:38.020
into the deeper layers of the skin,

01:44:38.020 --> 01:44:40.970
activates mitochondria,
which increases ATP,

01:44:40.970 --> 01:44:43.360
and directly or indirectly reduces

01:44:43.360 --> 01:44:45.090
these reactive oxygen species.

01:44:45.090 --> 01:44:48.250
These reactive oxygen
species are not good.

01:44:48.250 --> 01:44:49.390
We don't want them.

01:44:49.390 --> 01:44:52.390
They cause cellular
damage, cellular death.

01:44:52.390 --> 01:44:55.150
And for the most part just inhibit the way

01:44:55.150 --> 01:44:56.700
that our cells work.

01:44:56.700 --> 01:44:58.590
So if you've heard of red light

01:44:58.590 --> 01:45:00.800
or near-infrared light therapies

01:45:00.800 --> 01:45:04.060
designed to heal skin
or improve skin quality

01:45:04.060 --> 01:45:05.080
or remove lesions,

01:45:05.080 --> 01:45:07.900
or get rid of scars or
unwanted pigmentation,

01:45:07.900 --> 01:45:12.390
that is not pseudoscience,
that is not woo science.

01:45:12.390 --> 01:45:15.670
That is grounded in the very
biology of how light interacts

01:45:15.670 --> 01:45:18.410
with mitochondria and
reactive oxygen species.

01:45:18.410 --> 01:45:20.580
Some of you may also find
it interesting to note

01:45:20.580 --> 01:45:22.770
that some of the cream-based treatments

01:45:22.770 --> 01:45:24.290
for acne, for instance,

01:45:24.290 --> 01:45:26.630
like retinoic acid, Retin-A,

01:45:26.630 --> 01:45:30.070
is actually a derivative of vitamin A.

01:45:30.070 --> 01:45:33.760
And the pathway involving
retinoic acid and vitamin A,

01:45:33.760 --> 01:45:34.593
believe it or not,

01:45:34.593 --> 01:45:38.560
is very similar to the
natural biological pathway

01:45:38.560 --> 01:45:41.900
by which photopigments in the
eye convert light information

01:45:41.900 --> 01:45:44.670
into biological changes
within those cells.

01:45:44.670 --> 01:45:47.580
So the key point here is that light

01:45:47.580 --> 01:45:50.300
is activating particular pathways in cells

01:45:50.300 --> 01:45:52.860
that can either drive death of cells

01:45:52.860 --> 01:45:55.730
or can make those cells
essentially younger

01:45:55.730 --> 01:45:57.730
by increasing ATP

01:45:57.730 --> 01:46:00.570
by way of improving
mitochondrial function.

01:46:00.570 --> 01:46:02.530
And in recent years, there have been some

01:46:02.530 --> 01:46:05.410
just beautiful examples that exist,

01:46:05.410 --> 01:46:07.750
not only in the realm of skin biology,

01:46:07.750 --> 01:46:11.830
but in the realm of
neurobiology whereby red light

01:46:11.830 --> 01:46:13.810
and near-infrared light
can actually be used

01:46:13.810 --> 01:46:15.660
to enhance the function of the cells

01:46:15.660 --> 01:46:17.810
that, for instance, allow us to see better

01:46:17.810 --> 01:46:20.640
and indeed cells that
allow us to think better.

01:46:20.640 --> 01:46:22.350
So now I'd like to review those data

01:46:22.350 --> 01:46:25.690
because not only are they
interesting in their own right,

01:46:25.690 --> 01:46:28.530
but they also point to
some very interesting

01:46:28.530 --> 01:46:31.960
and powerful application of
low-cost or zero-cost tools

01:46:31.960 --> 01:46:34.500
that we can use to improve our vision.

01:46:34.500 --> 01:46:37.050
If you are somebody who's
interested in the use of red light

01:46:37.050 --> 01:46:38.910
or near-infrared light,

01:46:38.910 --> 01:46:41.770
so-called LLLT, low level light therapies,

01:46:41.770 --> 01:46:44.160
for treatment of dermatologic issues,

01:46:44.160 --> 01:46:45.950
so anything related to skin,

01:46:45.950 --> 01:46:49.200
I will include a link to a
excellent set of reviews.

01:46:49.200 --> 01:46:52.290
The first one is Light-emitting
Diodes in Dermatology:

01:46:52.290 --> 01:46:55.260
A Systematic Review of
Randomized Controlled Trials.

01:46:55.260 --> 01:46:58.520
That one includes review of a
very large number of studies,

01:46:58.520 --> 01:47:00.870
came out just a few years ago in 2018,

01:47:00.870 --> 01:47:02.710
and I think is very clearly

01:47:02.710 --> 01:47:05.230
and cleanly laid out for anyone to access.

01:47:05.230 --> 01:47:06.700
And you can see the degree of effects

01:47:06.700 --> 01:47:07.533
of red light, for instance,

01:47:07.533 --> 01:47:09.400
on treatment of acne
or scarring, et cetera.

01:47:09.400 --> 01:47:12.060
And I'll also provide a
link to another review,

01:47:12.060 --> 01:47:14.350
which is Low-level Light Therapy in Skin:

01:47:14.350 --> 01:47:16.120
Stimulating, Healing, and Restoring.

01:47:16.120 --> 01:47:18.130
So for those of you that
are interested, again,

01:47:18.130 --> 01:47:19.480
in dermatologic issues

01:47:19.480 --> 01:47:21.980
and the kind of restoring youthfulness

01:47:21.980 --> 01:47:25.870
and the kind of general themes
of anti-aging and longevity

01:47:25.870 --> 01:47:28.770
and how red light therapies
can be used for that,

01:47:28.770 --> 01:47:31.380
I would encourage you to
take a look at those reviews.

01:47:31.380 --> 01:47:35.280
What you're going to find
is that rarely, if ever,

01:47:35.280 --> 01:47:37.190
is there a study looking at whole body

01:47:37.190 --> 01:47:38.880
red light illumination

01:47:38.880 --> 01:47:41.950
for sake of treating and improving skin.

01:47:41.950 --> 01:47:44.350
And I mention this because
I get a lot of questions

01:47:44.350 --> 01:47:48.190
about infrared sauna
and global illumination

01:47:48.190 --> 01:47:49.440
with red lights.

01:47:49.440 --> 01:47:51.200
We'll talk more about cases

01:47:51.200 --> 01:47:53.430
where global illumination
of your whole body

01:47:53.430 --> 01:47:56.590
or your whole face with
red lights might be useful,

01:47:56.590 --> 01:47:58.420
but in terms of infrared sauna,

01:47:58.420 --> 01:47:59.920
I've mentioned on this podcast before,

01:47:59.920 --> 01:48:01.430
and I will certainly go deeper on this

01:48:01.430 --> 01:48:02.440
in an upcoming episode,

01:48:02.440 --> 01:48:04.880
all about the use of heat and temperature

01:48:04.880 --> 01:48:06.980
for augmenting our biology,

01:48:06.980 --> 01:48:11.880
but in general, infrared
saunas don't get hot enough,

01:48:11.880 --> 01:48:14.930
temperature-wise, in order to trigger some

01:48:14.930 --> 01:48:16.810
of the important effects on growth hormone

01:48:16.810 --> 01:48:18.800
and heat shock proteins and
some of the other things

01:48:18.800 --> 01:48:21.990
that sauna has been shown
to be excellent for.

01:48:21.990 --> 01:48:22.930
That's a general statement.

01:48:22.930 --> 01:48:24.550
I realize there are some infrared saunas

01:48:24.550 --> 01:48:26.440
that do get hot enough.

01:48:26.440 --> 01:48:29.410
There are very few data on the use

01:48:29.410 --> 01:48:32.620
of whole body illumination
with infrared saunas

01:48:32.620 --> 01:48:34.680
that really point to any specific

01:48:34.680 --> 01:48:37.430
mechanistically supported effects.

01:48:37.430 --> 01:48:39.410
Almost all the positive effects
that you're going to see

01:48:39.410 --> 01:48:41.900
of red light and
low-level light therapies,

01:48:41.900 --> 01:48:43.350
certainly the ones
discussed in the reviews

01:48:43.350 --> 01:48:44.220
that I just mentioned,

01:48:44.220 --> 01:48:46.840
are going to be the consequence

01:48:46.840 --> 01:48:50.740
of very directed illumination
of particular patches of skin

01:48:50.740 --> 01:48:52.190
that are seeking repair,

01:48:52.190 --> 01:48:54.300
that people are seeking the repair of.

01:48:54.300 --> 01:48:58.050
So again, I don't want to
disparage infrared saunas,

01:48:58.050 --> 01:48:59.410
but in general, they don't get hot enough

01:48:59.410 --> 01:49:01.100
to trigger most of the positive effects

01:49:01.100 --> 01:49:03.900
that sauna have been demonstrated to have.

01:49:03.900 --> 01:49:07.030
And it's unclear at all
as to whether or not

01:49:07.030 --> 01:49:09.380
they can enhance skin
quality, youthfulness,

01:49:09.380 --> 01:49:14.200
restore top layers of
skin that are damaged,

01:49:14.200 --> 01:49:15.760
repair acne, et cetera.

01:49:15.760 --> 01:49:18.140
So more on heat saunas and infrared saunas

01:49:18.140 --> 01:49:20.720
and their comparison
in an upcoming episode.

01:49:20.720 --> 01:49:23.580
So let's talk about a
clear set of examples

01:49:23.580 --> 01:49:26.810
where red light and near-infrared light

01:49:26.810 --> 01:49:30.410
have been shown to have
positive effects on our health.

01:49:30.410 --> 01:49:32.920
And these are the data that I
referred to at the beginning

01:49:32.920 --> 01:49:35.210
of the episode from Dr. Glen Jeffery

01:49:35.210 --> 01:49:37.070
at University College London,

01:49:37.070 --> 01:49:40.620
who, again, is a longstanding member

01:49:40.620 --> 01:49:41.710
of the neuroscience community,

01:49:41.710 --> 01:49:43.260
working on visual neuroscience,

01:49:43.260 --> 01:49:45.450
and who over the last decade or so

01:49:45.450 --> 01:49:48.570
has really emphasized the
exploration of red light

01:49:48.570 --> 01:49:51.550
and near-infrared light for restoration

01:49:51.550 --> 01:49:54.020
of neuronal function as we age.

01:49:54.020 --> 01:49:55.240
This is absolutely critical.

01:49:55.240 --> 01:49:59.410
We know that we don't
accumulate many new brain cells

01:49:59.410 --> 01:50:00.243
as we get older.

01:50:00.243 --> 01:50:02.270
And in some areas of our nervous system,

01:50:02.270 --> 01:50:05.330
such as our neural retina,
which is the part of our eye,

01:50:05.330 --> 01:50:07.630
that's responsible for
translating light information

01:50:07.630 --> 01:50:10.200
to electrical signals so that we can see,

01:50:10.200 --> 01:50:12.810
we don't get any new cells after the time

01:50:12.810 --> 01:50:14.020
in which we were born.

01:50:14.020 --> 01:50:16.600
So the ability to keep our neurons healthy

01:50:16.600 --> 01:50:19.430
is extremely important
for our visual system,

01:50:19.430 --> 01:50:22.190
extremely important for our hippocampus,

01:50:22.190 --> 01:50:24.300
an area of the brain involved in memory.

01:50:24.300 --> 01:50:26.610
And I should just mention that even

01:50:26.610 --> 01:50:28.080
if people don't get Alzheimer's,

01:50:28.080 --> 01:50:30.010
there's always going to be some degree

01:50:30.010 --> 01:50:31.900
of age-related dementia.

01:50:31.900 --> 01:50:35.700
Sadly, nobody is as cognitively sharp

01:50:35.700 --> 01:50:36.990
in the years before they die,

01:50:36.990 --> 01:50:39.240
as they are 20 years before that.

01:50:39.240 --> 01:50:40.360
It's just never the case.

01:50:40.360 --> 01:50:42.550
We're all getting worse at thinking,

01:50:42.550 --> 01:50:44.530
feeling, perceiving, et cetera.

01:50:44.530 --> 01:50:46.730
The question is how quickly
we are getting worse.

01:50:46.730 --> 01:50:49.590
So any mechanism by which we can preserve

01:50:49.590 --> 01:50:51.640
or reverse neuronal function

01:50:51.640 --> 01:50:54.393
turns out to be immensely beneficial.

01:50:55.400 --> 01:50:59.020
The Jeffery Lab has published
two studies in recent years

01:50:59.020 --> 01:51:03.540
on humans that looked
directly, no pun intended,

01:51:03.540 --> 01:51:05.830
at how red light and near-infrared light

01:51:05.830 --> 01:51:08.330
can improve visual function.

01:51:08.330 --> 01:51:10.350
I'm going to describe the
parameters of those studies.

01:51:10.350 --> 01:51:13.263
And then I'm going to describe
what they found, exactly.

01:51:14.470 --> 01:51:18.350
The mechanistic motivation
for these studies, again,

01:51:18.350 --> 01:51:22.520
traces back to this effect
of light on mitochondria.

01:51:22.520 --> 01:51:23.500
So to go a little bit deeper

01:51:23.500 --> 01:51:25.920
into that mechanism just briefly

01:51:25.920 --> 01:51:28.170
so that you can frame
any potential protocol

01:51:28.170 --> 01:51:29.453
that you would develop,

01:51:30.640 --> 01:51:35.640
when light arrives on
cells, including neurons,

01:51:36.050 --> 01:51:37.650
that light can penetrate into the cells

01:51:37.650 --> 01:51:39.020
if it's of the appropriate wavelength.

01:51:39.020 --> 01:51:40.820
Red light can do that,
it can get into cells,

01:51:40.820 --> 01:51:42.270
it can access the mitochondria,

01:51:42.270 --> 01:51:44.043
it can increase ATP.

01:51:44.900 --> 01:51:47.870
In general, anytime ATP is doing its thing

01:51:47.870 --> 01:51:50.100
to increase energy in cells,

01:51:50.100 --> 01:51:54.480
it's involving this thing
called cytochrome c,

01:51:54.480 --> 01:51:55.640
which is an oxidase.

01:51:55.640 --> 01:51:58.770
Anytime you hear ase, A-S-E, in biology,

01:51:58.770 --> 01:51:59.830
it's going to be an enzyme.

01:51:59.830 --> 01:52:02.970
It's involved in some process
of degrading a molecule

01:52:02.970 --> 01:52:05.240
and creating another molecule, typically.

01:52:05.240 --> 01:52:08.480
So ATP and cytochrome c
is going to give you ATP.

01:52:08.480 --> 01:52:13.330
Now, that's a great thing,
but it creates a byproduct.

01:52:13.330 --> 01:52:16.120
It breaks things down, such
that you get these ROSs,

01:52:16.120 --> 01:52:18.120
these reactive oxygen species.

01:52:18.120 --> 01:52:19.900
And those reactive oxygen species,

01:52:19.900 --> 01:52:21.190
for those of you that want to know,

01:52:21.190 --> 01:52:23.900
are involved in things
like redox signaling.

01:52:23.900 --> 01:52:27.320
And reactive oxygen
species actually change

01:52:27.320 --> 01:52:29.610
which genes are made in a cell.

01:52:29.610 --> 01:52:32.300
So the goal of any
treatment is to keep neurons

01:52:32.300 --> 01:52:35.880
or other cells youthful
and functioning well,

01:52:35.880 --> 01:52:38.920
and to prevent or reverse aging,

01:52:38.920 --> 01:52:41.350
is going to be to increase ATP

01:52:41.350 --> 01:52:44.180
and to reduce reactive oxygen species,

01:52:44.180 --> 01:52:48.530
and in doing so, to disrupt
some of the normal pathways

01:52:48.530 --> 01:52:50.253
associated with aging.

01:52:51.910 --> 01:52:53.640
The Jeffery Lab approached these studies

01:52:53.640 --> 01:52:55.840
with that understanding
of how mitochondria

01:52:55.840 --> 01:52:58.420
and reactive oxygen species and ATP work.

01:52:58.420 --> 01:53:01.620
And what they did was exquisitely simple

01:53:01.620 --> 01:53:02.910
to the point of being elegant.

01:53:02.910 --> 01:53:05.290
And what they found was
really, really exciting.

01:53:05.290 --> 01:53:08.650
What they did is they had people,

01:53:08.650 --> 01:53:11.450
subjects that were either
younger, so in their 20s,

01:53:11.450 --> 01:53:14.023
or 40 years old or older,

01:53:15.030 --> 01:53:18.010
view red light of about 670 nanometers.

01:53:18.010 --> 01:53:21.540
670 nanometers would
appear red to you and me.

01:53:21.540 --> 01:53:23.660
They, they had them do that, excuse me,

01:53:23.660 --> 01:53:25.660
at a distance that was
safe for their eyes,

01:53:25.660 --> 01:53:27.770
so at about a foot away.

01:53:27.770 --> 01:53:31.730
Now, a foot away from a
very intense red light

01:53:31.730 --> 01:53:33.060
could actually be damaging to the eyes,

01:53:33.060 --> 01:53:35.240
so they had them do this
at about a foot away

01:53:35.240 --> 01:53:37.990
from a red light that was
of low enough intensity

01:53:37.990 --> 01:53:39.990
that it did not damage the eyes.

01:53:39.990 --> 01:53:41.310
And they had them do that anywhere

01:53:41.310 --> 01:53:44.150
from two to three minutes per day.

01:53:44.150 --> 01:53:45.880
And in one study, they had them do that

01:53:45.880 --> 01:53:48.390
for a long period of
time of about 12 weeks.

01:53:48.390 --> 01:53:49.223
And in the other study,

01:53:49.223 --> 01:53:51.720
they had them do that just
for a couple of weeks.

01:53:51.720 --> 01:53:54.600
What's remarkable is that
when you collapse the results

01:53:54.600 --> 01:53:56.130
across these two studies,

01:53:56.130 --> 01:53:58.710
what they found is that when
looking at these subjects

01:53:58.710 --> 01:54:02.920
ranging from 28 years old
to about 72 years old,

01:54:02.920 --> 01:54:06.430
the major findings were
that in individuals

01:54:06.430 --> 01:54:08.960
40 years old or older,

01:54:08.960 --> 01:54:12.970
so in the 40 to 72-year-old bracket,

01:54:12.970 --> 01:54:16.650
but not in the subjects
younger than 40 years old,

01:54:16.650 --> 01:54:20.160
they saw an improvement
in visual function.

01:54:20.160 --> 01:54:21.500
That improvement in visual function

01:54:21.500 --> 01:54:23.820
was an improvement in visual acuity,

01:54:23.820 --> 01:54:26.900
meaning the ability to
resolve fine detail,

01:54:26.900 --> 01:54:30.520
and using a particular
measure of visual function,

01:54:30.520 --> 01:54:34.120
which is called the Tritan exam.

01:54:34.120 --> 01:54:36.940
T-R-I-T-A-N, Tritan exam,

01:54:36.940 --> 01:54:40.370
which specifically addresses the function

01:54:40.370 --> 01:54:42.400
of the so-called short wavelength cones,

01:54:42.400 --> 01:54:45.040
the ones that respond
to green and blue light,

01:54:45.040 --> 01:54:48.880
they saw a 22% improvement
in visual acuity,

01:54:48.880 --> 01:54:52.270
which in the landscape of visual testing

01:54:52.270 --> 01:54:55.400
is an extremely exciting result.

01:54:55.400 --> 01:54:59.260
Okay, so I think in most studies
of improvements of vision,

01:54:59.260 --> 01:55:03.350
you'd be very excited to see
an improvement of 5% or 10%.

01:55:03.350 --> 01:55:06.560
So a 22% improvement in visual acuity,

01:55:06.560 --> 01:55:09.020
even though it's in
this very specific form

01:55:09.020 --> 01:55:12.733
of visual testing, this Tritan
exam or this Tritan score,

01:55:13.760 --> 01:55:16.050
well, that turns out
to be very significant

01:55:16.050 --> 01:55:20.230
and translates to the real
world in an important way.

01:55:20.230 --> 01:55:23.110
In particular, as we age,

01:55:23.110 --> 01:55:26.160
we tend to lose certain
neurons within our retina,

01:55:26.160 --> 01:55:28.550
but we don't tend to lose cones.

01:55:28.550 --> 01:55:30.210
We tend to lose rods.

01:55:30.210 --> 01:55:32.320
We tend to lose other
cells within the retina,

01:55:32.320 --> 01:55:34.290
including the cells that
connect the eye to the brain,

01:55:34.290 --> 01:55:35.840
the so-called ganglion cells.

01:55:35.840 --> 01:55:37.620
Cones, for whatever reason,

01:55:37.620 --> 01:55:40.260
are pretty resilient to age-related loss.

01:55:40.260 --> 01:55:44.510
However, because rods and cones both

01:55:44.510 --> 01:55:47.760
are not just among the most
metabolically active cells

01:55:47.760 --> 01:55:49.150
in your entire body,

01:55:49.150 --> 01:55:53.010
but the most metabolically
active cells in your entire body

01:55:53.010 --> 01:55:56.250
that's right, your rods and
cones are the cells that demand,

01:55:56.250 --> 01:55:59.893
and that use the most energy
of all the cells in your body,

01:56:00.980 --> 01:56:01.860
not your skin cells,

01:56:01.860 --> 01:56:03.760
not your spleen, not your stomach cells.

01:56:03.760 --> 01:56:04.960
Even if you talk a lot,

01:56:04.960 --> 01:56:07.180
not the cells that are
responsible for moving your mouth.

01:56:07.180 --> 01:56:09.720
It is the rods and cones
of your neural retina

01:56:09.720 --> 01:56:13.047
that are responsible for
using the most amount of ATP

01:56:13.047 --> 01:56:15.660
and energy in your entire body.

01:56:15.660 --> 01:56:17.010
And because of that,

01:56:17.010 --> 01:56:19.710
those cells tend to accumulate a lot

01:56:19.710 --> 01:56:22.293
of reactive oxygen species as we age.

01:56:23.180 --> 01:56:25.410
Red light of the sort
used in these studies

01:56:25.410 --> 01:56:29.970
was able to reduce the amount
of reactive oxygen species

01:56:29.970 --> 01:56:33.380
in the rods and cones and
to rescue the function

01:56:33.380 --> 01:56:35.240
of this particular cone type,

01:56:35.240 --> 01:56:37.060
the short wavelength and
medium wavelength cones,

01:56:37.060 --> 01:56:38.500
which if you think about the study,

01:56:38.500 --> 01:56:39.750
is a little bit surprising,

01:56:39.750 --> 01:56:42.380
because it was red light
and near-infrared light,

01:56:42.380 --> 01:56:44.120
not short wavelength light,

01:56:44.120 --> 01:56:47.040
that was used in order to
create this improvement

01:56:47.040 --> 01:56:48.550
in cellular function.

01:56:48.550 --> 01:56:51.010
But if you step back a little bit further,

01:56:51.010 --> 01:56:54.750
it makes perfect sense because
there's nothing specific

01:56:54.750 --> 01:56:56.270
about the red light in the sense

01:56:56.270 --> 01:56:59.590
that it gets delivered only to red cones.

01:56:59.590 --> 01:57:02.410
That red light and near-infrared
light is being absorbed

01:57:02.410 --> 01:57:04.690
by all the photoreceptors within the eye,

01:57:04.690 --> 01:57:06.670
the rods and the blue cones

01:57:06.670 --> 01:57:08.460
and the green cones and the red cones.

01:57:08.460 --> 01:57:11.800
It's just that the red cones
absorb that light best.

01:57:11.800 --> 01:57:14.580
So the important takeaway here

01:57:14.580 --> 01:57:17.880
is that viewing red light
and near-infrared light

01:57:17.880 --> 01:57:19.820
at a distance at which it is safe

01:57:19.820 --> 01:57:22.460
for just a couple of minutes each day

01:57:22.460 --> 01:57:25.940
allowed a reversal of the
aging process of these neurons,

01:57:25.940 --> 01:57:28.660
which some people have
heard me say before,

01:57:28.660 --> 01:57:30.350
and I'll just say it again,

01:57:30.350 --> 01:57:32.230
the retina, including your photoreceptors,

01:57:32.230 --> 01:57:34.210
are not just connected to your brain.

01:57:34.210 --> 01:57:35.310
They're not just near your brain.

01:57:35.310 --> 01:57:37.450
They are actual central
nervous system tissue.

01:57:37.450 --> 01:57:39.590
They are the only two
pieces of your brain,

01:57:39.590 --> 01:57:41.500
meaning your neuroretinas
are the only two pieces

01:57:41.500 --> 01:57:43.330
of your brain that reside
outside your skull,

01:57:43.330 --> 01:57:45.050
or at least outside the cranial vault.

01:57:45.050 --> 01:57:48.300
So here we're seeing a
reversal of the aging process

01:57:48.300 --> 01:57:51.600
in neurons by shining red
light on those neurons.

01:57:51.600 --> 01:57:54.100
Now, of course, the Jeffery Lab

01:57:54.100 --> 01:57:55.750
is primarily interested in vision,

01:57:55.750 --> 01:57:58.090
and humans are most dependent on vision

01:57:58.090 --> 01:57:59.910
as a sense to navigate
the world and survive.

01:57:59.910 --> 01:58:01.270
So this is really wonderful.

01:58:01.270 --> 01:58:03.070
Here, we're looking at a therapy

01:58:03.070 --> 01:58:06.580
that can reverse age-related vision loss,

01:58:06.580 --> 01:58:08.730
at least in some individuals.

01:58:08.730 --> 01:58:09.610
But as you can imagine,

01:58:09.610 --> 01:58:10.990
the study was also done on these cells

01:58:10.990 --> 01:58:13.110
because they reside outside the skull

01:58:13.110 --> 01:58:15.360
and you can shine light
directly on them, right?

01:58:15.360 --> 01:58:17.020
I'm sure that there are
many people out there

01:58:17.020 --> 01:58:19.470
who are interested in how
they can improve the function,

01:58:19.470 --> 01:58:22.620
say, of the neurons in their
brain responsible for memory.

01:58:22.620 --> 01:58:23.453
And in a few minutes,

01:58:23.453 --> 01:58:27.020
I'll describe the non-invasive
applications of light

01:58:27.020 --> 01:58:31.080
to try and restore the function
of those cells as well.

01:58:31.080 --> 01:58:35.013
So a little bit more about the
studies from the Jeffery Lab.

01:58:35.940 --> 01:58:38.200
One of the things that they observed was

01:58:38.200 --> 01:58:41.780
a reduction in so-called
drusen, D-R-U-S-E-N.

01:58:41.780 --> 01:58:45.770
Drusen are little fatty deposits,

01:58:45.770 --> 01:58:47.470
little cholesterol deposits,

01:58:47.470 --> 01:58:49.920
that accumulate in the eye as we age.

01:58:49.920 --> 01:58:51.580
We've all heard about cholesterol

01:58:51.580 --> 01:58:53.130
within our veins and arteries

01:58:53.130 --> 01:58:55.690
and how that can clog
our veins and arteries

01:58:55.690 --> 01:58:58.760
and how, of course, clogging
of veins and arteries

01:58:58.760 --> 01:58:59.790
is not a good thing.

01:58:59.790 --> 01:59:02.580
Well, our neural retina
being so metabolically active

01:59:02.580 --> 01:59:04.170
requires a lot of blood flow.

01:59:04.170 --> 01:59:06.180
It's heavily vascularized,

01:59:06.180 --> 01:59:09.120
and drusen are a special
form of cholesterol

01:59:09.120 --> 01:59:11.300
that accumulate in the eye.

01:59:11.300 --> 01:59:12.930
As it turns out, these red light

01:59:12.930 --> 01:59:15.060
and near-infrared light therapies

01:59:15.060 --> 01:59:16.500
explored by the Jeffery Lab

01:59:16.500 --> 01:59:19.610
were able to actually reduce or reverse

01:59:19.610 --> 01:59:21.630
some of the accumulation of drusen.

01:59:21.630 --> 01:59:25.670
And so in addition to reducing
reactive oxygen species,

01:59:25.670 --> 01:59:28.010
the idea in mind now is that red light

01:59:28.010 --> 01:59:31.560
may actually reduce cholesterol deposits

01:59:31.560 --> 01:59:34.120
and reactive oxygen species

01:59:34.120 --> 01:59:36.560
in order to improve neuronal function.

01:59:36.560 --> 01:59:38.760
So what should you and
I do with these results?

01:59:38.760 --> 01:59:40.280
Or should we do anything
with these results?

01:59:40.280 --> 01:59:42.240
Well, first of all, I want to emphasize

01:59:42.240 --> 01:59:44.720
that even though these
studies are very exciting,

01:59:44.720 --> 01:59:45.970
they are fairly recent.

01:59:45.970 --> 01:59:49.010
And so more data, as always, are needed.

01:59:49.010 --> 01:59:50.690
There's some additional
features of these studies

01:59:50.690 --> 01:59:52.630
that I think are also
important to consider.

01:59:52.630 --> 01:59:55.820
First of all, the exposure to red light

01:59:55.820 --> 01:59:58.410
needed to happen early in the day,

01:59:58.410 --> 02:00:00.903
at least within the first
three hours of waking.

02:00:02.040 --> 02:00:03.140
How would one do that?

02:00:03.140 --> 02:00:04.670
Well, nowadays there are a number

02:00:04.670 --> 02:00:06.640
of different red light panels

02:00:06.640 --> 02:00:08.910
and different red light sources

02:00:08.910 --> 02:00:11.780
that certainly fall within
the range of red light

02:00:11.780 --> 02:00:14.300
and near-infrared light
that one could use.

02:00:14.300 --> 02:00:17.740
I don't have any affiliation
to any companies or products

02:00:17.740 --> 02:00:21.670
that promote or make
those red light therapies.

02:00:21.670 --> 02:00:23.430
I do own a red light panel,

02:00:23.430 --> 02:00:25.950
so I confess I have started
using this protocol.

02:00:25.950 --> 02:00:27.820
I am older than 40 years old.

02:00:27.820 --> 02:00:30.930
I also have been experimenting
with these red light panels

02:00:30.930 --> 02:00:35.550
as a way of addressing other
changes in biological tissues,

02:00:35.550 --> 02:00:37.180
for which I'm doing blood work, et cetera.

02:00:37.180 --> 02:00:39.520
And I'm going to talk about
that in a future episode,

02:00:39.520 --> 02:00:41.680
but that, of course, is
what I call anecdata.

02:00:41.680 --> 02:00:43.220
It only relates to my experience.

02:00:43.220 --> 02:00:46.420
So today, and certainly on all episodes

02:00:46.420 --> 02:00:47.740
of the Huberman Lab Podcast,

02:00:47.740 --> 02:00:50.750
we emphasize peer-reviewed
studies almost exclusively,

02:00:50.750 --> 02:00:52.050
talking about anecdata

02:00:52.050 --> 02:00:54.870
only when highlighting it as anecdata.

02:00:54.870 --> 02:00:56.520
So if you're somebody who wants

02:00:56.520 --> 02:00:59.630
to explore red light therapy,
here's what you need to do.

02:00:59.630 --> 02:01:02.150
You need to make sure that
that red light source,

02:01:02.150 --> 02:01:04.190
whatever source you happen to use,

02:01:04.190 --> 02:01:05.540
whether or not you
purchase it or make one...

02:01:05.540 --> 02:01:07.300
And in fact, these red light sources

02:01:07.300 --> 02:01:08.890
are very, very easy to make.

02:01:08.890 --> 02:01:11.630
You could essentially
take a bright flashlight

02:01:11.630 --> 02:01:14.130
and cover it with a film or a filter

02:01:14.130 --> 02:01:17.420
that would only allow
particular long wavelengths

02:01:17.420 --> 02:01:18.253
to pass through.

02:01:18.253 --> 02:01:19.880
This would be very easy to look up online

02:01:19.880 --> 02:01:20.750
and figure out how to do this.

02:01:20.750 --> 02:01:23.550
You could probably do this for,
you know, just a few dollars

02:01:23.550 --> 02:01:25.510
or you could purchase a red light unit

02:01:25.510 --> 02:01:27.470
if that was within your budget

02:01:27.470 --> 02:01:29.000
and something that you're interested in.

02:01:29.000 --> 02:01:30.840
You want to make sure
that it's not so bright

02:01:30.840 --> 02:01:32.610
that you're damaging your eye.

02:01:32.610 --> 02:01:34.360
A good rule of thumb is that something

02:01:34.360 --> 02:01:35.920
isn't painful to look at.

02:01:35.920 --> 02:01:37.400
And in fact, I should just emphasize

02:01:37.400 --> 02:01:39.560
that any time you look
at any light source,

02:01:39.560 --> 02:01:41.500
sunlight or otherwise, that's painful

02:01:41.500 --> 02:01:43.050
and makes you want to
squint or close your eyes,

02:01:43.050 --> 02:01:44.910
that means it's too bright to look at

02:01:44.910 --> 02:01:46.020
without closing your eyes.

02:01:46.020 --> 02:01:47.420
Okay, that's sort of a duh,

02:01:47.420 --> 02:01:48.960
but I would loathe to think

02:01:48.960 --> 02:01:50.420
that anyone would harm themselves

02:01:50.420 --> 02:01:51.770
with bright light in any way.

02:01:51.770 --> 02:01:52.980
I don't just say that to protect us.

02:01:52.980 --> 02:01:54.870
I say that to protect you, of course,

02:01:54.870 --> 02:01:56.730
because you are responsible
for your health.

02:01:56.730 --> 02:01:59.320
And again, retinal
neurons do not regenerate.

02:01:59.320 --> 02:02:02.170
Once they are gone and
dead, they do not come back.

02:02:02.170 --> 02:02:03.780
There's no technology to replace them

02:02:03.780 --> 02:02:06.270
at this current state in time.

02:02:06.270 --> 02:02:08.400
So please don't damage your retinas.

02:02:08.400 --> 02:02:10.870
So is a red light source safe to look at

02:02:10.870 --> 02:02:13.500
if it is not painful to look at?

02:02:13.500 --> 02:02:14.580
Chances are it is.

02:02:14.580 --> 02:02:16.020
And yet I would still encourage you

02:02:16.020 --> 02:02:19.220
to talk to your optometrist
or ophthalmologist

02:02:19.220 --> 02:02:21.940
before getting into any
extensive protocols.

02:02:21.940 --> 02:02:23.230
But if you are still determined

02:02:23.230 --> 02:02:25.050
to pursue the sorts of protocols

02:02:25.050 --> 02:02:26.270
that are in the Jeffery studies,

02:02:26.270 --> 02:02:28.450
certainly we'll provide
a link to those studies.

02:02:28.450 --> 02:02:31.170
Again, it involved looking
at these red light panels,

02:02:31.170 --> 02:02:35.400
blinking aloud for two
minutes to three minutes

02:02:36.300 --> 02:02:40.793
every morning for a period
of two weeks or more.

02:02:41.650 --> 02:02:44.870
And if you're older than 40,

02:02:44.870 --> 02:02:46.240
that could very well have an effect.

02:02:46.240 --> 02:02:48.560
If you're longer, younger
than 40, excuse me,

02:02:48.560 --> 02:02:50.800
that's unlikely to have an effect.

02:02:50.800 --> 02:02:52.500
At least that was what was observed

02:02:52.500 --> 02:02:54.110
in these particular studies.

02:02:54.110 --> 02:02:55.560
The lights were not flashing.

02:02:55.560 --> 02:02:57.260
It was continuous illumination.

02:02:57.260 --> 02:02:58.840
Again, you're allowed to blink.

02:02:58.840 --> 02:03:00.660
It does not have to even
be direct illumination.

02:03:00.660 --> 02:03:02.500
It can be somewhat indirect illumination,

02:03:02.500 --> 02:03:06.260
much as we described for
the use of UVB light before.

02:03:06.260 --> 02:03:08.230
The wavelength of light is important.

02:03:08.230 --> 02:03:10.220
It is red light and near-infrared light

02:03:10.220 --> 02:03:14.050
that is going to be
effective in this scenario.

02:03:14.050 --> 02:03:16.760
The authors of this study
emphasized that it was red light

02:03:16.760 --> 02:03:20.270
of 670 nanometers in wavelength

02:03:20.270 --> 02:03:25.270
and near-infrared light of
790 nanometers in wavelength

02:03:25.810 --> 02:03:26.870
that were effective

02:03:26.870 --> 02:03:29.760
and that those wavelengths
could be complimentary.

02:03:29.760 --> 02:03:33.240
That's probably why, or
maybe it's just coincidental,

02:03:33.240 --> 02:03:35.270
but it's a fortunate coincidence

02:03:35.270 --> 02:03:37.730
that a lot of the commercially
available red light panels

02:03:37.730 --> 02:03:38.563
that you'll find out there

02:03:38.563 --> 02:03:41.510
combine both red light
and near-infrared light.

02:03:41.510 --> 02:03:44.310
However, I want to emphasize
that most of the panels

02:03:44.310 --> 02:03:46.120
that are commercially available

02:03:46.120 --> 02:03:50.520
are going to be too bright to
safely look at very close up.

02:03:50.520 --> 02:03:53.040
And in fact, that's why most
of those red light panels

02:03:53.040 --> 02:03:55.010
are designed for illumination of the skin

02:03:55.010 --> 02:03:57.560
and oftentimes arrive in their packaging

02:03:57.560 --> 02:04:00.080
with eye protectors that
are actually designed

02:04:00.080 --> 02:04:01.800
to shield out all the red light.

02:04:01.800 --> 02:04:06.260
So take the potential dangers
of excessive illumination

02:04:06.260 --> 02:04:09.290
of the eyes with any
wavelength of light seriously.

02:04:09.290 --> 02:04:12.850
But if you're going to explore
670 and 790 nanometer light

02:04:12.850 --> 02:04:16.380
for sake of enhancing neuronal function,

02:04:16.380 --> 02:04:18.690
set it at a distance that's
comfortable to look at,

02:04:18.690 --> 02:04:20.380
and that doesn't force you to squint

02:04:20.380 --> 02:04:22.980
or doesn't make you feel
uncomfortable physically,

02:04:22.980 --> 02:04:24.350
as if you need to turn away

02:04:25.510 --> 02:04:26.343
during the period

02:04:26.343 --> 02:04:28.810
of that two to three-minute
illumination each day.

02:04:30.250 --> 02:04:32.430
In terms of turning away from light,

02:04:32.430 --> 02:04:36.040
I'll just briefly mention
that that is not an accident

02:04:36.040 --> 02:04:37.810
or a coincidence that
you have that response

02:04:37.810 --> 02:04:39.030
to very bright light.

02:04:39.030 --> 02:04:42.030
There is a so-called
photic avoidance pathway

02:04:42.030 --> 02:04:43.680
that involves cells within your retina,

02:04:43.680 --> 02:04:45.290
these ganglion cells that communicate

02:04:45.290 --> 02:04:48.150
with yet another brain station,

02:04:48.150 --> 02:04:50.980
a certain area of your
thalamus that communicate

02:04:50.980 --> 02:04:53.610
to areas of your brain that
are associated with pain.

02:04:53.610 --> 02:04:55.380
So literally that can trigger headache,

02:04:55.380 --> 02:04:57.503
and that can trigger the squint reflex.

02:04:58.420 --> 02:05:00.470
Biology is just beautiful in this way.

02:05:00.470 --> 02:05:03.520
Too much light is bad for us
in that it can damage our eyes

02:05:03.520 --> 02:05:05.190
and other aspects of our body.

02:05:05.190 --> 02:05:07.430
So if we look at a
light that's too bright,

02:05:07.430 --> 02:05:09.020
our eyes send a signal to the brain

02:05:09.020 --> 02:05:10.410
that gives us a sort of a headache

02:05:10.410 --> 02:05:12.140
and a desire to squint and turn away.

02:05:12.140 --> 02:05:14.080
So that can be a useful guide

02:05:14.080 --> 02:05:16.740
in terms of gauging how
bright a light should be

02:05:16.740 --> 02:05:20.020
or at least how far away you
should be from a bright source

02:05:20.020 --> 02:05:23.140
in order to safely engage
with that light source.

02:05:23.140 --> 02:05:25.920
So the studies I just
described, once again,

02:05:25.920 --> 02:05:28.020
involve the use of red
light early in the day

02:05:28.020 --> 02:05:29.430
within three hours of waking

02:05:29.430 --> 02:05:31.800
and are for the sake of
improving neuronal function.

02:05:31.800 --> 02:05:35.810
Red light has also been
shown to be beneficial

02:05:35.810 --> 02:05:38.200
late in the day and even
in the middle of the night.

02:05:38.200 --> 02:05:40.300
And when I say middle of the night,

02:05:40.300 --> 02:05:43.130
I'm referring to studies that
explore the use of red light

02:05:43.130 --> 02:05:44.640
for shift workers.

02:05:44.640 --> 02:05:46.420
I know that most people are not working

02:05:46.420 --> 02:05:48.070
in the middle of the night,
at least I hope they're not,

02:05:48.070 --> 02:05:50.470
but some of you may do
that from time to time.

02:05:50.470 --> 02:05:51.480
All-nighters for studying,

02:05:51.480 --> 02:05:53.610
I confess I still pull
all-nighters every once in a while

02:05:53.610 --> 02:05:56.600
to prepare things like
podcasts and other deadlines.

02:05:56.600 --> 02:05:59.780
I really try not to, happens
less and less as I get older,

02:05:59.780 --> 02:06:01.590
because I think I get more disciplined

02:06:01.590 --> 02:06:05.010
and/or less good at pulling all-nighters.

02:06:05.010 --> 02:06:07.380
But I realize that many
people are doing shift work,

02:06:07.380 --> 02:06:09.790
or they have to work
certainly past 10:00 pm.

02:06:09.790 --> 02:06:11.650
Or maybe they're taking
care of young children

02:06:11.650 --> 02:06:14.090
in the middle of the night,
and they have to be up.

02:06:14.090 --> 02:06:16.450
In that case, red light can
actually be very beneficial.

02:06:16.450 --> 02:06:19.050
And nowadays there are a
lot of sources of red light

02:06:19.050 --> 02:06:20.880
available just as red light bulbs.

02:06:20.880 --> 02:06:21.970
You don't need a panel.

02:06:21.970 --> 02:06:25.260
So what I'm basically saying
is that it can be beneficial

02:06:25.260 --> 02:06:28.140
to use red lights at night.

02:06:28.140 --> 02:06:30.830
The study I'd like to emphasize
in this context is entitled,

02:06:30.830 --> 02:06:34.210
Red Light: A Novel
Non-pharmacological Intervention

02:06:34.210 --> 02:06:36.723
to Promote Alertness in Shift Workers.

02:06:36.723 --> 02:06:38.040
It's a beautiful study.

02:06:38.040 --> 02:06:41.400
They explored the use of
different wavelengths of light,

02:06:41.400 --> 02:06:43.930
so blue light of 460 nanometers

02:06:43.930 --> 02:06:46.740
or red light or dim white light,

02:06:46.740 --> 02:06:49.540
of different brightnesses, et cetera,

02:06:49.540 --> 02:06:52.740
and looked at things like melatonin.

02:06:52.740 --> 02:06:55.190
How much does light of a given color

02:06:55.190 --> 02:06:56.850
and intensity suppress melatonin?

02:06:56.850 --> 02:06:58.620
They looked at cortisol, a stress hormone.

02:06:58.620 --> 02:07:00.370
They looked at wakefulness,

02:07:00.370 --> 02:07:03.640
how much or to what degree
could a given color of light

02:07:03.640 --> 02:07:06.250
increase wakefulness at
different hours of the day?

02:07:06.250 --> 02:07:08.340
The takeaway from this
study is very clear.

02:07:08.340 --> 02:07:11.393
If you need to be awake late
at night for sake of shift work

02:07:11.393 --> 02:07:14.810
or studying or taking care
of children, et cetera,

02:07:14.810 --> 02:07:17.540
red light is going to be your best choice

02:07:17.540 --> 02:07:21.253
because if the red light
is sufficiently dim,

02:07:22.150 --> 02:07:25.040
it's not going to inhibit
melatonin production,

02:07:25.040 --> 02:07:28.110
and it's not going to
increase cortisol at night.

02:07:28.110 --> 02:07:30.840
Cortisol should be high early in the day,

02:07:30.840 --> 02:07:32.390
or at least should be elevated relative

02:07:32.390 --> 02:07:34.520
to other times of day if you are healthy.

02:07:34.520 --> 02:07:36.870
A late shifted increase
in cortisol, however,

02:07:36.870 --> 02:07:39.060
9:00 pm cortisol, 10:00 pm cortisol,

02:07:39.060 --> 02:07:42.150
is well known to be
associated with depression

02:07:42.150 --> 02:07:44.360
and other aspects of mental health,

02:07:44.360 --> 02:07:46.400
or I should say mental illness.

02:07:46.400 --> 02:07:50.140
So if you do need to be awake
at night or even all night,

02:07:50.140 --> 02:07:53.660
red light is going to be
the preferred light source.

02:07:53.660 --> 02:07:55.410
And in terms of how bright to make it,

02:07:55.410 --> 02:07:58.180
well, as dim as you can,

02:07:58.180 --> 02:08:00.090
while still being able
to perform the activities

02:08:00.090 --> 02:08:00.923
that you need to perform.

02:08:00.923 --> 02:08:03.090
That's going to be your best guide.

02:08:03.090 --> 02:08:05.460
I'll provide a link to this study as well.

02:08:05.460 --> 02:08:08.150
Again, it's a really important study

02:08:08.150 --> 02:08:10.360
because it emphasized that
there are forms of light,

02:08:10.360 --> 02:08:12.280
red light, provided it's dim,

02:08:12.280 --> 02:08:15.420
that can allow you to
stimulate the alertness

02:08:15.420 --> 02:08:19.130
that light landing on
the eyes can provide.

02:08:19.130 --> 02:08:20.230
So it allows you to stay awake

02:08:20.230 --> 02:08:22.550
and to do whatever work
that you need to do.

02:08:22.550 --> 02:08:26.070
It does not seem to alter
melatonin production,

02:08:26.070 --> 02:08:26.903
so that's good.

02:08:26.903 --> 02:08:28.920
It does not seem to alter levels

02:08:28.920 --> 02:08:31.040
or timing of cortisol production.

02:08:31.040 --> 02:08:34.720
So yet another case where
red light used correctly

02:08:34.720 --> 02:08:35.830
can be beneficial.

02:08:35.830 --> 02:08:37.040
Up until now, we've been talking

02:08:37.040 --> 02:08:39.785
about the effects of shining
different wavelengths of light

02:08:39.785 --> 02:08:43.160
on the skin or on our eyes

02:08:43.160 --> 02:08:47.610
and the downstream health
consequences of that illumination.

02:08:47.610 --> 02:08:49.600
However, one of the most important goals

02:08:49.600 --> 02:08:52.050
of science and medicine is to figure out

02:08:52.050 --> 02:08:54.620
how to change the health of our brain.

02:08:54.620 --> 02:08:58.280
And of course, our brain is
contained within our skull,

02:08:58.280 --> 02:09:01.210
and therefore we can't just shine light

02:09:01.210 --> 02:09:03.490
onto the outside of our head

02:09:03.490 --> 02:09:05.410
and expect it to change the activity

02:09:05.410 --> 02:09:07.030
of neurons deep within the brain,

02:09:07.030 --> 02:09:11.590
unless those neurons are
linked up with our eyes

02:09:11.590 --> 02:09:12.620
or with our skin.

02:09:12.620 --> 02:09:14.430
And as it turns out, even
though there are a lot

02:09:14.430 --> 02:09:17.710
of brain areas that are
connected through neural circuits

02:09:17.710 --> 02:09:19.610
and hormone circuits through our eye,

02:09:19.610 --> 02:09:22.280
and believe it or not, also to our skin,

02:09:22.280 --> 02:09:24.163
many brain areas are not.

02:09:25.860 --> 02:09:27.830
Brain areas such as the hippocampus,

02:09:27.830 --> 02:09:30.280
which is involved in learning and memory,

02:09:30.280 --> 02:09:33.220
brain areas such as our neocortex,

02:09:33.220 --> 02:09:36.510
well, some areas of our neocortex
such as our visual cortex

02:09:36.510 --> 02:09:38.410
are indirectly linked to our eyes,

02:09:38.410 --> 02:09:39.660
so if we shine light in our eyes,

02:09:39.660 --> 02:09:42.450
we can change the activity
of neurons in our neocortex,

02:09:42.450 --> 02:09:44.970
but there are other brain areas

02:09:44.970 --> 02:09:48.860
that are not directly or
even indirectly connected

02:09:48.860 --> 02:09:50.100
to our visual system,

02:09:50.100 --> 02:09:53.140
not at least in any immediate way.

02:09:53.140 --> 02:09:54.140
So that raises the question

02:09:54.140 --> 02:09:56.450
of how do you change the
activity of neurons in the brain?

02:09:56.450 --> 02:09:58.140
Well, there's pharmacology.

02:09:58.140 --> 02:10:01.100
You can take pills, you can inject drugs

02:10:01.100 --> 02:10:02.657
that will change the
pharmacology of neurons

02:10:02.657 --> 02:10:04.860
and the way they operate and fire.

02:10:04.860 --> 02:10:07.810
Of course, antidepressants
are one such instance,

02:10:07.810 --> 02:10:09.750
opioid drugs are another.

02:10:09.750 --> 02:10:12.740
There's a huge array of
psychoactive compounds,

02:10:12.740 --> 02:10:14.730
meaning compounds that
will change the levels

02:10:14.730 --> 02:10:16.800
of chemicals in your brain.

02:10:16.800 --> 02:10:17.680
Some of those work,

02:10:17.680 --> 02:10:20.730
many of them also carry side effects.

02:10:20.730 --> 02:10:22.580
It's all rather indirect,

02:10:22.580 --> 02:10:24.360
meaning you have lots of different cells

02:10:24.360 --> 02:10:25.340
in different areas of your brain

02:10:25.340 --> 02:10:26.670
that utilize the same chemicals.

02:10:26.670 --> 02:10:29.100
So a drug, for instance,
to increase serotonin

02:10:29.100 --> 02:10:31.540
for sake of improving depression

02:10:31.540 --> 02:10:33.590
will also often have the effect

02:10:33.590 --> 02:10:37.020
of reducing certain neurons

02:10:37.020 --> 02:10:39.010
output of serotonin in the hippocampus

02:10:39.010 --> 02:10:41.300
and cause changes in
appetite or changes in libido

02:10:41.300 --> 02:10:42.453
and so on and so forth.

02:10:43.490 --> 02:10:45.290
You could imagine using
electrical stimulation,

02:10:45.290 --> 02:10:46.480
putting wires into the brain

02:10:46.480 --> 02:10:48.550
and stimulating specific brain areas

02:10:48.550 --> 02:10:51.360
in order to activate the
neurons in those brain areas.

02:10:51.360 --> 02:10:54.010
And certainly that works and
has been done experimentally

02:10:54.010 --> 02:10:56.900
and is done during
neurosurgery exams, et cetera,

02:10:56.900 --> 02:10:59.650
but involves removing a piece of skull.

02:10:59.650 --> 02:11:01.340
So that's not very practical.

02:11:01.340 --> 02:11:04.390
In principle, light
would be a wonderful way

02:11:04.390 --> 02:11:08.510
to modulate the activity of
neurons deep within the brain.

02:11:08.510 --> 02:11:10.363
But again, the skull is in the way.

02:11:11.560 --> 02:11:14.430
Recent studies, however,
have figured out ways

02:11:14.430 --> 02:11:16.730
that light can be delivered to the eyes

02:11:16.730 --> 02:11:20.250
to change global patterns
of firing in the brain

02:11:20.250 --> 02:11:22.690
in ways that can be
beneficial to the brain.

02:11:22.690 --> 02:11:25.530
And the work that I'm referring to now

02:11:25.530 --> 02:11:28.600
is mainly the work of Li-Huei Tsai at MIT,

02:11:28.600 --> 02:11:32.210
Massachusetts Institute of
Technology, and her colleagues.

02:11:32.210 --> 02:11:34.880
And what they've discovered
that there's a particular

02:11:34.880 --> 02:11:38.970
pattern of brain activity
called gamma activity.

02:11:38.970 --> 02:11:42.100
Gamma activity is one so-called wavelength

02:11:42.100 --> 02:11:44.240
of electrical activity in the brain,

02:11:44.240 --> 02:11:45.270
so not wavelengths of light,

02:11:45.270 --> 02:11:48.010
but wavelengths of electrical
activity in the brain

02:11:48.010 --> 02:11:50.550
that can be restorative
for certain aspects

02:11:50.550 --> 02:11:51.600
of learning and memory

02:11:51.600 --> 02:11:55.210
and can actually help create
molecular changes in neurons

02:11:55.210 --> 02:11:57.650
that lead to clearance of debris

02:11:57.650 --> 02:12:01.800
and even reductions in
age-related cognitive decline.

02:12:01.800 --> 02:12:06.800
So the way to think about brain
waves and brain oscillations

02:12:06.970 --> 02:12:09.320
is that neurons are electrically active,

02:12:09.320 --> 02:12:11.150
that involves chemicals, et cetera.

02:12:11.150 --> 02:12:14.877
And they can be active in
very slow, big waveforms.

02:12:14.877 --> 02:12:18.310
So you can think of, you
know, Delta waves, meaning,

02:12:18.310 --> 02:12:20.430
so you can imagine a wave
of electrical activity

02:12:20.430 --> 02:12:22.240
that comes along very infrequently.

02:12:22.240 --> 02:12:24.280
So a given neuron fires,

02:12:24.280 --> 02:12:26.970
and then some period of time later fires,

02:12:26.970 --> 02:12:29.730
and then some period of
time even later fires.

02:12:29.730 --> 02:12:32.550
Or you can imagine that that
same cell is very active,

02:12:32.550 --> 02:12:34.390
fires, fires, fires, fires, fires.

02:12:34.390 --> 02:12:36.050
You can imagine if it's firing very often,

02:12:36.050 --> 02:12:38.450
it's going to be short wavelength, right?

02:12:38.450 --> 02:12:40.380
Shorter gaps between firing.

02:12:40.380 --> 02:12:43.600
Or if it's firing very seldom,

02:12:43.600 --> 02:12:45.510
you're going to think about that

02:12:45.510 --> 02:12:47.310
as longer wavelength firing.

02:12:47.310 --> 02:12:51.070
Turns out that gamma waves
are one pattern of firing

02:12:51.070 --> 02:12:54.810
that lead to downstream
metabolic functions

02:12:54.810 --> 02:12:57.380
and biological functions that
end up clearing away debris

02:12:57.380 --> 02:13:00.250
that's associated with aging in cells

02:13:00.250 --> 02:13:03.340
and that also lead to molecular changes

02:13:03.340 --> 02:13:06.430
that enhance the kind of
youthfulness of neurons,

02:13:06.430 --> 02:13:07.263
so to speak.

02:13:09.230 --> 02:13:12.450
How do we induce gamma
oscillations within the brain?

02:13:12.450 --> 02:13:15.730
Well, what Li-Huei Tsai and colleagues

02:13:15.730 --> 02:13:17.260
have beautifully shown

02:13:17.260 --> 02:13:22.080
is that by delivering certain
patterns of light flicker,

02:13:22.080 --> 02:13:25.350
so lights going on and off
at a particular frequency,

02:13:25.350 --> 02:13:28.530
the brain as a whole starts to entrain,

02:13:28.530 --> 02:13:30.900
meaning it matches to
those particular patterns

02:13:30.900 --> 02:13:31.733
of light flicker,

02:13:31.733 --> 02:13:34.400
even though many of the
brain areas that do this

02:13:34.400 --> 02:13:38.710
are not directly within the
visual system or visual pathway.

02:13:38.710 --> 02:13:42.090
So the studies that I'm
referring to are several,

02:13:42.090 --> 02:13:43.950
but the one that I'd like
to highlight is entitled,

02:13:43.950 --> 02:13:46.820
Gamma Entrainment Binds
Higher-Order Brain Regions

02:13:46.820 --> 02:13:48.170
and Offers Neuroprotection.

02:13:49.318 --> 02:13:50.670
What they essentially did

02:13:50.670 --> 02:13:54.870
was to expose subjects to 40 hertz,

02:13:54.870 --> 02:13:58.120
which is a particular
frequency of illumination,

02:13:58.120 --> 02:13:59.170
to the eyes.

02:13:59.170 --> 02:14:01.360
So it's light goes on, light goes off,

02:14:01.360 --> 02:14:06.250
light goes on, light goes off
at a frequency of 40 hertz.

02:14:06.250 --> 02:14:09.310
And when they did that and
they recorded the activity

02:14:09.310 --> 02:14:10.410
of neurons within the brain,

02:14:10.410 --> 02:14:12.960
not just within the
visual areas of the brain,

02:14:12.960 --> 02:14:14.870
but within other areas as well,

02:14:14.870 --> 02:14:18.860
they observed increased
gamma oscillations,

02:14:18.860 --> 02:14:23.070
meaning that the electrical
activity of the brain at large

02:14:23.070 --> 02:14:26.110
started to match to the patterns of light

02:14:26.110 --> 02:14:28.020
that were delivered to the eyes.

02:14:28.020 --> 02:14:29.770
This is really exciting and very unique

02:14:29.770 --> 02:14:31.720
from the different types of phototherapies

02:14:31.720 --> 02:14:33.220
that we've been talking
about up until now.

02:14:33.220 --> 02:14:35.210
All the patterns of phototherapy

02:14:35.210 --> 02:14:36.730
that we've been talking about up until now

02:14:36.730 --> 02:14:39.340
involved constant illumination
with a given wavelength.

02:14:39.340 --> 02:14:44.340
Here, it is wavelength generating
patterns of illumination,

02:14:44.930 --> 02:14:46.620
light on, light off, light on, light off,

02:14:46.620 --> 02:14:48.530
at a particular frequency.

02:14:48.530 --> 02:14:49.780
So what they found, for instance,

02:14:49.780 --> 02:14:51.250
using this pattern of stimulation,

02:14:51.250 --> 02:14:53.690
and by the way, the
stimulation was called genus,

02:14:53.690 --> 02:14:56.180
gamma entrainment using
sensory stimulation,

02:14:56.180 --> 02:14:57.320
so G-E-N-U-S,

02:14:57.320 --> 02:15:00.030
gamma entrainment using
sensory stimulation,

02:15:00.030 --> 02:15:01.610
had a number of really
interesting effects.

02:15:01.610 --> 02:15:04.950
First of all, it reduced
so-called amyloid plaques

02:15:04.950 --> 02:15:06.710
and phosphorylated tau.

02:15:06.710 --> 02:15:08.400
Amyloid plaques and phosphorylated tau

02:15:08.400 --> 02:15:09.810
are associated with Alzheimer's

02:15:09.810 --> 02:15:12.890
and normal age related cognitive decline.

02:15:12.890 --> 02:15:14.653
So this is incredible, right?

02:15:15.960 --> 02:15:19.280
A pattern of flashing
light delivered to the eyes

02:15:19.280 --> 02:15:21.400
creates a pattern of neuronal firing,

02:15:21.400 --> 02:15:23.160
not just in the visual areas of the brain,

02:15:23.160 --> 02:15:25.640
but in other areas of the brain as well,

02:15:25.640 --> 02:15:28.750
that in turn trigger molecular pathways

02:15:28.750 --> 02:15:32.050
that reduce some of the markers

02:15:32.050 --> 02:15:35.580
and the cause age-related
cognitive decline in Alzheimer's.

02:15:35.580 --> 02:15:39.160
And in parallel to that,
they observed an upregulation

02:15:39.160 --> 02:15:40.939
of some of the biological pathways

02:15:40.939 --> 02:15:43.530
that lead to enhancement
of neuronal function,

02:15:43.530 --> 02:15:44.660
maintenance of synapses,

02:15:44.660 --> 02:15:46.530
which are the connections between neurons,

02:15:46.530 --> 02:15:48.350
and so on, and so on.

02:15:48.350 --> 02:15:50.850
They have discovered and
list out a huge number

02:15:50.850 --> 02:15:52.450
of these biological effects,

02:15:52.450 --> 02:15:55.300
both the reduction in
bad things, so to speak,

02:15:55.300 --> 02:15:59.120
and the improvement in
good biological pathways.

02:15:59.120 --> 02:16:02.100
And I find these studies so exciting

02:16:02.100 --> 02:16:05.600
because, first of all,
they're non-invasive, right?

02:16:05.600 --> 02:16:07.560
There's no drilling through the skull.

02:16:07.560 --> 02:16:11.790
They are very tractable
in the experimental sense,

02:16:11.790 --> 02:16:14.060
meaning that you can imagine

02:16:14.060 --> 02:16:15.970
that if 40 hertz stimulation turns out

02:16:15.970 --> 02:16:17.860
to be the very best stimulation protocol

02:16:17.860 --> 02:16:20.400
to induce these gamma
oscillations, well, great,

02:16:20.400 --> 02:16:22.330
but because it's non-invasive,

02:16:22.330 --> 02:16:25.140
it's fairly easy to explore
50 hertz stimulation,

02:16:25.140 --> 02:16:27.540
100 hertz stimulation,
20 hertz stimulation,

02:16:27.540 --> 02:16:30.247
and to do that with different
wavelengths of light.

02:16:30.247 --> 02:16:31.830
And so that's what's happening now.

02:16:31.830 --> 02:16:33.640
The Tsai lab and other
labs are really starting

02:16:33.640 --> 02:16:36.700
to explore the full range of variables

02:16:36.700 --> 02:16:39.710
that can impact oscillations
within the brain

02:16:39.710 --> 02:16:41.490
and their downstream consequences.

02:16:41.490 --> 02:16:43.640
So again, this is phototherapy,

02:16:43.640 --> 02:16:45.510
but phototherapy of a very different sort

02:16:45.510 --> 02:16:47.890
that we've been talking
about up until now.

02:16:47.890 --> 02:16:50.960
It's phototherapy designed
to trigger activation

02:16:50.960 --> 02:16:54.250
of biological pathways far
away from the very tissue

02:16:54.250 --> 02:16:55.260
that's being illuminated.

02:16:55.260 --> 02:16:57.017
And it calls to mind the
same sorts of mechanisms

02:16:57.017 --> 02:16:58.360
that we were talking about earlier,

02:16:58.360 --> 02:17:00.870
where illumination of
the skin with UVB light

02:17:00.870 --> 02:17:03.150
is setting off an enormous
number of different cascades

02:17:03.150 --> 02:17:04.550
in different organs and tissues,

02:17:04.550 --> 02:17:08.170
including the spleen, the
testes, the ovaries, and so on.

02:17:08.170 --> 02:17:11.790
So again, light has
these powerful effects,

02:17:11.790 --> 02:17:15.090
both locally on the cells that
the light is delivered to,

02:17:15.090 --> 02:17:18.470
but also systemically
in terms of the cells

02:17:18.470 --> 02:17:21.700
that are changing their
electrical and chemical outputs,

02:17:21.700 --> 02:17:24.750
are modifying lots and lots
of biological programs.

02:17:24.750 --> 02:17:27.970
Is there an actionable tool
related to these studies yet?

02:17:27.970 --> 02:17:30.290
Well, that sort of depends
on how adventurous you are.

02:17:30.290 --> 02:17:32.900
Right now, these studies
are being explored

02:17:32.900 --> 02:17:34.530
in the context of clinical trials,

02:17:34.530 --> 02:17:36.350
in people with Alzheimer's, dementia,

02:17:36.350 --> 02:17:38.533
and other forms of neurodegeneration.

02:17:40.330 --> 02:17:44.120
Is it dangerous to look at
a 40-hertz flickering light?

02:17:44.120 --> 02:17:46.860
Well, in general, the
answer is going to be no.

02:17:46.860 --> 02:17:50.020
However, if you're prone
to epilepsy, for instance,

02:17:50.020 --> 02:17:51.190
staring at a flickering light

02:17:51.190 --> 02:17:55.210
of a given continuous frequency
can induce seizure, right?

02:17:55.210 --> 02:17:56.590
That might surprise some of you,

02:17:56.590 --> 02:18:00.080
but it shouldn't, because
as this study illustrates

02:18:00.080 --> 02:18:02.620
and as anyone who's ever been out at night

02:18:02.620 --> 02:18:04.553
to a club or something illustrates,

02:18:05.690 --> 02:18:07.830
when you look at a strobe
light, for instance,

02:18:07.830 --> 02:18:09.960
your whole world of
visual perception changes,

02:18:09.960 --> 02:18:13.230
but actually, the rhythm at
which you perceive music,

02:18:13.230 --> 02:18:14.470
at which you perceive conversation,

02:18:14.470 --> 02:18:16.240
at which you perceive
the movement of your body

02:18:16.240 --> 02:18:18.740
actually changes according to the patterns

02:18:18.740 --> 02:18:19.750
of visual flicker,

02:18:19.750 --> 02:18:21.210
in most cases, strobe,

02:18:21.210 --> 02:18:24.840
if we're using the sort
of club dancing example.

02:18:24.840 --> 02:18:28.130
Your brain is in training
to its outside environment.

02:18:28.130 --> 02:18:30.860
So given the power of flickering lights

02:18:30.860 --> 02:18:33.140
to entrain brain rhythms,

02:18:33.140 --> 02:18:36.540
I think at this stage, it's
probably too preliminary

02:18:36.540 --> 02:18:38.800
to really suggest a specific protocol,

02:18:38.800 --> 02:18:41.450
but I would definitely keep an eye out

02:18:41.450 --> 02:18:43.750
for these sorts of studies.

02:18:43.750 --> 02:18:45.320
They are coming out all the time.

02:18:45.320 --> 02:18:47.960
And I think in a very short period,

02:18:47.960 --> 02:18:50.450
we are going to see specific protocols

02:18:50.450 --> 02:18:53.060
that one could use even at home,

02:18:53.060 --> 02:18:54.930
and of course, these are
non-invasive protocols,

02:18:54.930 --> 02:18:58.720
in order to place the brain
into a particular state,

02:18:58.720 --> 02:19:01.580
not just for sake of
offsetting neurodegeneration,

02:19:01.580 --> 02:19:03.930
but also for enhancing focus,

02:19:03.930 --> 02:19:05.950
for enhancing the transition into sleep,

02:19:05.950 --> 02:19:07.660
and other brain states as well.

02:19:07.660 --> 02:19:11.280
Today, I covered what I would
say is a lot of information.

02:19:11.280 --> 02:19:13.040
My goal was to give you an understanding

02:19:13.040 --> 02:19:16.720
of how light can be used
to change the activities

02:19:16.720 --> 02:19:21.450
of cells, organelles within
those cells, entire organs,

02:19:21.450 --> 02:19:25.930
and how that can happen
locally and systemically.

02:19:25.930 --> 02:19:28.550
We talked about the power of
light to impact our biology

02:19:28.550 --> 02:19:30.400
at the endocrine level, neuronal level,

02:19:30.400 --> 02:19:33.510
immune level, mood, et cetera,

02:19:33.510 --> 02:19:36.680
through both illumination
of the eyes and the skin

02:19:36.680 --> 02:19:38.730
and other tissues as well.

02:19:38.730 --> 02:19:42.390
I realize that even though
this was a lot of information,

02:19:42.390 --> 02:19:46.420
there are many aspects of
phototherapy that I did not cover.

02:19:46.420 --> 02:19:48.540
I know there's a lot of
interest nowadays, for instance,

02:19:48.540 --> 02:19:52.190
in the use of red light and
other wavelength light therapies

02:19:52.190 --> 02:19:55.460
for ovarian health and testicular health.

02:19:55.460 --> 02:19:59.140
In fact, I get a lot of questions such as,

02:19:59.140 --> 02:20:02.230
can red light be used to
improve testosterone output?

02:20:02.230 --> 02:20:04.460
And if so, is that best accomplished

02:20:04.460 --> 02:20:06.760
by shining red light on the skin

02:20:06.760 --> 02:20:09.540
or directly on the
gonads, on the testicles?

02:20:09.540 --> 02:20:13.500
I'm going to cover those
data at a future time.

02:20:13.500 --> 02:20:16.260
Right now, the studies that
have been done in rodents,

02:20:16.260 --> 02:20:19.200
I don't think are easily
enough translated to humans.

02:20:19.200 --> 02:20:21.500
And the studies that are
happening in humans now

02:20:21.500 --> 02:20:24.560
are exciting in the sense that
they hold a lot of potential,

02:20:24.560 --> 02:20:27.580
but the data aren't clear yet.

02:20:27.580 --> 02:20:32.580
However, the data using UVB
on the skin of men and women

02:20:33.010 --> 02:20:35.270
in order to increase hormone,

02:20:35.270 --> 02:20:37.260
in particular testosterone
and estrogen output,

02:20:37.260 --> 02:20:39.420
those data, I think, are very exciting

02:20:39.420 --> 02:20:42.030
and very actionable when we
talked about those earlier.

02:20:42.030 --> 02:20:43.910
So if you want more information

02:20:43.910 --> 02:20:45.650
on how phototherapy can be used,

02:20:45.650 --> 02:20:48.830
certainly we will do another
episode on phototherapy

02:20:48.830 --> 02:20:50.670
in these other contexts.

02:20:50.670 --> 02:20:53.040
If you're learning from and
are enjoying this podcast,

02:20:53.040 --> 02:20:54.790
please subscribe to our YouTube channel.

02:20:54.790 --> 02:20:57.450
That's a terrific zero-cost
way to support us.

02:20:57.450 --> 02:20:59.275
In addition, please
subscribe to the podcast

02:20:59.275 --> 02:21:01.410
